      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>2 FOR DEALLI:3YESS US E McDOuGALus Pateo SELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner, Saturday, March 4, 1905. MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>nac carrie to a —place ulicro a Mile stream ran babbling over the gravel: it was crossed by bridge of a rough plank with an uncertain ha:O- rel'. A coppice stood on either side of tile stream. As the girl reached one cud of tile ,mggigs., a young man appeared at the other end, and took a his bat with a smile which meant more than a simple greeting. "You here, Mr. Rutherford? I did not ex- pect—" '•1 .7,--)ped to see you here this mornirg, I wanted to see you. I hoped that you would be interested to hear that I have got my First Class."</p>
        <p>Every motorist who has driven much at night-time and whose route has lain through strange roads has experienced the inconvenience of entering strange and, apparently, nameless villages. He cannot tell whether he is still pursuing the right road and there is no one to help him. Most of the inhabitants , are in bed and fast asleep by nine or ten o'clock at night, and, unless be is suffimently fortunate to come across the village telegraph office, there is nothing in the place itself to afford him information concerning his whereabouts.</p>
        <p>Even the ordinary driver is quite aware of this draw- back to travelling at night in a strange part of the country; but it is the record-breakers who have had the fact forced upon them most persistently. When one recollects how much depends upon "keeping going" when a non-stopped run is in progress, it is easy to un ierstand that what has struck the casual motorist as an inconvenience has appealed far more forcibly to the aspirant for record hours. It is not surprising there- fore, to find that Mr. Archibald Ford, the present holder of the world's non-stop record, has thrown himself heart and soul into a scheme for having name-boards erected at the entrance to villages as well as at all im- portant circus-roads. That this plan, if carried through would be of the greatest service, not only to motorists, but also to other travellers and users of the road, is quite Obvious, and we cordially wish Mr. Ford every success in his efforts. We understand that a member of Parliament has been approached upon the matter with the view of it being brought before the House at the first favourable opportunity.</p>
        <p>" Oh ! I am so glad !" " The rest will be easy. A lectureship, a pro- fessorship later on, a name in the world, the Royal Society." He was a young man with a very pale face, but it was full of resolution. "Are you interested ?' "Of course I am. Tell me more about it. Where are you going this morning, Mr. Ruther- ford ?"</p>
        <p>"I am going your way. could I go ?" . _</p>
        <p>What other way</p>
        <p>He ci;ssed the bridge and touched her fingem reverently...</p>
        <p>She mile. "Mr. Rutherford," she said, "your companion will have to go your way." So she turned and walked with him—and lo it was his way, not her own.</p>
        <p>No practical man can fail to feel utterly astounded at times at the careless—one may even say at the criminally careless—manner in which trial cars are too often sent out by the manufacturers. Considering how much, from the makers' point of view, depends upon a successful trial being given to an intending customer, one would have supposed, naturally enough, that no stone would have been left unturned to make sure that the car was in every way so adjusted to give the best possible results. But no. Instead of that, wefind them run with brakes out of order, the clutch frequently either too fierce or else slipping abominably, the tyres so worn that they are showing the canvas backing— and the tool-box absolutely empty, so that, should a breakdown occur, driver and passengers alike would be most hopelessly stranded.</p>
        <p>Only last week we ourselves saw a trial car sent out upon which neither the foot-applied nor the emergency brake was acting, whilst the steering wheel was innocent of hooter so that it was quite impossible for the driver to pull up out of anyone's way or to signal to others that he was coming through. The car itself is one of the very finest upon the market to-day; but it is not nearly so well known as it should be—and little wonder, for when trials are undertaken with such slip-shod supervision, it is obvious that breakdowns must occur, and the passengers, ignorant perhaps of the principles of motoring, blame the engine when the firm and the mechanics are really in fault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>CaerTER IV.</head>
        <p>CaerTER IV.</p>
        <p>In the evening the same couple were alone in the drawing-room of the Cottage : it was a long, low room, with the windows opening to the ground and looking out west to catch the glories of the setting sun. The room was filled with the usual collestions. Water colours on the walls, curtains instead of doors, lamps, fenders and things of polished brass, bits of statuary, bits of carved wood, tables with books and music, a piano, rugs on the polished floor, what more could one want to indicate culture? In the gar- den, outside, strolled a middle aged man, cigar in mouth and hands in pockets. He glanced at the windows of the drawing-room with a curious look of gratification. "it will do," he thought., "I have seen for some time that it will do very I well. I hate always intended that she should marry and marry well. He is a gentleman born and bred, a gentleman to the finger tips, and she should like to find any girl her equal. even one born on the steps of a throne with a coronet on her head." He sat down on a bench, Presently, the girl struck a chord on the piano, and then her voice rose up—a strong, full voice, a rich voice, a sympathetic voice, a voice which was capable of hypnotising a young man if he listened long enough. "In the concert room," said the man on the bench, " she would make her fortune ; on the stage she would become the greatest actress of . her time ; as a musician—but she began late ' • as an artist—but her conceptions are better than her execution ' • colouring splendid, drawing wants fineness. Marriage—marriage with a gentleman. What must we tell him ? Italian parents? N. near relatives ? He must be told something his people would want to know—there's the rub —there's the difficulty. A girl without belong- ings except in Ratcliffe, a girl who might spring the most awful brothers and sisters upon her husband —but they don't know. An Italian 0011111 MarelleBi, perhaps ; South of Italy —Calabria, whete no one goes. The deception is innocent. Liz is dead and gone. Elise married. the lovely El itte, with her Italian eyes and hair—inherited from an organ grinder : her music, her voice, ller manners ! He s a lucky man : he will marry Out finest girl in England, daughter of a noble Ii hin family, my heiress —mine ; di ugh ter of my old friend, the Harchesi what ? . . . Alt ! she is finished. Now they will go on with their love making.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>The girl rose from the piano. " John," she • said with a very seirious face, " you have made site happy "—we need not report his answer, the ridiculousness of the lover has already been so ~ften illastrated—" but I have thought about things since this morning, and ;John, there is an ~sid almost before the beginning. Hush ! Yes ! know, but you must hear me out." She paused -mil looked out of the window while the tears •-rowdod into her eyes. " John, lam not at ali what you think me. You believe that I am an Italian, you have been told so, and a gentle- woman. You have understood so without being told —without being told. lam proud to think this, but it is a deception. I am not an Italian. no, simply the daughter of the poorest people in London. The young man turned.pale and gasped " You ?" " I myself, 'John, oh !mv guardian has done his best to make me forget my ;incestcrs, he would make people believe—but I insist not deceive you." "It isn't true," he said. "It can't be true. —you —the daughter of the poorest—" " Wait," she said, " I will show you what I was." She disappeared and in a few minutes name back, transformed. She had on a short white skirt, and pink tights, with white shoes and cs hite gloves : her hair was done in some ,trange fashim with a fringe,, a terrible fringe. . She sat down to the piano, and played a few " Now," she said, " I am Liz ; Liz, the jam girl ; Liz, who is proud to do a turn at this' Hall. lam Liz. This is my song." She began. in a coarse, loud voice and a Cockney accent, is is arse song, of which she sang one verse. " Lord :" said the man outside, " what on earth is she doing now ? That is her old song." Then she played a few more bars. " Liz used to dance as well. See." She performed a part of the dance which we Lave already seen on thse. hoards of the Music Hall. " I was Liz. QM: you understand ? You are a gentleman by birth :Is well ass education. Perhaps you cannot under-</p>
        <p>" You are Liz. You are Liz !" he repeated with every external sign of astonishment, for he remembered the girl. Her song, her dance, the shouts of the people, and the words of the man who sat before him ; the man who said that the girl might be made a gentlewoman ; the man who called the company, including his owo hrothers and sisters, a noisy and vulgar rabble. The evening came back to him—the whole even- ing ; the joy with which he beard the songs and saw the dancing ; the shame with which be listene.d to the man in front of him, and the per- -4.1.p t ion , for the first time, of what life might mean for one who could get scholarships and would work. " You sire Liz," be repeated, gaz- ing upon her. " I thought that you belonged— hy birth—" " You see I have no claim to that distinction. Therefore, Mr. Rutherford "—she held out her hand—" Farewell ! Y. u will be a great man ; 11,ere is nothing finer for a woman titan to be the wife of a great nom But I will not go to you with a lie perpetually in my daily life. Your people shall not reproach me with deception as well as Nimble birth.''</p>
        <p>" They, they," he eried—" you think me a gentleman by I.irth Why, Elise—no, not Liz— Elise—Liz is dead and gone, I am from the same place as yourself ; I remember you, the Music Hall, the dance, everything. I was there —I was there when you sang that song." " You ?" " I was Ihere, I say. My dear, it wasmy own secret that I feared to tell you —mine. I belong to what they kindly call the Kerb and the Gutter. my dear." He took her hand, and held it. "It is my secret, now yours and mine ; I shall rise in the world and anybody will find out our precious secret. One cannot bide it Only the obscure and incompetent can hide such a secret. They will say of Toe, which is quite true, that I am but a Poly. boy with a scholarship. If they like to say of you that you married the Poly. boy, if they say as well that you came also from the people, will it hurt you, my dear? Will it hurt —any who. may clome. after</p>
        <p>"John Oh John we 'belong to the same people —but—after all —you will be a great man. You can marry wherq you please. Will you never—never regret that—" The guardian at this juncture pushed aside the curtain and stood in the doorway. " Elise !" he asked with parental surprise, for this young lady in the unladylike disguise was in Ow arms of the young electrician. •• Sir," said the young man, I thank you in the name of }Time for destroying the Liz that 1 remember of Ratcliffe, where I was born." "Oh ! Lord !" the middle-aged philosopher sighed. " What is the meaning of this? Why, I only made her a gentlewoman for her to marry 1 gentleman." "And John," said Liz, "made himself a gen- tleman with much the same iptention, and now, after all, you see we are both contented with each other. Kerb and Gutter—John is the I suppOse, and I am the Gutter. Kerb and if-ut- ter,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LOCAL SHEPHERDRY.</head>
        <p>LOCAL SHEPHERDRY.</p>
        <p>I The annual report of the Warrington District of the Loyal Ancient Order of Shepherds (Ashton Unity) has been issued by Bros. Joseph Ashton, Pro. C.S. ; Joseph Mather, D.P.C.S.; James Parr, district treasurer, and Peter Edgar, district secretary. During the year 50 new members were admitted; death accounted for 17, ; and 35 allowed themselves to lapse, leaving the member- ship at 903, or a loss of two upon the year. The dis- trict funds stand at £6Ol 12s. 9d., as against £53865. 11d. the previous twelve months, and the funds of the nine lodges have a capital of £5,204 19s. 3d., as against , £5,111 11s. Oid., at the end of 1903, or an increase of 1193 9s. 4id. The lodges paid to the sick members the sum of £7773 75., or nearly £230 more than the previous ; year, and the district has paid from its funeral Ifund the sum of £228, which is in excess by £34. These figures are not regarded 'as satisfactory either financially or numerically. 1 There are no doubt many reasons, "continues the report" why the past twelve months have not come up .to our expectations and desires. The great depression 'which has been and still is being keenly felt throughout the country, has in our opinion been one of the chief Icauses why members have lapsed, and why our !enthusiastic brethren, that we are thankful to say there are amongst our ranks,havehad such difficulty in obtain- ling new recruits for our lodges. Men who are out of employment find it extremely difficult and hard to find I bread alone for themselves and those who are actually Idepfiltdent upon them, without the additional burden of 'contributing to a friendly society. It must be admitted that if lodges had in their most prosperous times estab- lished a fund which our predecessors have strongly and I urgently advocated—an out of work fond "—the same 1 would have been at the present time a valuable adjunct Ito such lodges. We commend the advisability of mem- bers of each lodge forming such a fund, and doing every- thing within their power to prevent the lapsing of mem- bers, End to encourage and persuade men who are out- side the pale of friendly societies to join our Order. Remember, brethren, we haye not only to fight against trade depression, but also in these days against societies I or clubs which are commonly called and known as "Ton- tine" and yearly dividing societies.' Very strong and pungent remarks were made in regard to these societies at our last A.M.C. Every opportunity, should, there- fore, be taken to explain the difference there exists between these and our own and other kindred socities. It must not be inferred that the foregoing remarks are made with the least idea to discourage and depress, but rather to stir us all up to greater energy in the future." The page of the report devoted to " obituary " gives the names of the persons who have died during the year, the cause of death, age, and amount of claims paid. The death of the two oldest men (SO each) is reported to have been due to "senile decay." Bronchitis aeconnnted for a death at Tit and there are six deaths at 70 or over that age, exclusive of those already men- tioned. Out of 23 deaths alluded to, there were 3 due to cancer. The total amount of claims paid was £228.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TO PROVIDE WORK FOR THE UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>TO PROVIDE WORK FOR THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>Corporation's Street Paving and Recreation Ground Schemes.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall on Wednesday morning Colonel A. G. Dumford R.E., conducted an inquiry on behalf of the Local Government Board into the application of the Corporation to borrow £5,252 for works of private street improvement and £1,500 for the Orford-lane and Victoria Park recreation grounds. Amongst those pre- sent were the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Lord, Benson, Ashcroft, Skelland, Evans and Ellison, Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), John Fair- eurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), and Dr. J. Guest Gornall (medical officer of health). It was stated that the population of the borough at the last census was 64,241, and the assessable value was £228,314. Outstanding loans in respect of the Sanitary and Public Health Acts amounted to £280,512 and in respect of local Acts for sanitary purposed to £74,257. A penny in the £on the Borough rate would produce £970 and on the General District rate £B2O. Mr. WILSON explained that the Corporation desired eight years to be allowed for the repay- ment of the loan of £5,252 for private street works. The Corporation had by resolution allowed property owners seven years in which to repay their apportionments. They found it was obsoletely impossible to obtain repayment of the money within that time and if the Local Government Board allowed the Corporation eight years as requested they would be able to clear off the loan without any difficulty. The INsPEcrore : The Board does not usually allow beyond seven.</p>
        <p>kr. Wrisili: If we have to pay within Iseven years the property owners ought really to pay a year in advance. We cannot get the money so early as a rule. The INSPECTOR: The obvious answer is to only give them six years.</p>
        <p>- Mr. WiLsoN, continuing, said the Corporation had received seven objections referring to four streets. Steps had been_taken to bring these cases before the magistrates, and he hoped within a month to furnish the Board with the decisions. The objections were not serious and he thought that the magistrates would have no difficulty in setting them aside.</p>
        <p>THE RECREATION SCHEMES. Mr. WILSON said it was estimated that £1,178 would be required for the purposes-of fencing and laying out a piece of land now used as a recreation ground oft Orford-lane, and for the provision of gymnastic appli- ances and a play-ground for children. It was also proposed to provide a quoiting ground with shelter and to plant a few trees. The land in question ;vas acquired by the Corporation on January 11th, 1895, under powers conferred by the Public Health Act, at a cost of £469 14s. which was paid out of the revenue of the Corpora- tion. The plot was leasehold for a term of 999 years</p>
      </div>
