      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CHILD RUN OVER BY A TRAM CAR. I</head>
        <p>CHILD RUN OVER BY A TRAM CAR. I</p>
        <p>£5OO Damages Awarded.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, an action was heard in which Blanche Maud Foster, through her father Thomas Foster, of Oxford-street, St. Helens. sought to recover damages for personal injuries from the New St. Helens and District Tramways Company, Ltd. Mr. Tobin, K.C.. and Mr. Maxwell were for the plaintiff ,and Mr. H. L. Riley for the defendant company. The case for the plaintiff was that on August 17,1903, when the plaintiff was four years old, she was going home from school with other girls. While crossing Duke-street, near the corner of Talbot-street, she was knocked down by a tramcar belonging to tke defendant company, and received such serious injuries to her leg and foot that she would be crippled for life. The I negligence of the company was alleged to consist in the excessive speed at which the car was travelling, as ' shown by the fact that it took the driver thirty yards to pull up. Dr. Masson, of St. Helens, described the child' injdries as a lacerated wound from the left knee to the ankle, tearing the flesh and exposing the fibula which was fractured in several places. The flesh of the foot was torn, and the ankle bones fractured. The foot was now inverted and wasted, and would never be right I again. The child's collar bone was also fractured.</p>
        <p>-The defence raised was that there was--itO-naiiigenee on the part of the defendant company, that the tramcar was travelling at an ordinary rate, and that owing to tha child rushing out right in front of it in such a way that the driver could not see her, the accident was unavoidable.</p>
        <p>The jury found in favour of the plaintiff, awarding £5OO, to be invested for the benefit of the injured child His lordship gave judgment and made an order in accordance with the verdict.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE VETERAN. On Tuesday evening the members of Runcorn Co- operative Society presented a cheque for 300 guineas to Mr. Jonah Evans on his retirement from the post of general manager and treasurer. He had filled this position for thirty.seven years. Mr. Evans was one of the founders of the society.</p>
      </div>
