      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>GREAT LAWN TENNIS PLAYERS.</head>
        <p>GREAT LAWN TENNIS PLAYERS.</p>
        <p>Their Methods Illustrated.</p>
        <p>In a few more weeks the green sod will have sufficient spring in it to justify the marking-out of the tennis court and the over-hauling of "tackle." Last summer was an ideal one for lawn tennis players, and the suita- bility of the weather for the game brought about the I introduction of a large number of tyros. Both the latter I and their more experienced brethren will, we are sure, ; learn with interest of the publication recently, by Messrs. Macmillan and Co., of " Great Lawn Tennis Players their Methods Illustrated?' The work has been produced by the collaboration of Mr.G.W.Beldam. i who s responsible for the illustrations, and Mr. P. A. Vaile, who supplies the text, and the book has been I prepared on the same lines as Mr. Beldam's " Great Golfers," which proved such a notable success last year. The illustrations,which number over:two hundred,are not "posed "photographs, but are in every instance taken in actual play. They represent the most characteristic strokes of the players, and it v;ill be found that every player of note, both man and woman, is included in this remark- able series of action-photographs. Each picture is dealt with in detail by Mr. Vaile, who has also much that is critical to say of the gfip and tactics of English players, as well as many sucTertions to offer that should prove of interest alike to experienced and the inexperi- enced player of Lawn Tennis. The work contains an article by Mr. E. G. Meers on" The advanced tactics of the game " and on one "The Half Volley," by Mr. G. A. Caridia, the well-known exponent of that stroke. Students of the game will not begrudge the price, 12s. Gd., when they discover how much they can learn from the profusely-illustrated pages of this, the ablest compendium on lawn tennis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NEW Ha:HER-GHAT-4: SCHOOL FOR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>NEW Ha:HER-GHAT-4: SCHOOL FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>..... At their meeting on Tuesday, Runcorn Education Committee decided to build a two-storey school for standards V., VI., and VII., to accommodate 300 boys and 300 girls, and Mr. Jos. Wilding, surveyor tO Run- corn Urban District Council, was appointed architect to prepare plans, at a commission of five per cent., to include supervision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>from 1866; the chief rent was .£7B ss. 5d., of which £l7 ss. 11d., was repaid to the Corporation by five under-lesees, leaving a total payable by the Corporation of .660 19s. 6d. The Warrington Corporation Act of 1899 gave the Corporation power to provtide pavilions i &amp;c., n the parks, gardens and recreation grounds, and to borrow moiley for the purpose. The BOROUGH SURVEYOR gave evidence in support of the application and explained tho plans. In answer to the INSPECTOR it was stated that there would probably be a small charge for the use of quoits, &amp;a., although the matter had not yet received con- sideration.</p>
        <p>With regard to Victoria Park, Mr. Wilson explained that .2322 was regnired for the erection of a shelter, pavillion, and unclimable fence, together with other works in connection with the park and bowling green. Under the Corporation Act of 1899, Victoria Park, which was 52 acres in extent, was required for the sum of .£lO,OOO. It was proposed to make a small charge for the use of the bowling green.</p>
        <p>TO PROVIDE WORK FOR THE UNEMPLOYED. Mr. LONGDIN having submitted further details of the schemes Alderman Smethurst supported the application On behalf of the other members, of the Council present he could say that they in Warrington were in the same position as he believed existed in London. There were men out of eniployrwent and the Committees were very anxious to push forward the schemes nanted in the ap- plication in order to provide work for the unskilled labourers in particular. Trade had been very bad in the Borough for sometime and the committees were en- deavouring to do what they could to alleviate the suffer- ings of the working people. Consequently the Copora- tion applied for the loan, believing that the improve- ments would be beneficial to the inhabitahts of the town at large. He asked the Inspector to use his good offices in order that the sanction of the Board might ,be ob- tained as quickly as possible. The INSPECTOR said he was holding inquiries for the remainder of the week, but he would send in his report early next week, accompanied by the Council's request for an early decision on the grounds mentioned. No doubt the Board would facilitate matters in every way. Councillor JAMES EVANS endorsed what Alderman Smethurst had said. He understood that the work was already due to be carried out. The Council had on several occasions considered the unemployed question, and they thought the improvements would be a means whereby they could relieves, to a considerable extent, the distress that was prevalent. The inquiry then closed.</p>
        <p>NEW SCHOOLS FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Education Authority have decided to erect a temporary iron school near Windle Schools, Cooper-street, at a 'Kist of £2lO, to accommodate one hundred scholars and plans have beeen submitted to the Board of Education for the Thatto Heath Council School. They have also pressed the Vicar of St. Helens and the Managers of the Denton's Green Infanis School to proceed with the erection of a permanent school to replace the present temporary building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LAU, =GEM 11,ESIS1/710.1</head>
        <p>LAU, =GEM 11,ESIS1/710.1</p>
        <p>KERB AND GUTTER,</p>
        <p>SIR WALTER BESANT (Antkor of "Al! Sorts and Conditions of lien," V.)</p>
        <p>In the small, cheap music hall beside the public house tl:e usual East End throng were gathered. In the gallery were the boys and the girls with their sweethearts. Sometimes the girls paid when the boys had no money: there is no false pride on that score. In the body of the hall sat rows of working men, with pipes and beer, and the wives of working men ; small clerks were there also : mates of the ships, for it is a riverside place ; and a company whose various occupations may be guessed as long as the observer pleases because he will never succeed 'in finding out. Among them, this evening, sat two of t!..y&gt; up- per sort : the company recognised thrtsu as ohms :" they sat together on one of tta fore- watt benches, and they looked about then;: they looked at the audience more than at the stage: they had come to that music hall with the pur- pose of looking at the people, not of hearing the songs. _</p>
        <p>"'resently one of them got up. "We have had enough," be said, " let as go." " Stop for this next turn. See, it's a girl thiA time, sit down again. The funny man retires at last."</p>
        <p>Amidst a rapturous shouting of "Liz ! Liz I" the girl came on quietly. She did not bound on the stage like the ordinary dancing girl, with a flare and a wave of the hands, nor aid she swim on gracefully with a sweet smile like the singing woman. She emerged quietly from the side look- ing very serious and rather anxious. She was quite a young girl, not more than fifteen or so. iSitC was dressed in a short white skirt provided by the management, and in pink tights. She haq not " made up" her face, so that she looked hor- ribly pale in the strong gaslight ; her hair and her eyes were black ; she wore a terrible fringe over her forehead ; and she had long white gloves hiding niest of her arm : it would have been es well if the glove had bidden the whole of that limb, because what was revealed was red and suggested work with sleeves tucked up. • " Liz Liz Liz !" they cried. _ _</p>
        <p>" One of our gals," said a young fellow of the clerkly profession. "Two or three of them do their turn at the Halls. Liz ! Liz ! Liz She's the cleverest. Listen ! she's going to sing. She can sing too, and she can dance round you." The orchestra struck up a lively measure and the girl began. She had a rough voice, un- cultivated but strong. The high notes were good and full, the quality of the voice was quite good, and she sang in tune.</p>
        <p>It was a sentimental ditty that she gave het audience. When she finished they cried for more. She struck up a song of the comic kind. It de- manded that terrible gift, the power of " arch- ness." The girl tried to be arch : she was so young and so badly trained that the desired ef- fect failed, but the people knew the song and bawled the chorus, and did the archness for themselves. Then the orchestra played another tune, and she began to dance. It was a curious dance, apparently of her own invention. It be- gan with the figure which you may see performed by the children in the neighbourhood of Drury Lane on the asplialte, to. the barrel organ. Thi. figure is the first taught to their elder sister iii the corps de ballet. The girl on the stage wool titiOngil it conscientiously, holding her skirt.; and her feet quite properly. Then el., changed her dance and broke into something very much like a hornpipe, or perhaps it was a break- &amp;WTl.</p>
        <p>"Liz ! Liz : Liz !" shouted the enraptured company. She bowed gravely and retired. " Liz ! L;z !" But she would not come on again. " I told you," said the young clerk to hi, friend, " Liz ! Liz ! Liz ! that she could dance and simr. Now you see. Liz ! Liz !" " W4'; Lave found an artist," said 0110 of tie gentlemen to his friend. " Your geese are all swans as wual." " „ . _ _</p>
        <p>" With—great gifts. If I know a yoke when I hear it, she has one ; if I can recognise a fine ear, she has it ; if I undendand dancing she M 1 danseuse. She looks artistic through and throngb. I couldn't see her finger tips but I am sure they have the true artistic shape." "Go and make her acquaintance then, over a glass of beer." " She is the sort of girl," continued the man of observation, "that one Would like to make to lady."</p>
        <p>"She in already a lady. They are all ladle, except the men who are all gentlemen." "A highly bred and refined gentlewoman," " Oh ! come—come."</p>
        <p>Take her; out of this rabble." He looked about the room his eyes fell on a lad behind him. "This noisy, vulgar rabble. Surround her with an atmosphere of gentle companions, gentle manners, gentle speech, gentle ideas. Train these talents of hers. Then you shall see the value of that heredity you talk about so much." " Come," his companion took him by the arm, " lot us catch a tram or a 'bus or something and get away west."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 11.</p>
        <p>The lad on whom the speaker's eyes had rested —a boy of strong and rugged features—a boy born in Yorkshire—listened hard and fervently, but after the two were gone, changed colour and was both ashamed and angry. For the first time in hie life be learned that the people in the music ball—his own people—were a noisy and vulgar rabble. They were veritably his own peo- ples: his brother be knew was in the gallery with his sweetheart, his sister was there as well with her chap, his father was not there because he was in the public house. As for himself, he seldom came to the ball ; home still remained his ideal place of amusement because be spent his even- ings over his books. He was on the lowest rung, but he was on the ladder which leads the fortun- ate to dizzy heights. In other words, he had al- ready obtained one of the small scholarships open to lads of the Board Schools ; he was working to get Eanother ; he had begun already to understand that there were possibilities for him even beyond the place and rank of foreman of works, which he had hitherto regarded as the crown of all ambi- tion. "A noisy and vulgar rabble !" The boy read other books outside his technical subjects : he read all the books upon which he could lay hands: he knew what was meant by these words. He looked round him, the scales fell from his eyes, be understood his new position, his heart tank like lead, he was only one of a noisy and vulgar rabble. He got up and left the "noisy and vulgar rab- ble" of the Hall; he walked home through the noisy and vulgar streets, where the girls sareamep and laughed, and the lads hustled and pa.shed each other and the girls ; and be saw that they were of a truth a "noisy and vulgar rabble. He thought how one scholarship might lead to another, the first for the lower kinds of technical learning, that which makes the good craftsman ; the second for the higher kind, that which makes the foreman in the engineering works ; the third —ah! the third !—of which he had hitherto thought little—it leads to Oxford and Cambridge, the teaching and profession of science, to the black coat and superior manners of a gentleman. So with a new ambition he reached his borne, and seized his books.</p>
        <p>CILLPTER 111.</p>
        <p>Seven years later the girl strolling alone across the lonely fields was dressed with quiet taste which is not unusual in a gentlewoman. Her face, if a face can express the truth, was stamped in every line with refinement, culture, art, kind- ness, and all the womanly virtues, including the final gift, that bestowed by Veaus herself, which makes a girl not only lovely and sociable, but willing to be wooed, and ready to sacrifice herself for the man she loves. No one would have be- lieved that some years ago this girl had sung a fearful comic song and danced a breakdown upon the stage of the most humble of music halls. She herself had almost forgotten the little episode in her history. It was so far away, she had been taken from her old companions so long, her name, her face, her mind, her manners her dress, her language, had all been changed; it was only occasionally that she remembered vague- ly the childhood spent in the streets, dau.,ing and playing on the pavement, the constant unpleas- antness of drink among the residents, the poverty, the unsatisfied cravings after food and clothes, the Board school and the teachers,—all these things had become like a dream to her, and a dream splendid with sunlit mist which hid the outlines. She was now the adopted daughter of a gentle• man—none other than the man who had discov- ered in her the possession of those artistic quali- ties, who took her from the noisy rabble and edu- cated her, and surrounded her with the atmo- sphere of the gentle life, And, as he said at the Music Hall. "Where isitour heredity now f." _</p>
        <p>• immah—••</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND THE SECRET ABOUT ZAIIII-BUK. GLEANINGS. Interesting Facts About a Household Need.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND THE SECRET ABOUT ZAIIII-BUK. GLEANINGS. Interesting Facts About a Household Need.</p>
        <p>M. LIPPAIANN has obtained coloured fringes from interference of the waves of light by reflection of abeam of light from two mirrors perpendicular to one another. His experiments were brought A clear, healthy skin is a treasure rarer during the before the Academy of Sciences, Paris. bleak month of Marah than at any other season. If one has escaped the skin eruptions which are often the out- THE waste gas of coke furnaces is now utilised corn 'ls of the winter's close confinement then roughness scaling and other blemishes are liable to arise. Zain: for providing motive power. Installations of the Buk, the new healing balm, forms an ideal emollient sort exist at Altenwald, in the basin of the Saar, for both emergencies. Minsterstein, and Neunkirchen. Motors of 303 to Whoever watches the healing of a wound or chronic 600 horse-power are driven by means of the gas. sore with Zam-Buk is face to face with one of Nature's greatest wonders. The healing process thus set in motion is nothing but a phenomenon of regeneration— a natural process of replacing destroyed tissues by new ones. The antisepetic substances contained in Zam-Buk first attack and kill off the microbes or germs that are the cause of the inflammation or disease, and then the healing ingredients proceed to build up new tissue to replace that which has become damaged or diseased. New cells appear like a builder extending a new row of Messrs. Siemens and Halske, of Berlin, have houses ; the whole of the wounded area is gradually invented a method of preparing calcium cyanamide , overlapped ; and soon it is difficult to tell where the by heating lime and carbon in a stream of air in a injury or sore has been, so perfect and complete is the growth of the skin. resistance furnace. The cyanamide fixes the The secret of Zam-Buk was discovered by an eminent nitrogen of the air without previously forming chemist, who recognised the imperfection of existing calcium carbide. , ointments, salves, and embrocations (which too often, ' contain rancid animal fats and mineral poisons), and turned his inquiries for the right article in the direction of those rare medicinal herbs growing beyond the borders of civilization. His shrewdness brought him in touch with some of nature's grandest secrets; and the production of Zam-Buk, after a long period of experimentation, marks a new era in the art of healing. Zam-Buk is Nature's perfect cure for chronic sores, chafed skin swellings, abscesses, pimples, ulcers, eczema, scalp disease, running sores, piles, bad legs, diseased ankles, festering sores, poisoned wounds, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, sprains; and for soothing all aches and pains. Price Is. Ild. per box, or 2s. 9d. for large family size (2s. 9d. box contains nearly four times the Is. 14d.); of all chemists, or:post free from the Zam- Buk to., 4, Red Cross-street, London, E.C. Zam-Buk keeps the skin soft like velvet, notwithstanding the worst March winds.</p>
        <p>ME. E. BUCKINGRAM. of the United States Depart- ment of Agriculture, finds that the proportion of carbonic dioxide in soil increases, while that of oxygen decreases in going downwards. The propor- tion Of nitrogen remains nearly constant.</p>
        <p>TITANIUM AS FIREPROOFER</p>
        <p>Remarkable fireproofing properties are credited to oxide of titanium. Flannelette treated with it could not be made to burst into flames, and other textiles are claimed to be made equally resistant, while it cannot be removed by dyeing, boiling, or washing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PEBBLE-LIKE PLANT.</head>
        <p>A PEBBLE-LIKE PLANT.</p>
        <p>Burchell, the traveller, discovered in South Africa a curious plant resembling the pebbles of the ground. It is of the genus Mesembrianthemum. Several varieties of this plant, which appears to mimic the 0011 es among which it grows, are described in the Transactions of the South African Philosophical Society. One species, the M. bolusi, growing about Harm, produces two leaves about the size of -a duck's egg, of a brownish green, resembling stones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SCIENCE-STUDY IN THE DESERT.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE-STUDY IN THE DESERT.</p>
        <p>Botanical and zoological stations for experiment or observation multiply in different countries, but perhaps the first established in a desert is that of the Carnegie Institution. It is near Tucson, Arizona, and two biologists, Messrs. Colville and MacDowzal, have been quartered there studying the flora of 'the locality, especially cacti. Among these is the yucca Radiosa, which keeps the drifting sand from burying it by lengtheniva its trunk. One specimen rooted in the soil was found with a trunk rising through a sand-dune about 30ft. high. NATURE'S INVENTIONS BEAT MAN'S.</p>
        <p>There is probably no human invention which nature has not already worked out beforehand for the convenience of some of her creatures, says Science Siftings. Take, for instance, the hypo- dermic syringe, which was heralded with a flourish of trumpets. Its exact counterpart is found in the sting of the scorpion, almost the earliest creature of the rocks. The electric light is a great boon, but it is not a new thing. The deep sea lines of the Challenger brought up fish which, swimming in ocean darkness 3,000 fathoms below, have a row of lamps fitted along each side to light them in the pursuit of food. There is a spider which makes a balloon and knows how to manage it perfectly. And one of the builders of great lighthouses confesses that he got his most valuable suggestion from the bole of a tree.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ELECTRIC IGNITION lAIMOTOR-CARS.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC IGNITION lAIMOTOR-CARS.</p>
        <p>The most troublesome part of a motor-car is the electric ignition. In many ways, which are only learnt by painful experience, it is possible for the electric arrangements to fail to act. A general adoption, however, of the form of ignition apparatus devised by Sir Oliver Lodge would seem to over- come many of the difficulties in the way of motor- travel. In principle, Sir Oliver proposes the con- necting of two Leyden jars, charged by a coil, so far as their outer coatings are concerned, by a piece of wet string or other bad conductor. This pre- vents the second spark of the discharge circuit from taking place, and thereby renders the 'first very tame. The discharge is of an oscillating character, \ and of great frequency, so that it ceases to obey the laws which control ordinary currents, and assumes a character which is exceedingly well suited for motor-car practice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OXYGEN IN SURGERY AND MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>OXYGEN IN SURGERY AND MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>Oxygen gas has lately extended its usefulness in medicine and surgery. It is employed as a cure for anthrax,t pleurisy, intoxication by carbonic oxide, asphyxia, strangulation, drowning, sea- sickness, and so forth. As a deodorant and bacteri-- cide it is also effective. Perhaps the most practical way of getting the oxygen is by means of "oxylith " (or oxygen stone); a substance • prepared in the electric furnace and yielding pure oxygen when dissolved in water. A simple apparatus for generat- ' in" it has been brought before the Academie de Medicine, Paris, by Dr. Albert Rodin, a hospital doctor. It produces the pure gas at a pressure of fifteen centimetres, chiefly for the medical prac- titionera. Ten cakes of oxylith produce seventy- five to one hundred litres of oxygen. Each cake weighs fifty grammes. Physicians and surgeons may like to know that the oyxgen apparatus can be obtained at the Societe l'Oxylithe, 115, Rue Cardinet, Paris, and that a little book on pure, oxygen in surgery, by Dr. Schmeltz, of the Faculty of faris, has been published by Jouve, Paris. It describes the bacterial action of the gas proved by the work of Dr. Schmeltz at the Hospital Saint Antoine, Paris, under the direction of the well- known Dr. Thiery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MANUFACTURED DIAMONDS.</head>
        <p>MANUFACTURED DIAMONDS.</p>
        <p>There have been several attempts to make the diamond, but they have only succeeded in produc- ing tiny stones. M. Henri Moissan, the distin- guished French chemist, formerly made some crystals, and WS recent analyses of the Callon Diablo meteorite have led him, says the Globe, to try again. Repeating his old experiments, he got similar results—namely, small transparent crystals of a "drop" and eight-faced shape. Sir William Crookes has likewise obtained clear and black crystals by the Moissan process. MaioTazia, iy means of pressure and heat combined, has also made microscopic diamonds. The Diablo meteorite contains small diamonds in fissures of the metal connected by cracks to the nucleus of sulphide of iron. It seems likely, then, that sulphur has dis- placed the carbon from carbide of iron. The meteorite also contains phosphorus and silicium, which might intervene in the separation of carbon. By melting Swedish iron in an electric crucible and adding sulphide of iron, then plunging the crucible into cold water, Moissan obtained little diamonds. The cold water penetrating the carbon crucible cooled the iron towards its heart, where the liquid carbon solidified in the form of dia- monds. The diamonds thus obtained by adding sulphide of iron are like those made without • if, but there are more of them. Diamonds made by addition of silicium are more irregular in form. The addition of phosphide of iron gave no dia- monds. M. Moissan concludes that carbon, at ordinary pressure, submitted to a high tempera- ture, vaporises and yields graphite, whereas carbon liquefied under a high pressure yields diamonds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ST. HELENS OUTRAGES.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS OUTRAGES.</p>
        <p>Murderous Attacks on two Policeman.</p>
        <p>A dastardly attempt to murder a St. Helens police- man was made just before midnight on Monday night About 1110 shots were heard in the somewhat lonely locality of Boundary-road and Knowsley-road. Several persons rushed in the direction, and found Police-constable Adams badly wonnded. Medical aid was summoned, ; Cotton quickly attended. He found that the OA-1 had been shot in the left shoulder, and several pellets were lodged in the body. The constable was removed in the horse ambulance to St. Helens Hospital, and was followed by the doctor. It is supposed that Adams was shot by a poacher or poachers, several of whom are known in that district. The officer has been responsible for the appearance of several game trespassers at the police court recently. A much more serious affair happened shortly after this at the village of Eccleston, about two miles on the Knowsley side of St. Helens, where a man shot at Police-constable Howells, of the county constabulary, hitting him in the breast.. The incident took place at a point between the Eccleston police station and the village, and when he was found the constable, besides being shot, had been, stabbed itithe head and behind the ear. Assistance was rendered. but Howell is in a critical state. Howell is a well-known officer and previous to about two years ago was stationed at Bold Heath for some years. He has been in the force sixteen years. Constable Howell lives at Rainford-road, just outside St. Helens, and a Press representative called at his station on Tuesday morning, and saw Mrs. Howell, who gave her husband's account of the terrible affair. It appears that about a quarter to twelve o'clock on Mon- day night, Constable Howell was walking along from Eccleston Village in the direction of the police station at the corner of Mill-lane. When he was about forty yards from the polioe station, and walking slowly, he saw a flash, and he thought his coat was on fire. After recovering from the surprise he found that he was injured, and said, "Lord help me. lam shot." He across the road and found a man crouching under the high hedge on the opposite side. The man said, "I am sorry I missed you." Howell said, " Very likely ; but you have not missed me." He then closed with the man, and they went into it "ding-dons," and the constable put the man down, and during the struggle he recognised him. After struggling for some time, the constable was obliged to relax his hold, owing to his left arm failing him. He found that he was getting weak from loss of blood, and the man then drew his knife, and the struggle recom- menced. It was very dark, and no one came to the rescue, and the struggle went on for over twenty minutes. The officer lost his lamp, whistle, and truncheon, and h. was stabbed three times on the left side of the head,and once on the back of the neck.ln the struggle the poacher drew his knife right through the constable's wrist, over the ball of his band, and up his finger. The assail- ant was determined to do mischief, and in defending his throat the constable was cut several times on the mouth and chin.</p>
        <p>After they had wrestled for about twenty minutes the constable became quite exhausted and the poacher said to him, "I have done you." Howells said "No, you have not done me. lam able to do a bit yet." The man then said, "Go on your knees," and the officer although never giving up, felt that it was highly probable that the man was going-to load his gun again and shoot him dead. He seemed to think better of this however, and went away in the direction of Eceleston. Dr. Wilde, of Prescot, was brought to Howell, who pre- sented a most terrible sight, being covered mith blood fsom head to foot.</p>
        <p>THE POLICE DESCRIPTION OF THE WANTED</p>
        <p>- MAN.</p>
        <p>The following is the police description of the man wanted in connection with the outrage :--David Earp, widower, about 51t. 7in. in height, blind of the right eye, with small features and shaggy brown beard, turn- ing grey ; dressed in a dirty brown suit, and wearing a cap apparently thrown carelessly on, but drawn down over the blind eye. The desperate character of the attack cn the two constables, find the frightful struggle which Police- constable Howells had with the man with the gun, was the sole topic of discussion in St. Helens and Prescot on Tuesday. The police are under the impression, from some inquiries, that the perpetator of the outrage may have committed suicide, but although every pool and plantation for a considerable distance round was thoroughly searched no trace could be fonnd.</p>
        <p>OVI FORTUNES IN DIAMONDS. The £5,100 which the famous Agra diamond realised at Christie 's seems a relatively small price (says the " Westminster Gazette ") to pay for a gem of over 31 carats, with a romantic history of nearly four centuries. The " Hope " diamond, a sapphire-blue brilliant only IS carats heavier, is:valued at £20,000 to £30,000; and there is among the Crown jewels of Russia a red diamond of 10 carats only for which £15,000 was paid. Among larger atones, the " Orloff," which blazes in the sceptre of the Tsar, was exchanged in 1772 for £90,040, an annuity of 4.000 roubles, and a title of nobility; tit* Duke of Orleans paid £130,000 for the Pitt diamond; which is now valued at three times that sum. The " Koh-i-noor" is worth every penny of £120,000; the " Excelsior " in its natural state was assessed at around million pounds ; and even this sum would not probably purchase the wonderful stone recently found at the Premier Mine, which turns the scale at 11b. doz. avoir- dupois.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DANGERS OF MARCH WINDS,</head>
        <p>DANGERS OF MARCH WINDS,</p>
        <p>No month in the year is so fraught with danger to _ j those in only medium health as March. Spells of warm , sunshine tempt one to cast off healthy winter clothing. IMPROVEMENT IN MICP.OSCOPY. Then without warning comes a keen, biting, east wind, An improvement in microscopy has recently chilling one to the very marrow. A system already attracted the attention of experts in Jena, Germany, debilitated by the trying winter cannot withstand this, which Prothises to increase the possibilities of and hence March is a month o with many f sickness research by that art. It utilises an entirely new people. Chill, rheumatism, influenza, and other dis- I greaers are now very common. The best way to treat principle of radiance. When ordinary sunlight ,is I colds and chills, and that weakly tired feeli ig which analysed by a prism, says an American paper, tne , precedes influenza, is to have a hot bath, take a cup of observer sees all the colours of the rainbow. At I good beef teat and a couple of bile beans, and turn into disorder common in March is that of blood eviouslv warmed. one end of the series is a violet band, and at the other a red one. The rays which emanate from the , impurity. Winter's heavy eating fills the blood with 1 a Anotherbed p r di former arg shorter than the Others. Out beyond I various impurities which reveal their presence in the the visiblr violet rays are others having a still form of pimples and skin eruptions. A course of bile shorter wave length, but ordinarily unseen. Glass , beans will be found to speedily dense the blood and obstructs them, but a prism of quartz transmits , put the weakened digestive and circulatory system in them. These "ultra violet" rays have a high . order. photographic value. The astronomer is thus enabled ! By creating pure, rich blood, and stimulating circu- to obtairt„, records of lines in the solar spectrum , lation, bile beans do all that can be done to ,brace and which he r cannot see. Now, some of the Jena