      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>many of the advantages to which Mrs. Gilman refers are already enjoyed by soldiers living in barracks. There everything is systeinktised. There is one mess instead of 100 kitchens, and one or two hundred domestics doing the work of half a dozen. But some- how or other barrack life does not appeal to our modern sense of happiness and comfort. Labour saving im- provements may be all very well, and even they are un- necessary if one is content to live in a sentry box, but to do as little work as possible is not everyboly's am- bition in life, and the happiest woman is the one who takes a pride in her home, instead of seeking to spend as much time as possible out of it. MADGE.</p>
        <p>Dress at the Second Court. —Although the King gracously intimated that coloured toilettes might be worn at the second Court, all those ladies personally known to their Majesties, and many others who have not that honour, set aside such dresses, and appeared in mourning or half-mourning for the Grand Duke Sergius. The dressmakers had a most harassing time, and the expense of the second toilette fell heavily upon many women who really had little or no choice in the matter. Those who had selected white, also mauve, soft greys, heliotrope, or any of the half-mourning shades, had reason to congratulate themselves, and indeed it is said that many women to whom a second Court gown would spell serious financial difficulty, invariably choose these tints, in case of emergency. A Debutante's Court.—Princess Ena of Batt3nberg's gown was the one note of relief in the Royal Circle. It was of white satin, veiled with tulle chewing a tracery of silver. The toilette was as simple as possible, no lace, or flowers appeared thereon, and the Princess's jewels were pearls only. It was quite a debutante's Court, and a novel note, much, in evidence' was the Idea, of lining the train with some pale colour. To some girls theSll-white presentation gown is by no means becoming, and to such, this innovation will prove a welcome one. A Flower Season.—Roses, roses everywhere, in the millineryjworld, and tinted in retch weird and unnatural colourings as to render them infinitely more curious than pretty. Purple, pale blue, violet, terra cotta,green, brown, and black roses, are in great request, and it seems a thousand pities when so many exquisite shades, true to Nature, are available. Violets are being used to cover little shapes entirely, such as the "pill-box" or the small round toque with deep brim, the sole trimmings being a cluster of roses on one side. The • all-flower headgear however is rarely a success,a certain harshness and stiffness about it proving anything but becoming to the majority of women. Bridal Fashions.—A rainbow " procession of brides- maids has been seen upon several occasions lately, and the success of the scheme is undoubted. At a recent society wedding the bride in snowy white was followed by two whiteclad pages, then by a pair of "blue maids." Next came two white maids, then two pale pink, two , more white onces bringing up in the rear. In each case I the flowers carried matched the gown, and the whole effect was charming in the extreme. The fashionable bride is just now aiming at great simplicity. She I wears a simple dress, a plain tulle veil, and no jewels of any kind. A Society Leader.—Lady Algernon Gordon-Lencox. who only waited to present her young daughter, Miss I Ivy Gordon-Lennox, at the first Court before proceed- ; ing to New York, is considered the smartest dresser of I the day. Lady Algernon gets most of her toilettes from Paris and Vienna, and is always a little in advance I of everybody else. Her coiffure too, is wonderfully elaborate, and very Parisian in style. Miss Ivy Gordon- Lennox, who is just eighteen, inherits much of her mother's good looks, and taste in dress, and both in Dublin and London, and now in New York, has been , the object of much interest and admiration. The Hobbies of a Marquis.—The Marquis of Bute whose engagement is just announced is not yet twenty-four,and succeeded on the death of his father in 1900,t0 property I CHILDREN'S COUGHS worth several millions, besides the magnificent seats, ' Cardiff Castle, Aiountstuart, Dumfries House, and Moch- I Mrs. BALLIN, the great authority on Childrep's rum in Wigtownshire. The Marquis, however, is little I Diseases, strongly recommends seen in Society drawing rooms. Sport and travel are I so far his only tastes, and he is no sooner back from a I VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. shooting expedition in Hungary than he is off on .a Mrs. ADA S. BALLIN, 5, Agar-street, London. fishing excursion in Norway. Having seen Pretty nearly Editor of Womanhood," writes:—" VENO'3 LIGHTNING all that Europe can offer in the way of sport, the COUGH CURE is an exceedingly successful remedy. It Marquis has now gone on a big game shooting expedi- is very pleasant to take, and the relief it gives is very tion in Central Africa, and expects to be away some rapid. The preparation is perfectly safe far children. months. I Airs. HAGUE, Ratcliffe Crescent, Lockwood, near Sir Walter Scott's Home.—When in London, the Huddersfield, writes :—" My tittle boy suffered from Marchioness of Bute resides at St. John's Lodge, hoarseness and bronchitis. We all thought he never Regent's Park, where a grand ball and garden party will would be cured or speak plain again. After taking be given during the season. In her husband's lifetime, VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE he was able to speak Lady Bute had the choice of several splendid country plainly. I have nevsr seen anything to act so quickly." seats as a place of residence, but strangely enough there I Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUCH CURE at Chemists is no Dower house belonging to the Bute title, and the and Drug Stores. Price 93d., is. lid. and 2s. 9d. Sold Marchioness has taken a lease of Abbotsford, belonging everywhere. to Mrs. Maxwell-Scott. Mrs. Maxwell-Scott, who is related to Lady Bute, is a great-granddaughter of Scot- land's rereatest noveDst, but she has not resided at Abbotsford for many years. Though not by any means a large place. it costs at least £5,000 a year to keep up. It was Scott's ambition in life to enact the part of a great feudal lord, but his enjoyment to the home he had created with so much pride and joy was destined to be brief one. it was the beginning of the financial diffi- culties which overwhelmed him, and for many years before his death Sir Walter Scott was a stranger to Abbotsford where he returned to die in September, 1832. Lady Warwick goes Electioneering.—Lady Warwick lias long been a champion of trade unions and the Workineman, and she is now completing her arrange- ments for a motor car tour round all the constituencies which will be contested by a Labour candidate at the General Election. The rule which prevents peers from taking an active part in Parliamentary elections, does not apply to the peeresses, end Lady Warwick is well acquainted with all the arts of electioneering. Soon after her marriage some twenty-five years ago, Lady Brooke, as she was then, greatly helped to return her busband for Colchester, in the neighbourhood of her Essex property. Lord Brooke was of course a Con- servative. "I was very young then," Lady Warwick explained many years afterwards, and added that since she had taken a deep interest in educational and social matters, she had become a Liberal. She is sometimes called a Socialist, but theueh she has had her motor call painted red, Lady Warwick does not advocate any alarming revolutionary doctrines. The Ideal Home.—Mrs. P. C. Gillman, who like Dr. Osier, comes from America,has tiled much to say against the modern system of bousekeeping. Domestic indus- try she says, remains much the same as it was in the Stone age, which means that nearly half the labour in the world is wasted in useless toil. In the ideal home of the future, which Mrs. Gilman pictures, there will ' be no cooking, servants or housework. All meals, seientifically prepared and cooked, will be brought in from some central depot, all cleaning will be done by experts, called in, and there will be no corners or un- necessary hanging to collect dirt or dust. There will be no fireplaces, one switch will turn on warmth, another light, and a third the ventilating fan. Every- thing in fact will be done and regulated by clockwork, and babies will be boarded out at nurseries where they will be brought up under the most scientific system. Such is the picture which Mrs. Gilman points of the the ideallboine, and which she thinks will brighten the existence of future generations. It may be so, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE.—A special service was held at Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel on Sunday evening to the 'memory of the late Captain John Harrison, of Birch- dale, who for many years was a prominent worshipper ,at the chapel. The Pastor, the Rev. J. S. Hughes occupied the pulpit and preached to a large congrega- tion from the text "So he bringeth them unto their desired haven." In the course of his remarks Mr. Hughes said that during his long career on sea Mi. Harrison had had:many thrilling experiences which com- bined with his steady and devout character, made him an exceptionally interesting personality to converse with. Mr. Hughes related three thrilling episodes in the life of the late captain, how he was run down by another vessel, near New Brighton, his own boat being well nigh out in two, his mate lost, and himself nearly drowned, how he was the victim of a murderous attack on the part of a revengeful mate, and how he was wrecked ten years ago, near Fleetwood, he and his mate clinging to the rigging for four hours during a terrible hurricane, he praying and cheering to his mate during the whole time It was great heroism on the part of a man in his 66th year. That wreck was disastrous to him, for he lost his vessel, but amid all these strange dispensations, he never lost faith in God. With indomitable spirit ha started again, and continued to pilot a vessel until he met his unfortunate accident about a year ago. He was a quiet, unobtrusive Christian man, ever ready to bear his testimony for God and religion. Suitable hymns, the favourites of the deceased, were sung, such as "there Is a land of pure delight," "Rock of ages," "Jesus, lover of my soul., ' The choir sang a hymn iof which he was very fond "God be with you till we meet again." Mr. James Strange, jun.. presided at the organ and played the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
      </div>
