      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A.</p>
        <p>A good muster of members were present at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. Mr. D. Owen occupied the chair. Mrs.Hannan sang in brilliant style. " The Promise of Life " and "The Everlasting Day," being accompanied by Mr. B. Wadsworth. Mr. Robbins, who had just rdiurned from a visit to Wales, gave a capital address on" The Revival," which created a favourable impression on all present, the speakea remarking that he was in hopes of a revival [akin place in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>• The last meeting of the twelfth year of this society was held on Sunday, when, three new members joined, and were heartily welcomed. The year closes with 589 enrolled members, Mr. J, W. Slack presided, and spoke upon " Think." He said it was not always to all men an easy task to think rightly. As a brotherhood they should do all that lay in their power to get, men around them to think rightly, especially upon profane and bad language, intemperance and gambling. According as they could get men to think seriously, so would these evils deminish. Councillor Barlow, of Urmston, spoke upon" Is not this the Carpenters Son 2" He said the greater part of Christs life had been spent as a worker. The Jews had a spirit of depreciation of the Carpenters Son, but Christ honoured labour, and Christ to day honoured the labourer, and despised the lazy. Man by the sweat of his brow, earned bread and provided for those dear to him. Too often the profes sional looked down upon the workingmen, commonly understood. In the truest sense all were working men, and God honoured labour at the anvil, and the mill, as greatly as he honoured the man in the office, or the law. They were all workers together for God and Man. Miss Lucy Travis sang effectively " The Toilers". Mr. W.H. Payton presided at the organ, Mr. W. Beawick jun., and the Rev. John Yonge, the president, also took parts in the meeting. The secretaty announced the 12th Anniversaky to take place next Sunday, and hoped they would have a crowded church. He remarked that ex- cept on Christmas Day and one other Sunday,new mem- bers had been enrolled every Sunday during the last 6 months. He hoped for a large enrollment next Sunday to begin the new year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DISTRICT NURSING ASSOCIATION</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NURSING ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>Successful Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>As a practical way of showing their appre :iation of the splendid work of the Warrington District Nursing Association a band of young people promote 1 a sale of i work in aid of the nstitution, It is gratifying to record that the function, which took place in Cairo-st. school on Saturday, was a distinct success, the net sum realized 4beingl £2O. The arrangements had been entered into with commendable enthuziastr Miss Whit- ney ani Miss Baskerville discharging te, duties of secretary and treasurer respectively. The schoolroom, with its attractively-arranged and heavily-laden stalls, presented a bright apparauce, one of the most noticeable features being an exhibit of cleverly executed fret-work by Mr. Gibson, of Hardy - street. The following acted as stall,. holders :—Mixed stall, Mrs. Whitney, Mrs. Dixon, Miss Marsh; useful stall, Miss P. Baskerville, Miss Dreher, and Miss Houghton; fretwork and picture stall, Miss E. Whitney and Miss Trowe; fancy stall, Miss L. Baskerville, Miss N. Baskerville, and Miss Whittaker; refreshment stall, Mrs. Blease and several helpers; bran , tubs, Miss G. Baskerville and Miss B. Whitney. The sale was formally opened by Mrs. James Fairclough, Mr. F. W. Monks presiding. The CHAIRMAN expressed his regret that owing to illness his father was unable to attend. As regarded the sale; he understood the promoters had done all that; work as a sort of thank offering and in order to show their appreciation of the servites which the nurses were rendering to the inhabitants of the town in general. Those who had organized the sale had themselves experienced the benefits of the attentions of the nurses and the idea entered their minds firstly that as they could not contribute as much as they would like in money, they might by working collectors snide various kinds and,by a Little effort on the part of them- selves and their friends,turn them into cash to be placed at the disposal of the Association. He believed that, upwards of four hundred tickets of admission to the sale had been sold, and they hoped to see a large attend- ance later in the afternoon and in the evening. Mrs. FAIRCLOUOH said she was sure no words of her's were needed in commendation of the very excellent insti- t ion for which the sale had been promoted. Everyone present knew the splendid work that was being done by Miss Whitfield and her staff, and the untold comfort they had brought into hundreds of homes. She had no doubt all their patients appreciated their services, but it was not given to everyone to show that appreciation in the practical way that the Misses Whitney and BaskerviHe and the other workers had done. All the reward they asked for their ' months of work was that the articles might soon be sold and a substantial sum handed over. She hoped every visitor to the sale woulddo his or her utmost in further. , Alice of that most excellent object. (Applause.) ivrs. Fairclough having been presented with a beautiful bouquet, the sale was proceeded with. , During the evening an enjoyable musical programme was submitted. Selections were given at intervals by Miss Williams's mandoline band, and there were also phonograph items by Mr. W. Whitney. Songs were creditably rendered by Miss W. Jones, Miss Lena Etchells (Halle's choir, Manchester), Miss Marsh, Miss Walker and Mr. J. Hannan; while Mr. and Mrs. Hannan were heard to advantage in the duet "Love's dream is o'er." Humorous sketches by Mr.E. A. Sanders created considerable mirth. Mr. T. Taylor ably discharged the duties of accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IN FAVOUR OF BAZAARS. Archdeacon's Reply to the Vicar of Burtonwood.</head>
        <p>IN FAVOUR OF BAZAARS. Archdeacon's Reply to the Vicar of Burtonwood.</p>
        <p>• Opening a sale of work at Runcorn on Saturday, the Ven. Archdeacon Wood said that although the function was not a bazaar, yet it was a bazaar's younger sister and he thought that what was sometimes said about bazaars was intended to include sales of work and all their relations. He was not at all disposed to regard them as did his rev, brother on the other side of the Mersey(the Rev. A. NI. Mitchell, vicar of Burt° nwood) who had been telling everyone that of all the wicked institutions there were in existence bazaars were the worst. He could not help thinking that he must have got among a very bad set of bazaars. On the whole he • (Archdeacon Wood) was disposed to think bazaars were very good things indeed. They had their drawbacks,but what in the world was there that had not. He had no doubt it was true of bazaars or of other things that a I great deal dopended upon the people working them. Mr. ; Mitchell said they made the ladies flirt, that they swindled everyone connected with them, I that they produced "copper Christians," and ; various other things. He did not agree that I bazaars produced "copper Christians." What ; produced "copper Christians" was carelessness and indifference, not bazaars, he was sure. What produced ! flirting, he did not know. (Laughter.) There was a very sensible leader in a Liverpool evening newspaper on Mr. Mitchell's statements. The writer said with regard to flirting it was not at all unlikely to be carried on wherever a great many people were brought together, and that bazaars had not a monopoly in the matter. (Laughter.) Then as to the allegation of swindling. jf people came to bazaars to buy bargains—and he was told a great many people did in Runcorn—one must be disposed to think that the swindling was on their part, and not on the part of the people who sold. (Laughter.) He did not think in connection with the many bazaars held there that there had been anything but very fair dealing, but if people came with plenty of money in their pockets and did not know what to buy, and wished to get rid of the money for the ' benefit of the object, he did not see why the young ladies should not assist them And receive as much as they could. (Hear, hear.) If a stallholder had an I article for sale at 2s. 6d., and somebody liked to pay 1 10s. for it there was no law of morality or religion to I prevent the money being accepted. He was very much i surprised that the Vicar of Burtonwood charged bazaars and sales of work with making people drink. The writer of the leader said that might be true, but that , the drink must be tea. (Laughter.) No doubt a great ' quantity of tea was drunk, but it was perfectly harm- less. The practical advantage of bazaars was that they enabled an immense number of people who otherwise would be unable to contribute to combine in a friendly way to give their labour and interest, and as soon as a clergyman found that the people had reached exhaustion, the best thing he could do was to set them to the I task of getting up another bazaar. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>J. P. DUGGAN.</head>
        <p>J. P. DUGGAN.</p>
        <p>I have met with some very stratge characters behind prison bars. Some were philosophers, sotne were fools, or " mostly fools."---but those whom I have now in my mind's eye were marked by special "peculiarities" which marked them off from their fellows.</p>
        <p>One such ram aria was Thomas R—, now undergoing a sentence of seven years' penal icr.ritude for what some thought, at the time, was but a foolish freak and, others, a grave •:riminal offence.</p>
        <p>The son of a solicitor in South Lo4idon, Thomas had some private means of his OM. and devoted his time to the study of anarchism and—clocks. A•ttaching himself to a Socialist Club, near 3reenwich, he became an apt pupil in that school of thought "—or thoughtlessness—and vhen, after the murder of Presider. t Carnot, in Paris, his successor refused to cminute the leath sentence passed on the murderer, Thomas determined to strike a blow at all governments (by way of reprisal) and dropped a pennyworth of gunpowder into the letter-box of an ipoffen- sive postmaster in Greenwich. _</p>
        <p>. „ There was little or no damage done and the whole thing would have been forgotten in a week. .But this did not suit Thomas. He wanted to be famous and he thought that his ..vhiff of gunpowder had made him so. Finding that little noise was mule about. this exploit, he wrote a letter to the postmaster idding defiance to all constituted authority and, ts a result, he was arrested aud tried, under tin- " Explosives Act," for this outrage on the letter- box. He was found " Guilty "and Mr. Justice Bruce sentenced him to 7 years penal Servitude. . I chanced to be one of ' a batch of nine prisoners who were transferred frctu Chelmsford Gaol to Parkhurst Convict Prison in June 18—, and, when we entered the train at Waterloo, found that Thomas was my viz a rig. Ile was in a conununicative mood; and. .as the rules of discipline are somewhat -relaxed on 'ltch occasions, we had a lengthy conversation on the journey between Waterloo and South- _ ampton. . _</p>
        <p>"thomaS had, somehow, got it into his mind that he was a " political ' prisoner and »ot a ~ornmon felon; and onel of the first Wags be !tsked me was " what the papers had to shy " about his doings. He was, evidently. tinder the impression that the whole of England, from F,and's End to John 0' Groats, was ringing with' the letter-box fiasco, whereas, so far as I knew, not a single paper had made the least comment ttp.on _ .</p>
        <p>'To all appearancr, Thomas was of sound in- tellect ; he could converse coherently and, in- .leed, learnedly on most subjects, and altogether he made a rather favourable impression at this ttrst interview.</p>
        <p>We had not been in Parkhurst many days Lefore Thomas proved himself to be a " crack- pot." I met him one morning, on the Pxoreise ground, and the following dialogue . might nave been heard by anyone standing within a :ew yards of us. Coming behind me, he whispered : " I say, No. 43, isn't this a beastly place F would like to blow the whole prison up, avernor and all." I was anxious to turn the conversation into mother channel, aud knowing that Thomas eturued his , dinners to the kitchen daily, uii touched, I asked him why he didn't eat his :neat and soup. " Never ate meat nor drank soup in my</p>
        <p>"But, why? Are you a vegetarian 2" " No, but I never did eat meat, and, as they voir't give me anything in place of my dinners, which I always send back, it is evident that the authorities are starving me." I tried to impress the plain fact upon him that he was starving himself; but Thomas would not have that at any price and, with a contemptuous sneer at my stupidity, wound-up the conversation with this tremendous pro nouncement : You are thick-headed, No. 43. Were you not so, you could see things as they are. I inn a dangerous political prisoner and a sworn ,oiemy of all Governments. The cabinet would like to kill me, if they could do so decently; but, instead of sending one of their assassins into my cell to murder me outright, they choose to le tine die of starvation, by withholding food that I could eat, and forcing on me what they know I can't eat. But I am not going to die until I strike, a blow at Downing Street that will shake the Empire.", Another remarkable ciaracter, still behind the bars, is the man Bonscm who is serving 5 years in connection with a stock-exchange swindle: lie is a wealthy man, and his one idea, when I knew him; was that his riches would purchase his freedom There were several other " monied men" behind the bars at Parkhurst in my time, and, -Aunt of liberty, they could—some of them did —buy anything they took a. fancy to (in a quiet, 2landestine way, of course). One such man was Saker, the junior member of a firm of solicitors well known in Lincoln's Inn.</p>
        <p>Unlike Bonson, Saker was an Israelite who had no guile. Simplicity was his distinctive ' mark and peculiarity; and ,it was always a puzzle to me how such a man could have been guilty of the fraud on account of which he is now undergoing 10 years' penal servitude. The mystery was cleared up one day when Saker—who then had charge of the workshop in which I was employed—gave me a brief his- tory of his case. Saker was the victim of circumstances. The firm (of which he was junior member) became involved in very serious difficulties and the" In- corporated Law Society " took up the matter. Saker—although a rich man and but recently married —offered to take all responsibility (under certain conditions) and he was arrested on a , charge of fraud. As everyone acquainted with legal life knows, there are very often complications—wheels with- in wheels—which baffle even the most astute minds and make an intelligible appreciation of facts very difficult. This was the case here. Saker was com- mitted for trial and, in due course, was removed from Holloway to Newgate Prison where (on the eve of his trial) lie was visited by the heads of the firm and received an assurance that the case would be put on the list of the then Re- corder (the late Sir Charles Hall) and that, if he pleaded guilty, he would get off with a very light sentence. • This arrangement was agreed upon and, next day, Saker was placed in the Old Bailey dock before the Recorder. To the surprise of every one in Court and, especially, of Saker himself, Sir Charles Hall rose from the Bench and seeming to remember, suddenly, an important engagement elsewhere, he adjourned the Court until the following morn- ing. One result of this unexpected incident was that Saker's case was transferred to "No. 2 "Court. Not wishing to depart from the arrangements made with his partners, he pleaded " Guilty " to the charges and, to his dismay, was sentenced to 10 years penal servitude by a Judge known to. be particularly severe in dealing with fraudulent solicitors. Saker made several attempts to have the case re-opened : but, as there is as yet no court of criminal appeal in this country, he spends his days and nights vainly looking out, from behind the bars of Parkhurst Prison, for something to "turn up" whicti may deliver him from the cage wherein he has already passed 4 years. From behind the bars of the same cage an- other remarkable Convict is also looking out; but with eyes directed, not towards the New Law Courts, but towards the Treasury, against the First Lord of which he says he intends to bring an action for compensation, for false imprisonment. This gentleman's name is Josiah Grayson. He is an American by birth, a speculator by pro- (having forfeited all " remission • Marks '')—an Cl will claim 2/6 per diem for the whole of that time, he intends to proceed against the Treasury to recover the modest sum of £319 is. lid. ODbj release from prison. I met with a very large number of men be- hind the bars who would have done credit {.6 any society : university men, professional men. business men and men of no particular calling but with some means at their command. The hitter class is, generally, the im-Nt " of- fish and least inclined to sociability. Thit I, of this Oit.remely reticent :111d select body 1 convicts got himself into enmities one day t rthwent..l %lin of the ten::•!te.</p>
      </div>
