      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SUGGESTION TO MADAME PATTI.</head>
        <p>A SUGGESTION TO MADAME PATTI.</p>
        <p>We have a suggestion to offer to Madame Patti, says the Sketch. Why should she not write her Re- miniscences? Few women living have had a more interesting life and have come across so many distinguished and notable people. She was the petted darling of that brilliant Imperial Court which vanished, as in a dream, from the Tuileries. She has been on terms of really intimate friendship with many Crowned Heads and their Consorts, and, as those who have the privilege of her acquaintance are well aware, she is an admirable racontexese.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>• POULET AU RIZ A L'ITALIMiNE. —For the rice, boil 40z. of this carefully as for curry (Patna it should be); then, after draining and drying it, toss it with loz. of butter till hot in a clean, dry stewpan; stir till thoroughly mixed with the butter, season with pepper and salt, add sufficient tomato sauce or puree to moisten the rice nicely, and finally stir in about 2oz. of finely-grated, dry, mild cheese, Parmesan or gruyere to he quite correct. The fillets of a freshly-boiled fowl, or of a cold one carefully reheated, are then mixed in With the rice, and th whole served piled up in a dish together, a rich white sauce and grated cheese being handed round with it; or else the chicken arranged in the centre of the dish and the rice round it.</p>
      </div>
