      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Pouses anti ?Laub on ale.</head>
        <p>Pouses anti ?Laub on ale.</p>
        <p>The text of the</p>
        <p>THE CONDEMNATION North Sea Commis- OF ROJETESTVENSKI. sion's report com- pletely disposes of the statements which were circulated a few days ago. It is a severe blow to Russia, and must be received in St. Petersburg with something like consternation. Confident persons in Paris had assured the world that the Commission had no only exonerated Admiral ROJIIESTVENSKI, but had laid down the principle that a navel com- mander who thinks his ships in danger is justified in firing on any neutral vessel that may have the misfortune to be within gunshot. Such a ruling would have made life on the high seas in war time a ceaseless terror, and it was not sur- prising that strong protests were heard on all sides except the Russian side. But the Paris forecasts turned out to be quite ludicrously wid e of the mark, and we can only ascribe them to Russian sympathisers who have more zeal than I sense. The Commissioners have virtually adopted the British case. They find that Admiral ROJIIESTVRNSEI is personally responsible for the order to fire on the trawlers, and that the order is Wholly unjustifiable. The Baltic Fleet were in no danger. The alarm was simply a chimera begotten of reports which the Admiral had been unable to' verify. He had instructed his officers that they must fire if they had reason to believe in an "evi- dent and imminent attack." This instruction, says the report, was perfectly sound. This is I obvious, and it helps us to understand how the Commission was credited with the absurd conclu- I</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>sion that a naval officer is justified in firing on any vessel that seemed to him suspicious. What the report declares most emphatically is that on the Dogger Bank there was no "evident and imminent attack." There were no torpedo-boats. DON QUIXOTE was not more grotesquely wrong when he mistook a flock of sheep for a company of giants, than ROJIIESTVENSKI was wrong when his mistook the humble British trawlers for the dreaded foes. We have always said that when the Russians fired on the Swedish cargo steamers they were not in a fit state of mind to distinguish between enemy and neutral, between one kind of shin and a totally different kind. The majority of the Commissioners are clearly of the same opinion. They accept unreservedly the evidence of our fishermen. The trawlers were exactly where they ought to have been. They were exhibiting proper lights, and when their commander set up a green mega he was using the ordinary singal of his fleet So grossly ignorant was the Russian Admiral of the Dogger Bank and the fishing fleet and the usages familiar to every sailor in the North Sea that he mistook the green rocket for a Japanese signal to attack. Then the ridiculous Captain KLADO saw torpedo boats which had no exist- ence, and the Russians fired not only on the trawlers, but on their own ships. The cruiser Aurora was damaged, and it was not until the cruiser had signalled :his identity that the fusilade of panic was discontinued. Tne report hints ivory plainly that evidence from these particular vessels had been suppressed. If the Russian agents had been animated by any candid desire to get at the truth, they would have produced witnesses from the Aurora and from another cruiser that was mis- taken for an enemy by :the wondrous KLADO. Instead of doing that they founded their case on the self-conficbnce of that officer, who fondly imagined that he had captured the Commis- sion. Except the Russian Commissioners, who persisted in the fable of the torpedo boats, the members of the tribunal declined to accept Captain KLADO at his own valuation. It is true that the report endeavours to soothe the Russian susceptibilities. It blames Admiral ROJHESTVENSKI for not intimating to the British authorities what had happened, but absolves him from the charge of inhumanity in sailing away • without rendering any kelp to the unfortunate victims of his bluader. We have our own opinion about that. We have not the slightest doubt that he discovered the mistake, but was determined not to admit it. He has been backed up in this obstinacy by his Government, and the Russian case has been a characteristic mixture of fiction and bounce from first to last. The report is partly judicial and partly diplomatic. On its judicial side it is a complete vindication of the British attitude ; on its diplomatic side it pays a tribute to the martial qualities and the " humanity " of the Russian culprit. What may be the " martial qualities " of this extraordinary sailor we do not know. It is significant that a Commission com- posed of distinguished Admirals says nothing in praise of his seamanship. We cannot expect that he will receive even a repremand from his Govern- ment, but it is sufficient, perhaps, that he stands condemned before the world for an act of unpar- donable stupidity.</p>
        <p>Goir.g, going—and nearly gone is the Govern- ment's majority already. Thursday was the open- ing day of the routine Parliamentary session, and the majority of the Governme-lt on three divi- sions was 24, 26, and 28. This for a party which came into power with a majority of 134! Truly the omens of Thursday for Mr. Balfour and his ministers are indeed evil. They have practically lost control of the House at the very commence- ment, of what is obviously going to be a very stormy Session. Defeat stares them in the face, and it will not require many more such hair- breath escapes to make the ministerialists care not how soon it comes. Their friends are wearied of the situation, and the opposition may not even need the "snatch " division about which thoy have often been twitted. It will be no consolation to the Ministry to find that , North Westmorland has remained true to the cause once espoused by Mr. Richard Rigg. The latter's recantation has had no effect on the political views of the dalesmen, and they have returned Mr. Leif Jones in the same triumphant fashion that was Mr. Rigg's good fortune in 1900. Even hope, which lives eternal, must well nigh have departed from the breasts of Mr. Balfour and his followers.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott has wisely taken the trouble to explain what was meant by his recent assertion 11 that he should "have no interest" in the Warring- ton election. Naturally, such a remark from so advanced a progressive as the Secretary of the Trades and Labour Council created considerable surprise in the town. Was Councillor Stott going to sulk in his tent on the election day like a certain warrior of old ? seemed to be the tenour of the criticism aroused. We are glad to :find from his letter that such is not Councillor Stott's intention by any means. "As fart as:my vote and the result of the election are concerned," he says, " I shall be very much interested." This may be taken to mean, we should thiLk, :that Councillor Stott realises the importance of throwing every grain into the scale against the mem- ber who is an avowed Protectionist and has proved himself no friend to Labour. His vote on behalf of Mr. Crosfield is apparently assured and we hope that his fellow mem bars of the and the L.R.C. will be as ready to exercise their electoral privilege in the same direction when the time comes. Councillor Stott gives excellent reasons why his active support should be transferred from Warrington to New- ton Division, and we cannot quarrel with him for exercising his undeniable right of personal choice in the matter. If he and his friends vote in Warrington and work in Newton it is as much— and as little—as can be expected by the Liberals But this much they do rely upon, and that is tha the Labour men who are disappointed at no having a candidate of their own will take care that the importance to themselves of the issue here is not obscured. The return of the Conservative candidate will set back the cause of Labour a good many years, and that is a calamity every Labour man should du his utmost to prevent.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jesse Collings, M.P., the faithful follower of Mr. Chamberlain,. has sent a very plain, but a very remarkable, letter to a Nottinghamshire farmer with regard to Protection and its effect on the agricultural interest. The letter, (which is pub- , Imbed in the "Nottingham Daily Express ") says: "The farmer cannot grow corn in face of the competition of the whole world. If Protection were given him—and o.othing ahort of 61. o ss. per qulrter would be of any use—it would, I fear be of no use to the tenant farmer. As soon as, prosperity comes to the tenant farmer through Protection the landlord takes advantage of it to a large extent and sometimes wholly by raising rents. Therefore Protection to an amount to be of any use is impossible under our present system. My remedy is that contained in the Land Purchase Bill—namely, to turn the tenant farmer into the yeoman owner of his farm."</p>
        <p>The above extraordinary expression of opinion from such a quarter is likely, we should imagine, to cause an alarmed flutter in the dovecotas of the Tariff Reform League. Here we have the candid admission from Mr. Jesse Collings that Protection, to be of any benefit to the farmer, :Lust go to the extent of six or eight shillings per quarter on im- ported grain—thus opening out the prospect of an enormously dear leaf fcr the working-classes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>And this impost, it appears, is not even for the ! benefit of the tenant-farmers, but, as the Free Traders nave always contended, it will go into the pockets of the landlords in the shape of increased rents. Mr. Collings also admits, with astonishhig simplicity, that Protection is impossible under our present system. What will his revered chief, Mr. Chamberlain, say to this ? And what, too, becomes of the appeal made by Mr. Chamberlain in the rural districts on behalf of his agitation ? Verily, we are living in strange times ! This outspoken letter from Mr. Jesse Collings mc.kes it almost unnecessary for Free Traders to devote any more time to the defence of their cause. Workingmen must take note that Protection to help the farmer means slx or eight shillings a quarter on imported grain— the lanalords to pocket the plunder and the people to pay. The vote at the next election can alone enable the people to defeat this fraudulent scheme, inaugurated by Mr. Chamberlain, to make the poor poorer and the rich richer.</p>
        <p>The call to arms is producing a ready response from the Liberals of Stockton Heath. They are determined to do all in their power to put au end to the reign of Toryism in the big constituency, and every effort is being made to educate the electors on the vital questions which so much concern the welfare of the country. The public meeting on Tuesday eveaing was well-attended and the thoughtful interest with which the speeches were listened to was in itself encouraging as show- ing an appreciation of the great issues involved. From Dr. Massie, the treasurer of the National Liberal Federation, came a trenchant criticism of the Government's misdemeancurs. His exposure ,of the obstrucbive Tory tactics in Parliament gave the meeting some idea of how discussion is stifled and we hope Stockton Heath voters, along with the majority thorughout the country, will show their unmistakable disappoval of the discredit- able methods adopted by the Government to attain its ends. In the short time allotted to him Mr. King, the prospective candidate, showed how our home trade is hampered by exces- sive national expenditure which curtails the spending power of the people, and helps to swell the ranks of the unemployed. Mr. A. H. Crostield attended to encourage Mr. King in his plucky fight and he pithily summed up the Government's policy when he said they had "deliberately neglected to study the interests of the greatest number of the nation." Next Tuesday, Stockton Heath people will he afforded an opportunity of hearing Mr. W. H. Lever, who as a manufacturer with a world-wide trade, and works in England, America, Germany, and France, can speak from experience of the great benefits accruing from our Free Trade policy.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday when, judging from the agenda, there will be a less protracted sitting than of late. .Considerable progress is reported in several directions since the last meeting. Some of the special resolutions which were than passed, have already been acted upon with a celerity somewhat remarkable. Sub-committees have been appointed to consider what steps can be taken for feeding poor children ; to make known to Companies and others intend- ing to build new works the suitability of Warrington as an industrial centre; and to report upon the best means of providing a labour registration office. This newly-acquired expedition in the dis- patch of public business is admirable, and encourages one to hope that it may be applied in some other directions where stagnation has long been apparent. For instance, what about Warrington Bridge widening ? The Council passed the plans and let the contract for the work months ago, but there is no sign of work being commenced. We do not suppose this is the con- tractors' fault. Who is to blame ? Perhaps Alderman Smethurst will inquire. This trifling with a public improvement is little short ot a scandal, and the delay suggssts that there may be influences at work which the Corporation, if a self- respecting body, should ignore.</p>
        <p>We notice from the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee that a Corporation appoint- ment has been conferred upon Mr. W. L. Williams, the chairman of the Junior Gmservative Associa- tion. Out of a large number of applicants he has been appointed, subject of course to the approval of the Council, to the vacant position of road-foreman at a salary of £2 per week. Two amendments to the recommendation are to be moved by Councillor P. Peacock.</p>
        <p>There was not the slightest element of opposi- tion to disturb the serenity of the proceedings at the Local Government Board inquiry on Wednes- day. Comparatively few people will, we imagine, be disposed so quarrel with the Corporation for proposing the expenditure of £6,750 on private street works and the laying out and equipment for various games of Victoria Park and Orford•lane recreation grounds. In the interests of public health it is of first importance that streets should be in a thoroughly sanitary state, and the corpora- tion have been repeatedly urged to undertake more work in this direction. Recreation ground schemes, too, are of almost equal necessity, for the children in many congested neighbourhoods have far too limited facilities for indulging in beneficial outdoor recreations. The Inspector was asked to impress upon the Local Government Board the importance of an early decision in the matter, as the Corporation are anxious to provide work for upemployed labourers by carrying out the schemes almost immediately. The desire is to be com- mended, for it will,at any rate, give the authorities an opportunity of ascertaining how many genuine unemployed there are in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages. 2 Work for Unemployed. Local Shepherdry. Science Notes, etc. 3 Women's Chat and Fashions. Local Churches. Nursing Association. "The Land Monopoly," by Mr. C. E. Parker. Building Society Meeting. 7 National Free Church Council. Football. Billiards. Rural Council. LADIEs' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Personal. Mr. A. Dean, assistant curator at the Warri ton Museum has been successful in his application for the curatorship of the Belfast Museum. Mr. Dean received his appointment under the Corporation in 1901. Mr. Charles E. Place, assistant market inspector has been appointed to the position of chief inspector of weights and measures and markets inspector under the Wakefield Corporation. Mr. Place also entered the servim of the -Warrington Corporation in 1901. He was one of 90 applicants for the post, and is to be congratulated on his success. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Adjourned Licensing Sessions. These sessions will be held at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday when the business will include the consideration of the renewal of the George Hotel licence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Associated Chambers of Commerce.</head>
        <p>Associated Chambers of Commerce.</p>
        <p>The forty-fifth annual meeting was held at the Hotel Metropole, London, on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday :last, the Warrington Chamber being repre- sented by the vice-president (Mr. Holman Kingdon) and the hon. secretary (Alderman Bennett). Mr. Wilkie the Candidate for Dundee. Mr. Alexander Wilkie, Newcastle, general secretary of the Associated Shipwrights' Society, has been adopted as Labour candidate for the Parliamentary representation of Dundee. Mr. Wilkie, it will be remembered, was recently requested to become the Labour candidate for Warrington but his executive declined to allow him to accept the invitation. Scholastic Success.</p>
        <p>Harold Dutton, a student at the Warrington Grammar school, passed in the first division of the London University matriculation examination held in January last, the results of which have been published. Two thousand candidates were presented, 225 being placed in the fist division and 666 in the second division, the remainder failing to satisfy the examiners. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt Collision In Arpley _Road.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hall, farmer, Of Daresbury, was proceeding along Arpley-road, on Saturday evening in charge of a horse and spring trap when he came into collision with a motor car from Chester. The harness arid trap were damaged, and the horse was led across the bridge to Mr. Wilkinson's shop. Twenty minutes after the accident the animal which had been badly frightened, fell to the ground and died. Mr. Hall was considerably shaken but otherwise escaped injury.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OP TONICS. I 63. SANKEY-STREET, WaTringtoH.—Ad. Meeting of the Warrington and District Bowl- ing League. A well attended meeting of the Warrington and District Bowling League was held on Tuesday eyen- ing. It was decided that the League should be con- tinued and a sub-committee was appointed to consider the rules. The majority of the clubs which were connected with the League last year have again signified their attention of joining and a successful season is .anticipated. The question of running a second division has been considered but nothing definite has, aa yet been done in the matter. Servants' Sad Suicide.</p>
        <p>_ On Thursday morning Miss Rachel Barton (31), domestic servant in the employ of Mr. Weaver, 223, Wilderspool Causeway, was missing from her employers' house. Upon a search being instituted her body was found in and recovered from the river in the rear of Wilderspool Causeway. At the inquest last night the evidence showed that the deceased had suffered for some time from religious mania. The Coroner handed the jury a letter left by Miss Barton, the contents of which he declined to divulge to the Press. It is understood, however, that one sentence ran "I am too wicked to live."—A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.—Advt. Progressive Labour League. The weekly meeting of this league was held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening. Mr. 'T. Barnes presided and was supported by Mr. J. Howes. A paper on "Patriotism " was submitted by Mr. Hitchen, and an interesting discussion followed. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Committee for their admirable arrangements for the demonstration in the Co-operative Hall. Promises to address future meetings have been received from Mr. Fred Maddison, candidate for Burnley, and Mr. E. Vivian, candidate for Birkenhead, in addition to Mr. Charles Fenwick, M.P., and other prominent Labour men. At the weekly debate on Tuesday next Mr. J. Elwell, junr., will introduce the subject of " Free Trade." Warrington Poor Boys' Summer Camp. A committee meeting in connection with the Boys' Summer Camp was bek: at the Education Offices on Monday afternoon. Mr. J. Moore Murray presided, and there were presbat Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins(secretary),Mr. A. H. Lee (treasurer),and the Rev. Father Whittle. The only business was to arrange a meeting of the sub- scribers to be held shortly to receive the secretary's report and balance sheet and to appoint a committee for the coming year. It is to be hoped that the public generally will again this year show a practical interest in this deserving institution and enable the poorest lads of;the town toenjoy a healthful holiday at the seaside which would otherwise be denied them. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTSIN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Warrington and District Boot Retailers' Association.</head>
        <p>Warrington and District Boot Retailers' Association.</p>
        <p>The members of this newly-formed association held a meeting at the Horsemarket-street Cocoa Rooms on Monday evening, Mr. Owen (Public Benefit) presiding in the unavoidable absence of Mr. W. Roberts, the president. Matters of importance were discussed, the chief item being the earlier closing of the shops. At present some shops are open until 8.30 and nine o'clock and the members felt that there was room for considerable improvement. It was the unanimous 'opinion that something should be done in the matter, and the question will be considered again at a future meeting for definite steps to be taken. It was resolved to add the name of Councillor Ellison to the list of vice-presidents.</p>
        <p>A Magistrates Practical Sympathy. At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday two youths, John Hargreaves and William Worsley were charged with sleeping out.—The evidence showed that the lads had been sleeping out off and on for twelve months. The defendants said they had tried to get work but failed. Their parents had turned them out. Mr. E. Woods (Longford Wire Works), one of the magistrates offered to find work for the defendants, and upon their undertaking to accept the offer they were discharged. On Monday they presented them- selves at the Longford Wire Works and commenced work.</p>
        <p>Testing the Water Suppy. Periodical tests of the supply of water available at the War- SatrauraddaeybaYfternoon a trhineglteocan Fmiarenufßarcitgoaridees, aarned 10)1ei l n visit was paid to Messrs. P. Walker and Sons' brewery in Dallam-lane. Mr. Frank Turner (superin- tendent) was in charge of the brigade which was well represented. Numerous tests were made, and, on the whole,it was found that there was a satisfactory supply of water which could be thrown to a great height. By the tests the Brigade acquire a knowledge of the conditions at the principal works in the town which should prove of the great value in a case of an out- break of fire. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 12 guineas with change speed gear Ste., unequalled.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Ad Vt.</p>
        <p>Goncert at the Workhouse. The inmates of the Warrington Workhouse were on Saturday evening entertained to a concert organized by Mr. E. Birchall and Mr. J. Isherwood, the efforts of the entertainers without exception meeting with their heartily expressed appreciation. " The Provi- dence Glee Party " contributed" Pro Fundo Basso" and a "Laughing chorus " for each of which they received an encore. The glee party were Miss Mona- field, Miss Grounds, Mr. E. Grounds and Mr. W. Grounds. Mr. J. Shakeshaft sang " Carrie Lee "and "Barque of Dreams,'. and Mr. A. Grounds gave "Mona " and " Anchored." For the latter he received an encore. and responded with" The Village Blacksmith." Miss Mabel flame gave two excellent mandoline solos " Alice where art thou" and " Killarney," and Master Willie Rhodes received hearty applause for his song "Jfark, I bear the angels sing." Mr. J. Yates introduced gramophone selections, and the humorous element was well sus- tained by Mr. J. Isherwood and Mr. E. Birchall. Mr. Herbert Millwood was the accompanist. Presentation to Miss Shaw.</p>
        <p>Miss Shaw, v,ho has just retired from the position of head-mistress of the infant department of the Parish Church Schools, after 47 years' service, was on Tues- day afternoon made the recipient of a handsome diamond ring subscribed by the scholars. The Rector, in making the presentation, spoke of the length of time Miss Shaw had been headmistress, and of the high esteem in which she was held by all with whom she came into contact. Ile was sure they were all regretted that she had severed her connection with the school. (Applause.) Miss Shaw, in reply, expressed her great gratitude to the children, and hoped they would all grow up and do their best to be a credit to the school and the church. (Hear, hear.) Mrs. Stansfield, headmistress of the Lower Girls' school, also referred to the great loyalty and love they all felt for Miss Shaw. Miss Taylor, headmistress of the Upper Girls' schools, added her tribute, and expressed the hope that Miss Shaw would live many years to enjoy her well-earned rest. Songs were given during the afternoon by the children.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Women's Liberal Association Meeting.</head>
        <p>Women's Liberal Association Meeting.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance at a meeting of the Warrington Womens Liberal Association which was held at the Liberal club on Monday evening. The ladies were entertained to tea by Mrs. Jackson iiolmes and Miss Bessie Dutton. After tea Miss Dutton g tve an interesting address on " Thrift " dealing with both the need for imperial and individual thrift. Mrs. Jackson Holmes read a carefully pre- pared papek on " Vivisection " in which she strongly denounced the cruelties practised 17 vivisectionists. Both ladies wcre heartily thanked for their speeches and hospitality.</p>
        <p>"PR E lER " CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— J. S. GMLLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.— Advt. . Alderman Bennett and State Railways. At the annual meeting of the Association of Chambers of Commerce of the United Kingdom com- menced in London on Tuesday, Mr. Cunningham (lunfermline) moved that in the opinion of the associ- tion "it was decided that a Royal Commission should be appointed to call evidence and ascertain whether Iv, have the best possible system of conducting our railways; and, if not, whether it would be for the advantage of the trade and commerce of this country, and ultimately to the benefits of the State,in its financial relations, that the railways should become national property.—Alderman Bennett (Warrington) seconded the motion, remarking that the present railway system of the country was wasteful, involving enormous expenditure without giving adequate return, he railways were run in the interests of the shareholders and not in the interest of the country. They ought to grapple with the subject in the interests of the indus- tries of the country.—Mr. Stibel said the proposal was a preposterous one, and as business men they should refuse to discuss it. (Applause.) —The resolution was rejected.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The Late Mr. Henry Lee's Will. The wills recently proved include that of the late Mr. Henry Lee, J.P., aged eighty-seven, of Bedford Lodge, Broughton Park, Salford, a life director of Messrs. Tootall, Broadhurst, Lee and Co., Ltd., cotton 'Spinners and manufacturers, and on the board of Dolskow, Vaughan, and Co., Ltd., and William Deacon's Bank, Ltd., the amount being sworn at £6o,97o.—Probate of Mr. Leo's will has been granted to his son, Mr. Harold Lee, of Fairfield, Broughton Park, and Mr. Arthur H. Lee, of Oatlands, West :Kirby, and to Mr. Ziba Armitage, of Preston Brook, !zrear Warrington, the executors. By his will the testator gave numerous shares in Tootall, Broadhurst and Co., to his children and grandchildren, £2,000 to his son Harold, and his daughters Florence Pyman and Ellen Armitage • 140 shares in William Deacon's and Co. to his daughter Rosalie Kelsall, and 1.2,000 in trust for his grandchildren Milicent and Erica Lee. His silver plate, given to him es treasurer of the congregational fund raised during the cotton famine, lie left to his daughter-in-law, Agnes Lee, and a picture by Horsley, called " Corning Down to Dinner," to the Corporation of Manchester. The residue of his property Mr. Lee leaves to ,his children and the issue of any deceased child. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, unsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Billiard Prize Distribution. The prizes won in the Christmas Billiard handicap in connection with the Warrington Catholic Young Men's Society were distributed at a smoking concert Which was held in the So-ciety's rooms, Winwick- street, the other evening. Mr. Owen Toole (president) occupied the chair, and was supported by the Rev. Father T. 0. Swarbreck, 0.5.8. (chairman), the Rev. ! Father L. Huggins, 0.5.8., and Messrs. E. J. Keatinge, I P. Delaney, D. Houghton, M. Cunningham, ! A. M. Crowe, H. Rigby, J. Hardiman, J. Boyle, &amp;c. The Rev. T. 0. Swarbreck, 0.5.8. (chaplain), asked the members to keep before them the objects of the society,—to3forward Catholic interests, to provide suitable recreation and social improvement and good ! ill. He dwelt at length on the great benefits accru- ing from the strength acquired by the unity of ' Catholic young men, and remarked that the great question of education, involving the vital interests of , their schools, was a question in which the Catholic ! -Young men's societies must play a conspicuous part. The President presented the prizes in the billiard ! handicap as follows ;—I, silver watch, Mr. Alban Richardson ; 2, travelling bag, Mr. M. Cunningham; I umbrella, Mr. J. Duskin ; 4, gold medal, Mr. B. IlacDonald. He had, he said, also been deputed to ! Present, on behalf of the Catholic Association—their ! Predecessor society now defunct—to Mr. A. M. Crowe, the. late secretary, and Mr. Roger Archer, the late manager, handsome umbrellas in token of valuable ' services rendered to the CathAic Association. Mr. ! Crowe, in replying, expressed his surprise and gratitude. What he had done for the old club was horn of the desire to promote the moral and social Welfare of the association. This kind recognition of of his services would always be a cherished recollec- tion to him, and he asked them to accept his heart- felt thanks. Mr. Roger Archer reiterated all Mr. Crowe had said and warmly expressed his gratitude to., the Catholic Association. An excellert rausicial Programme was contributed to by Messrs J. M. Bate, J. W. Bibby, B. Bate, P. Bate, P. Delaney; E. J. Keatinge, and the Rev. Fathers Swarbreck. and Bugging.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL Pretty SpectacTe-ai—The Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL Pretty SpectacTe-ai—The Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. R. A. NAYLOR.</p>
        <p>There have been several Band of Hope festivals in I Warrington, but that which was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening must be accounted by far the most successful which it has been the good fortune of the Warrington Band of Hope Union to promote. Seldom has a brighter and more picturesque spectacle been witnessed in the building, and the children and those TesPonsible for their training must be cordially con- gratulated upon a happy consummation to long and careful preparation. In the first place the audience was such as to gratify the promoters' rosiest expectations, , every seat being occupied, while many people had to be content to stand. If the attendance could be taken as indicative of greater interest in the temper- ance cause, then surely there is every reason for the , workers to feel encouraged. Of the entertainment it may truthfully be said that it gave the greatest delight to the assetablY, as was strikingly evidenced by the numerous , demands for repetitions of exceptionally creditable items. Very little time was devoted to preliminary , speech-making. Mr. R. A. Naylor (president of the Union) was in the chair, and he was accompanied by Miss Nayor. There were also present on the platform the Rev. H. B. Firth (vicar of St. James's), the Rev. 41 S. Hughes (Hill Cliffe Baptist Church). and Mrs. Simpson (president of the Ladies' Auxiliary). MR. R. A. NAYLOR AND INTOLERANCE. A WORD ABOUT THE " THELWALL NUISANCE." The CHAIRMAN said he could hardly express the pleasure it gave him to come and find that the War- rington Band of Hope Union was still alive and active and carrying forward to the best of its ability the good cause of teruporance. He regretted very much that he W 8 n not more with them in that work and lie had felt -very great dissatisfaction that owing to circumstance over which he had no control he had bad to leave the little village of Thelwall and the Band of Hope work in that place with which he had been associated so long. Whatever happened, they might depend upon , . -ig—that</p>
        <p>One thin,—that 'he would stick' true 'and remain firm to the great total abstinence Principles which lay at the foundation of all real temperance enterprise. Seeing there were a certain number of people in the country who if they drank most drink to excess, they recognized the desira- bility of establishing temperance in the minds of the rising generation. That, as far as he could see, was the only way to secure to ourselves a sober community in some future generation, acting upon the great principle ' laid down by the wise man—" Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it." Those who had enjoyed the opportunity of receiving temperance training and example in their ' Younger days saw the greatness of the truth in that " beautiful and very straightforward text from the Holy Scriptures. He had not come there to tell them to do something that he was not prepared to do himself (Hear, hear). The safest path was that walked by the total abstainers. The early total abstainers Sound that out and in a very rough manner one of them described the philosophy of the tem- perance movement in the never-to-be-forgotten words : "If you never take the first glass, the Dein himself cannot make you take the second." If they were perfectly true and honest to their convictions, and trod in that particular path of safety, setting at the same time a .safe example to the rising generation, they would Ire themselves from responsibility in the matter. (Hear, bear). Therefore he asked them to stand up for the band of hope movement and, as they did so, not to PT anything nasty about those people who did not happen to see eye to eye with them. If they had de- voted their attention to the furtherance of real temper- ance instead of being captivated by the glamour of the</p>
        <p>LL____ ‘ . . _ modern developments Of the movement, they would have occupied a far different position to-day. About 20 per cent of the adult population of this country were,be supposed, total abstainers and the mistake those , 20 per cent, made was that they dictated to the others what</p>
        <p>I they ought to do. As long as 80 per cent of the British people thought they required strong drink, they would have It in some form or another. Temperance people in the past had gain3d for themselves rather an evil reputation of being very cantankerous. Why should they have any enmity against anyone? Why should not they carry out their work to the best of their ability and not have any harsh words to fling at those who were engaged in a very difficult occupation. If they only stood true on the platform that was arranged by the early temperance men of Preston and sustained in this town for many yeas, and if they only spoke kindly of those who differed from them the move- ment would roll along until it became universally , victorious. Miss Naylor and he had been hoping to come and reside in this district again. (Applause.) They knew why they had been driven away, and unfortunately the cause that had driven them from their own home still kept them away 'at the present moment. They dare not even sleep in their own house for they might never waken up alive in this world. Friends who had come to see them in the past had been made unconscious by a terrible nuisance. The poisonous gas escaped and travelled into the place where they ought to live, and they were warned that if they stayed any longer they could not expect to live. Therefore they had gone away. and what had driven them away was in existence still. He was only telling the audience that, to show how a home could be desolated by that which could be avoided, and if they could only get the men and women to be abstainerr, and to rally round their' standard, how manyof those who now had miserable homes would find peace, happiness, cbmfort, and plenty—( Applause.) Practically the whole of the children appeared in character, and the effect produced by the variety of well-chosen colours was exceedingly pleasing. The queen (Lily Kendrick) was crowned with fitting cere- by the retiring queen (Elsie Jones), and afterwards the garland and sceptre were presented. An exceptionally meritorious programme was gone through, the chief features being the maypole and national dances, for which girls and boys had been trained by Miss Ethel Spinks, Mrs.Whitmore, Miss Sankey, and Mr. R. P. Wil- liams. The strong chorus was conducted by Mr. A. Spruce, Mr. F. Podmore and Master Clatworthy presi- ding at the organ. We regret that owing to extreme pressura on our space we are unable to give a fuller report. Mr. F. Gandy had discharged the secretarial duties, and had able assistants in Messrs. H. Woolley and R. E. Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</head>
        <p>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</p>
        <p>[By Godfrey Dean.] _ The Unending Pursuit.</p>
        <p>I patiently made my way among the West End shoppers, while considering where to spend the next hour or so before going to Cadogan-square. When, whom should I see, as I stepped from th 3 Arcade, but Phyllis herself—Phyllis, veiled (I never see a charming woman thus without thinking of masked batteries !), with lips parted in a smile whose meaning could not be missed, if her eyes had not flashed delight. After all, for the lover the world of women is divided : there is she—and the rest.</p>
        <p>"What a lot of people are about," Phyllis said. "There were a moment or two ago," I said. 'Aren't there now ? "</p>
        <p>" I had forgotton the fact," I replied, gravely. "Then," Phyllis suggested, "as I came out to do some shopping, and there is no excitement left, I will return." Tantalus! And I have another hour left. "Won't you finish your shopping?" I asked. Phyllis looked disappointcd. "It is done," she said.</p>
        <p>The brougham pulled up. I saw her in and pre- pared to make my adieux. But Phyllis looked straight ahead.</p>
        <p>Even in repose her face has a tender expression, as who should say, "I love." Full fifteen seconds passed, then she turned: "If you would rather come at the time," she began. I sat down beside her.</p>
        <p>"I see," Phyllis said, "one of the most popular literary men of the day—the one you spoke of as writing so—so—introspectively, was it, you said ? " "That was how I described it." —"So introspectively of women—has married." " Yes." "I am afraid," tentatively, "he won't write half so nicely in future." I could not endure that a suspicion of anything of the kind should linger in Phyllis's mind. Marriage, of course, is the perfect state. "All great painters have had beautiful models or sitters to inspire their art," I suggested.</p>
        <p>Phyllis did not speak. "Then why not literary men?" I asked. "Rather," she said, "what claims have they.? .. One should show reason "—her smile was very demure— "for making a change, not challenge opinion as to why it should not be made." I thought this observation was not original ; but so long as Phyllis saw the truth of the argument, what matter?</p>
        <p>"Many," I replied. "The claims of the literary man in this respect are just as strong as, if not stronger than, those of the painter. He has to produce many ; sides of a woman's character, while the artist portrays her physical beauty or the external—if subtle—signs of her personality—if she have one." Only now did I realise that I had been beguiled into this explanation of my point of view without an idea of Phyllis's reason for the fear she expressed. If Phyllis must make such bold suggestions then she ought to jwstify them. "You haven't told me," I reminded her, "why you think his work will deteriorate." " Haven't I P"—with the expression of one asked a difficult question. " Well, but here we are now, and tell you when you are in." How women like to surround themselves with pretty trifles! While I waited, glancing casually about the room, I caught sight of a number of small articles which I immediately associated with Phyllis. Once, a trinket say, has left the cold atmosphere of the jeweller's shop and comes into the possession of some daughter of Eve around it seems to cling the character of its owner, and never again has it its former "impersonality." My glance wandered to a portrait of Phyllis—some- thing I had not before seen. It was not a likeness, in the usual acceptance of the word, but was finer than anything of the kind. The artist having percep- tions for the subtleties of a personality, and a brush just as sensitive to his mind, had suggested them on ' the canvas. The man had done what I did not think possible. I held my breath from fascination. The picture was signed, but I carefully avoided reading the signature. I had rather remain ignorant of the name of one who had so appreciated Phyllis— ! a human weakness mine. _</p>
        <p>A slight sound disturbed my revervie. Turning round I saw that Phyllis had returned. She did not appear though, to have noticed what I was I:eking at. •</p>
        <p>" Now, Phyllis,l wish you to tell me why you think that the writer's work may deteriorate with his marriage." She mused_; while I watched her face change ex- pression with her thoughts. Eventually; after a slight shake of the head (quite unconsciously, I.s if in avoidance of an insistent suggestion), she glanced quickly at me, as one who would reassure herself against doubts. I believe she did not think I was looking at her,and as our eyes met I read the inquiry ill hers. " What is it, Phyllis ?" I asked. Though the eloquence of her look told me, I desired to hear it from her.</p>
        <p>" I would rather not tell you." she said, in a low, hesitating voice. "I think you are in honour bound after such a doubting "—I was going to say "look," which would have been a mistake, but I recovered myself, as Phyllis glanced surprisedly at me, and added, " attitude of a short time ago." " Well," she said, " if you must know, when one is sure of anything the interest it may have held is often lost."</p>
        <p>" Do you believe that ?" I asked. " It is possible," she said. And if one could be sure of a woman. Of this, at least, one may be sure, that when you understand one perfectly you will understand all women. But the former will never come to pass ; it is not ordained— because, perhaps, of Phyllis's reason. " That one should be sure of a woman is impos- sible," I said ; " and marriage will only convince a man of this. For the pursuit of the nature of a woman does not end with marriage—it is unending; it is only a further step towards an always receding goal." Phyllis smiled at My tone. " But what about the author ?" she asked. " His work ought to have a finer quality still." I said, " because he now has a delightful muse." " And are you sure the result will be better ?" " Of course," I said ; " I say it without prejudice, but—l had rather be a prejudiced person." (To be continued.) (These articles originally appeared in the " London Echo.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
