      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 4, 1905</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 4, 1905</p>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF OLD CHINA.</p>
        <p>[By Edmund Osborne.]</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Society may look with gratification on the results of their work. Whilst all the objects for which the Society was founded have not as yet been attained, yet in fair degree much has been attempted and something accomplished. The Society has gathered together some, if not all of the Warrington worthies; and the goad old town from which everything worth preserving really sprung is :110w known throughout the length and breadth of this little island. To study and discuss learned works and accomplishments takes up much of the time of the favoured individuals. Those who are not " keen "on placing plates on buildings and lanterning a halo round the bald head of some unhappy individual who once may have had the temerity to put his foot on this hallowed spot have the satisfaction of knowing that a billiard table now occupies one of the rooms, and that smokeroom chatter makes the old fogey's faces illumine the walls of the tapestried room with imitation oak panellings. Some of the stories make the poor old parrot bend his head in sorrow when he thinks of other days devoted to objects altogether dissimilar though perhaps a little more learned. MR. H. STUART PAGE.</p>
        <p>Last evening, our talented townsman Mr. H. Stuart Page gave much pleasure and instruction to many of his admirers. Some of us have looked upon that gentleman as a kind of ' china maniac' and oft' times when we have been privileged to meet him in his home at Padgate have sighed and thought what a pity it was to find such a really good fellow hastening to his mental destruction. Our views from last night have taken a. decided turn. His paper on " Old China " was one of the most interesting and instructive that the writer has ever listened to, and gave much satisfaction and pleasure to every one present. The " Romance " probably appealed to me most 'and I think that the following remarks taken from his lecture will be of interest to the"uninitiateras well as to the lovers of the "antique." To deal with the whole of it would take 'up too much space, so I am reluctantly compelled to pick out some of the thoughts he conveyed so readily to us, with the hope that at a future date I may be enabled to continue the story. Mr. Page told us that the story of pottery is the story of mankind. From the time when man first learned to Take crude vessels of sun-dried clay we can read in his pottery the story of his progress through advancing stages of intellect until he arrived at a high state of civilization. The production of vessels of earthenware as well as being probably the oldest craft in the world has also been the most constantly in operation and the most intimately connected with the every-day life and habits of the individual. It therefore reflects the changing customs of passing times, and the various phases of the artistic impulses of mankind as nothing else can. Nothing is more susceptible to this artistic impulse than the plastic clay; yet the finished produet is practically imperishable, and has preserved through countless generations an infallible record of the march of civilization. The story of the potter's wheel, that 1 spinning table on which the moist clay is deftly ! moulded by the sensitive fingers and delicate touch into homely cup and simple platter, or more ambitious , vessel, with all its sorrows and joys, its signs and symbols, its struggles and achievements—with its many sacrifices—for the production of something which in its conceptions and finish would satisfy the , most aesthetic and critical observer of the ceramic art, ! is a silent witness of the past linked with the present. ' The process of pottery " throwing,"as it is called, has given to writers of. all ages an idea to illuminate their questionings into the mystery of things, whereby an uncontrollable fate shapes the destinies of us unwill- ing creatures of circumstances, who are but " as clay in the hands of the potter." Omar Khayyam was perhaps more than any other writer conscious of his relationship to the cup from which he quaffed his wine. It, too, might even once have lived, and both were to descend again to the common earth from which they both were taken. You will remember in that quatrain how he tells so quaintly and with such a wondrous knowledge of human nature, how— " In the Market-place one dusk of day, I watch'd the potter thumping his wet clay; : And with its all obliterated tongue It murmur'd, " Gently, Brother, gently pray!'. In Robert Browning, too, we find a cognate truth to that expressed teaching, contrasting the artistic soul 1 with the soul, of those things which a connoisseur or 1 collector of art treasures loves to think that " they too, might once have lived." Some have got it into their heads that the makers of our "cups, jugs, plates and such like things " were entirely mercenary and made their goods with as little tender regard for the ideal in art as a shareholder has in a blast furnance, in forgetting that competition had not then wriggled its way into our commercial life and that we were ! satisfied to work in love, putting our soul into it, I never wearying or tiring of the desire to make some- thing which would satisfy our longing for the I ideal. "If I can produce only one beautiful ' work of art I shall be able to rob malice of its venom, and cowardice of its sneer, and to pluck out the tongue of scorn by the roots." " Every single work of art is the fulfilment of a prophecy : for every work of art is the conversion of an idea into an image." And it was the con- version of that 'idea into an image' that all through the ages that have gone before we have had men and women who have been piepared to sacrifice the body for the advent of the soul. . . .</p>
        <p>The modern collector is chiefly interested iii thee pottery and porcelain of the 18th century, examples of which: Mr. Page placed before his friends. In the first place he impressed upon those of us, who by no stretch of the imagination can be called 'China maniacs the difference between pottery or earthen- ware, and porcelain or china, because as he pointed out they are essentially different. Pottery is simply baked earth—earthen Ware ; porcelain is an artificial mixture of certain mineral elements which melt and fuse together under great heat, forming a body that is hard, dense, and semi-transparent. In a sense all porcelain is pottery, but pottery is never porcelain. Porcelain looks harder and feels colder than pottery. And he went on to explain how the name was given to it and told us that porcelain was not a European invention. All the efforts that were made by the potters of Europe from the lfith to the 18th century to discover the secret of making pottery white and translucent, were simply efforts to imitate a ware that had been made in China for hundreds of years. Although a few specimens came to Europe by the old trade routes of the middle ages, it was bound to remain almost unknown to Western nations, while the mode of transit was confined to the caravans that slowly made their way across Asia with Eastern goods for the European markets. The fragile pottery of China could not be expected to make that long and perilous journey on the backs of clumsy camels in any quantity. It was not until the establishment of a regular trade with the East by the Cape route that the ware was imported in any quantity and came to be generally known. At that time it was such a unique and precious substance, so different from anything known in Europe, that myths and legends, quite appropriate to its Eastern origin, were woven about it. Some of these may have had their foundation in the reverence with which the Chinese themselves regarded it—to them it was never common or mean, but was bound up with their literature, their history, and their com- plicated mythology, in quite a marvellous fashion. Curious monsters and contorted dragons were but signs and symbols. The sacred horse, the immortal bird, the five clawed dragon and many other quaint devices which are passed over by us as Chinese oddities have mime deep and distinct meaning. krid vases were given by the Emperor as rewards for good and noble deeds. _ _</p>
        <p>At the beginning of the 18th Century, when the custom of tea drinking was becoming common, and the East India Company had a large and regular trade with China the ware began to be imported in market- able quantities. And one can imagine the feelings of our ancestors as the objects of art and use, wrapped in all the &amp;amours of the unknown and mysterious East,carrying with them son.e of the fascination of the land of their production, were unloaded from the East India ships. The fairy tales that were woven about them would come to be regarded with almost reverence and awe. Our own crude attempts at production resolved themselves into slip ware which was coarse and heavy in form, and uncouth in ' representation of some apology for decoration. Leather bottles and jugs were not then things of the past, and one can readily realize that the demand for such articles would create a desire in the minds of our own countrymen to discover the secret of its manufacture, which they eventually did.</p>
        <p>14.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RISE IN SUGAR.</head>
        <p>THE RISE IN SUGAR.</p>
        <p>MR. LLOYD GEORGE AND THE " EGREGIOUS ' FOLLY OF THE CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>A Loss of Nine Million Pounds.</p>
        <p>Speaking in the House of Commons on Tuesday night Mr. Lloyd-George continued the debate on Mr. Kearley's amendment to the Address. This proposed to add the following words: But we humbly represent to your Majesty that your Majesty's Government, in committing the country to the policy of the Brussells Sugar Convention, have inflicted heavy losses upon trade diminished employment of labour, enormously increased the cost of a necessary food to consumers without any compensitory advantage and we humbly submit to yourplajesty that these evil results call for an immediate remedy; and that the con- vention should be denounced at the earliest possible moment.</p>
        <p>Referring to Mr. Chamberlain's speech at the pro- ceeding sitting, Mr. Lloyd-George said that the right hon. gentleman would not have statistics, especially official statistics, and with regard to facts, he had de- veloped a knack recently of omitting from them every element which was relevant. He had even omitted the relevant parts of his political quotations. The previous night the right hon. gentleman had quoted from, he thought, " Rejected Addresses "—(laughter)—evidently he regarded that as a congenial study. He had asked : Who fills the butchers' shops with large blue flies ? (Laughter.) But the most important part of that quotation was the first line. aud the right hon. gentle- man had omitted it. It was :</p>
        <p>Who makes the quartern loaf and Luddites rise? (Opposition cheers and laughter.) As Mr. Chamber- lain treated his poetry so he treated his facts, and mixed them up. He had said that the price of sugar was no higher than in this country. But he had omitted the very important fact that since the Sugar Convention was adopted the price of sugar had gone down by one half on the Continent and had almost doubled here. (Hear, hear.) If that was Mr. Chamberlain's idea of presenting the case to a working-class audience one could quite understand why most of those working-class audiences arrived at the hall in motor-cars. (Loud opposition cheers and laughter.) The right hon. gentle- man had said he believed in the common-sense of the country. No doubt, and that was why they were post- poning the appeal. When the Sugar Convention Act was passed the Prime Minister had said that the price of sugar would decrease, but he had evidently</p>
        <p>GOT UP HIS BRIEF BADLY. The Government and their supporters had said that another result would be to increase employment. The Opposition, on the contrary, said it would increase the price of sugar, and that it would decrease employment. As a matter of fact, after two years' experience, employ- ment had decreased in the sugar industries by something like 15,000, and about 50,000 were for some weeks on half time. As to price, sugar, so far from going down, had gone up by something like 130 per cent. Thesl events had justified up to the hilt the two pre- dictions made on the Liberal side of the House. Another circumstance Mr. Chamberlain completely overlooked was that the Convention had cheapened sugar on the Continent, and so increased its consump- tion by 750,000 tons. In France it had doubled the consumption. The price went down by nearly one half when the Convention came into operation. If they said that shortage of supplies affected the price, then the shortage of supply was very largely due to the increased consumption on the Continent. Last month we imported the same quantity of sugar as in 1903 and 1901, but the difference in price was £BOO,OOO. (Hear, I hear.) That meant, if the same price were kept up for the year an actual LOSS OF SOMETHING LIKE NINE MILLIONS. (Opposition cheers). Mr. Chamberlain had told them that if there was a shortage of a small quantity it meant an increase of price quite out of proportion to that shortage. The same thing applied to the small surplusage. That was perfectly true, and there never was a greater Free Trade argument. But why so clever a controversialist should have introduced it in favour of the Sugar Convention completely baffled him. The Convention bad doubled the price of raw material here and had halved it for our competitors. We were four times worse off in competing with foreign countries than we were before the Convention. That was a great illustration of the policy of preventing dumping in order to increase employment for this country. (Opposition cheers.) Mr. Chamberlain had reminded the House that in 1889 the confectioners themselves had asked for the abolition of the bounties. (Ministerial cheers.) Yes ; but the right hon. gentleman was the last person Li the world to taunt anyone with opinions held fifteen years ago. (Opposition cheers.) It was the experience the confectioners had had since that time that had caused them to realise that the bounties were a benefit to their interest. A firm of toffee makers had stated that they employed one-fourth feeror hands now than previously, and that last year their turnover dropped £30,000. They were now maintaining their export trade by selling without a profit—that is, they were dumping." The Government had handed over the in- dustries of this country to a Commission representing our great rivals. They had simply armed foreign countries with the means of destroying some of our best industries. Never was there such folly. Nefarious was not the word for it. It was egregious. (Opposition cheers.) We had entered into a conspiracy with our trade rivals to rob our own warehouses. Mr. Chamberlain had said that the Convention had stopped fluctuation of pricer. Yes ; the fluctuation was all in one direction now. It was a constant rise. That was the result of a year or two's experience in "fiscal reform." (Opposi- tion cheers.) "MIRACLES OP FALSE PROPHETS." Mr. Chamberlain had asked, What on earth has this to do with the fiscal controversy?" It had everything in the world to do with it. (Renewed Opposition cheers.) It was a warning to the people of this country not to attempt to interfere with trade by artificial means of this kind.</p>
        <p>An Hon. Afar;Ver. (opposite) : You are Feee Traders and you support bounties. Mr. Lloyd-George: Not at all. That is a funda- mental error of hon. members. We are simply taking advantage of them. (Cheers and counter-cheers.) If a foreign country likes to give us a present of six millions a year to assist our industries why should we be so fatuous as to refuse it? (Opposition cheers.) Continu- ing, Arr. George observed that when we accepted from foreign countries, as the result of their own rtupidity, the benefl t and advantage derived from the follies of their fiscal system, that was not a departure from the policy of Free Trade. It was the essence of it. We bought in tho cheapest markt for the benefit of our own people. We were constantly hearing bright prophecies as to what would happen if we tried some quack remedies of this kind or that. This had shown how dangerous it was to listen to those prophecies. The Sugar Convention Act had exposed the miracles pf the false prophets. (Opposition cheers.) Every pre- diction made by the Government had failed. They had tried to stop" dumping," but they had only succeeded in hampering our own industries and helping to build up ' those of our rivals. More labour and more employment had been promised. What were the facts ? An increase of a few hundred in the sugar refining industries and a decrease of 15,000 in others. What consolation it was ! for the fifteen thousand thrown out of work to be told that there were a few hundreds added to the sugar refinin,,v employees at Greenock and elsewhere ? it was said that our industries were submerged by the waves of foreign competition. Mr. Bonar Law had last year pictured Sir Henry Campsell-Bannerruan as clinging to the rock of Free Trade principles, shivering as the waves subdued, as foreign Competition went down. But there was no receding wave of foreign competition. It had on the contrary, increased, and the hon. member had helped to blast the rock. (Loud Opposition cheers.) The debate was continued by Mr. Bonarlaw, Mr. Gerald nalfonr, and others, the amendment being finally rejected by 211 votes against 276,a Government majority of 65.</p>
        <p>025,000 THROWN ON THE STREETS. Mr. M'Kenne said it could not be seriously contended that the Convention had not had an effect on the price of sugar- Would the Government, if their hands were free, be prepared to agair pass a Convention Bill? Mr. Gerald Balfour—Quite ready. (Ministerial cheers). Mr. M'Kenna replied that in that case the Govern- ment were hopeless. (Opposition cheers.) We were paying 10 millions a year more for our sugar than we were paying prior to the passing of the Con .ention Ten millions a year, as the member for West Biraiin- ham had pointed out, meant the employment of 120,000 men at 30s. a week, and that, in turn, meant subsistence for G 25,000 people. That was to say, in the right hon. gentleman's words, we had now 625,000 people thrown upon the streets because the price of sugar had been raised by ten millions in the course of a year. (Op- position cheers.)</p>
      </div>
