        <p>remove one of them. So they might conclude that the MR. BALFOUR STRONGLY CRITICISED blocking was done with his approval. ("Shame.") • Another way Mr. Balfour stopped discuisson was to stay away from the House and then he could not be THE FOLLY OF A FISCAL ALTERATION. made to answer. He took the pay—that was pleasant enough—but he need not stay and do the work: his principle was not to do the work. The The welcome activity of the Liberals in Stockton DRUDGERIES OF STATESMANSHIP Heath was shown on Wednesday, evening, when a were beneath him. When Mr. Gladstone was leader of meeting was held in the National Schools in support of the House he was always at his post—(hear, hear) —but Mr. Balfour was scarcely ever there. He sauntered into the candidature in the Knutsford Parliamentary Divi- the ',House for a casual half-hour an i then snowed sion of Mr. King.— Mr. Henry Roberts presided over a his ignorance of what was going on. The large attendanee, being supported by Dr. Massie motto of Mr. Balfour's majority seemed to be (Treasurer of the National Liberal Federation), Mr. "Whistle, and T will come to you, my lad," so they A. H. Crosfield, Ur. A.J. King, Mr. C. Jackson-Holmes, came from the lobbies without having heard the debate Mr. J. Mounfield, Mr. E. Lloyd Jones, Mr. J. Long- and voted exactly as he told them' and rescued him land, and the Rev. J. S. Hughes. from all his scrapes. Mr. Balfour had destroyed the collective responsibility of the Cabinet, which was now MR. ROBERTS' REASON FOR RETURNING TO a "go as you please Cabinet." Any minister could POLITICS. practically say anything that he liked, because Mr. Mr. Roberts said he did not think he need make any Balfour was always explanation as to his attendance there in so honourable ' CAPABLE OP DISOWNING HIM a position as chairman of the meeting. He must say afterwards. He (Dr. Massie) could not see why Mr. however that had it not been for one particular ques- , Chamberlain should ever have resigned. Why Lien he was not sure that he would have taken a very , shouldn't he have sat on the Cabinet and had his own active part in the coining general election. As a personal opinion and gone fighting about the country business man whose living and a little more than his for that opinion? Why he should resign was a bit of a living depended absolutelY upon the trade of the mystery, bst a good many people thought he foresaw country he felt so strongly that the policy promulgated the tfouble coming in South Africa, and wanted to clear by Mr. Chamberlain, which had not been repudiated by out of the way in good time. (Hear, hear.) Perhaps certain responsible leaders of 'the Conservative party, Ihe also wanted to execute what military men called a and which involved a change in the trade conditions of ' strategic movement to the rear—(laughter)—and then the country, was of such a character that to his from the position of power without responsibility he mind, if adopted, it would mean commercial suicide. would be able to give the Government a push forward In his own interests, and he admitted at once he was 'in the direction he desired. Was there ever a greater , absolutely selfish in the matter, in the interest of every- I shame in this world and a greater scandal than that the I one connected with the trade and commerce of the chief Protectionist should go out of the Government , country, in the interest of the working classes upon and that his whom the extra cost of living would fall in undue pro- PROTECTIONIST SON portion he had made up his mind that he would do his should be put to superintend the financial affairs of the level best to prevent a possibility of retrogression in nation? (Applause.) Just as he was in reference to the regard to the free trade policy of this country. war so he was in reference to this matter,—Ar. Balfour (Applause). He believed they could not have had a did not feel he had any more responsibility than any more splendid illustration of the man in the street. He did not seem to care how he FOLLY OF INTERFERENCE dislocated trade in the country; and trade was dis- than was afforded by the present condition of the sugar located to a certain extent becanse businessmen did not industry. (Hear, hear.) The more that question was know what was coming or where they were. Meanwhile, ventilated in every possible detail and the more satisfied in a Government like this under the command of a man the people of the country would be that to extend the like Mr. Balfour, the old watchwords of self-govern- samepolicy to flour,meat and other commodities necessary merit, equality, freedom, and progress were go- for life would bring about the same condition of things jug out of fashion. NV° were governed by in the lives of the working people of the country as a cabinet—that meant we were governed by an existed before the repeal of the corn laws and he was oligarchy—but worse than that we were governed by one goingto make it his policy so far as he couidto prevent man who governed the cabinet. What a farce it was to a re-institution of that terrible state of things. (Ap- assemble Parliament once more simply to register his plause.) The object which the promoters of the sugar decree ! The only other possible reason for sununoning convention had, or pretended to have, at heart was the Parliament was that Mr. Balfour might add to his *epu- benefiting of sugar planters in the West Indies and tation for shuffling and create once more a reputation other British territory, and to carry out that purpose for never being able to give a straight answer. What the country was at the present time paying, was the mental and moral root of all this mischievous taking the lowest estimate, something like £7,000,000 a behaviour? It was not merely that Mr. Balfour had an year more than it was two or three years ago for the aristocratic contempt for all popular institutiors. same quantity 'of sugar. It would havelbeenanextremely Like his friend Sir William Anson he undoubtedly be- good bargain to have got out of the difficulty, if it was lieved that direct election was bad for everything and that a difficulty of assisting the impecunious sugar growers indirect election was good for everything. But it was of the West Indies—many of whom were impecunious direct election that brought him his majority,. it for precisely the same reasons as were many of the supported him in the House, and so Mr. Balfour's view manufacturers of this country who because they were of a great national institution was regulated by what behind the times in regard to the management it would bring to him and to the party which he led of their own concerns were clamouring for Like Pistol in Shakespeare, who regarded the world an alteration in our fiscal system—by giving as his oyster, so Mr. Balfour regarded the House of them . £250,000 or £1,000,000 a year outright, Commons as his oyster. (Laughter.) Mr. Balfour had rather than pay £7,000,000 a year for the privilege of a great contempt for distinctions between things. He giving them the partial support whichthey got from the would not regard anything as positively certain; he did enhanced price of sugar, (Applause.) lie had many not believe that anything mattered very much. good friends in the Conservative party and was not (Laughter.) He regarded in his heart that one opinion going to make a personal quarrel of the question, but was nearly as good as another. With him, nothing was was going to fight on principle. One of his friendshad altogether itself, but it might at the same time be said that all the Radicals wanted was office and that something else. (Laughter.) Words with Mr. Balfour which followed—pay. He did not see himself that it were simple was any greater crime for Radicals to seek office and pay than it was for Conservatives to hold office and pay, especially when they were holding it contrary to the will and desire of the nation. (Applause.) It seemed to him that at the present moment the Conservative Government including the Primo Minister himself, stood in a more discredited position than it had ever done before. Record after record had been given as to what was the feeling of the country upon certain matters and yet in spite of' all, the Government said they would not resign until they were turned out. The country was desirious of expressing its opinion upon the fiscal question and a Conservative Government denied them the opportunity until the whole thing had become a scandal. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THINGS TO PLAN' WITH. When he sent an armed mission into Thibet, he called it a peaceful mission. When he passed an Education Act in the interests of the Church of Engl*nd ha called it a boon to Nonconformists. (Laughter.) When he passed a Licensing Act which gave a great bribe to the trade, he called it a tribute to eternal justice. (Laughter.) When he sanctioned a regulation for virtual slavery in the Transvaal he called it indentured labour ; and when be ruined the sugar trade he called it broadening the area of sugar cultivation. (Laughter.) When he advocated suicidal retaliation ho called it freedom of negotiation; when he did not want to say Protection he called it freer trade; and when he did not ! want to talk of a tariff arrangement as a tax upon the people he called it a toll upon the foreigner. (Laughter.) ' He (Dr. Massie) wondered what they would have next. One felt inclined to suppose that Mr. Balfour might call an invasion by a burglar of a private house a polite call after dinner. (Loud laughter.) When a man used words simply as counters to which he might give what value he pleased, he was a dangerous man, and when he , was the leader of a party in the House of Commons he was a man perilous to the country. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Balfour's tactics went far to justify those people who spoke of politics as simply a trade. He (the speaker) got sick of hearing of the subtle and indefin- able charm of Mr. Balfour's manner: he got tired of hearing what a good fellow he was at the bottom. Oae knew many good fellows at the bottom who were per- fectly worthless at the top. (Laughter.) They were told that</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR CRITICISED. Dr. Massie, who wasreceived with applause, confined his remarks chiefly to a criticism of the conduct of Parliamentary affairs by Mr. Balfour. The thought, he said, had weighed heavily upon him for some time past, and he hoped it would give his hearers cause for serious reflection, as to the manner in which Mr. Balfour had degraded the House of Commons, as a deliberative assembly. By his expressions during the Boer war Mr. Balfour had earned the stern rebuke of the " Times " for what they called his jaunty and most frivolous tone. Those accusations coming from an indulgent friend could be regarded as the truth and to them could be added the trading upon the patriotism of the country and the subordination of national interests to party claims. (Hear, hear). Mr. Balfour had been willing to admit that upon certain , impartant matters he knew nothing more than the man in the street and he had so used his majority in the 1 House of Commons that they were practically mere men in the street, and thereby degraded the great deliberative assembly of the nation. Mr. Balfour had had an unprecedentedly large majority with which he had mistuanaged a great War, and squandered the savings of the nation, increased the national debt by £160,000,000, and increased the rational expenditure from £90,000,000, to £1.10,000,000. As an illustration of the waste of public money Dr. Massey quoted the proposal to estab- lish a naval base on the coast of Scotland. For that purpose, he said,Mr.Balfour paid an extravagant sum for souse land at Boss Side which, after the purchase was completed, was found to be useless, there being 100 feet of mud. (Applause.) By his unjust Education Act Mr. Balfour had aroused the UNQUEIsICH ABLE B OSTILIT Y of half the religions population of the country, had awakened the solid resistance of the principality of Wales, and had even called forth remonstrances from religionists in America. Mr. Balfour had said he could not see that Nonconformists had a grievance, but he might have learnt about that grievance had he not been too contemptuous to learn. When the deputation visited the House of Commons to lay the grievance before him somehow or other, speaking purely figuratively, his turned up nose got in the way of his eyes, and he was I unable to see anything before Slim. (Applause.) He had I occasioned the disgust of even his Church of England friends by his huge bribe to the " trade" by changing what was a temporary privilege into a per- manent vested interest, to be bought back at enormous cost by the nation with funds so low and at prices so high that it would make any sensible reduc- tion in the number of licences impossible and which put a great obstacle in the way of temperance reform. He had degraded what should be the great deliberative assembly of the nation by turning his majority into a mere machine for registering the decrees of the Government, and for retaining office agains the will of the country as shown in byet election after bye-election. (Applause.) In his so called reformi in the procedure of the House of Commons he had, contrary to all rules, flung upon the House new rules in their roughest state which by experi- ence had proved fatal to the proper conduct of the House. This mismanagement of a Government was never so thoroughly exposed as by questions and by the new rules which he had imposed upon the House ques- tions had been put into a far corner. The best legislation I recorded in the past had been introduced by private !members but at the present time the vocation of the private member was lost as he could not hope to have a measure carried through. He had ruthlessly applied the I closure and used that weapon in a manner in which it was never intended it should be used thereby prevent- ing proper discussion and amendment. When the fiscal question was being discussed on every platform, the House of Commons was the only delibera- tive assembly in the country where discussion was pre- vented. The governnaent, that was Mr. Balfour and Mr Chamberlain, agreed that it would be the great cry at ! the next election, and therefore it was only right that ; the House of Commons and the nation should know I where the Government was and what its view on the subject was. But there was A DANGER IN DEFINITENESS and Mr. Balfour resolved not to be definite. He wanted to keep in with the Free Food Unionists by pro- fessing that he was not a Protectionist and be wanted to keep in with Mr. Chamberlain's Protectionist followers by hinting that his convictions were unsettled on the subject. As a matter ! of fact he was a bit of a Protectionist—there was no doubt about that,—but he wanted it to keep till a con- venient season. Mr. Chamberlain said there was no difference in principle between Mr. Balfour and himself —which he (Dr. Massie) heartily believed—and Mr. I Balfour had taken no opportunity of denying it. The reason why the House of Commons was the best place for the question to be discussed in wds that when a man made a speech there his statements could be at once sifted without any expense—a lie could be at once nailed to the counter. But the Government did not want that. Thek wanted the discussion to go on outside the House of Commons on platforms in , the country where ex Vane and undenied statements could be made and get a start. Meanwhile the founda- tions of Free Trade were being sapped, and Mr. Cham- berlain was holding the fort till Mr. Balfour could give it over to the enemy. There had been four maimed and mutilated discussions in the House of Commons forced on Mr. Balfour, and on not one of those occa- sions had Mr. Balfour uttered a single intelligible opinion on the question. (Laughter.) Was not this trifling with the dignity, the reputation, and the func- tion of the House ? (Hear, hear.) Then again, a bogus blocking motion could be put up by a Conservative member when he had no intention of actually bringing</p>
        <p>MR. BALFoUR WAS SO URBANE, but a boa constrictor could be urbane. (Laughter.) They heard of his entrancing smile, but he ought to have a label: "This animal, when it smiles, is dangerous." (Laughter). His smile, good fellowship and urbanity must be reduced to their proper dimensions. They were all some- what the worse for wear. (Laughter.) The conduct of Parliamentary business which he (Dr. Massie) had described, served to cover up the shirking of hard work which the country seriously required; it covered up an ignorant and yet cynical subordination of national interests to party claims. In fact, it had turned Mr. Bah four from the responsible minister of a free country into the dodging boss of a party. (Applause.) It rested with the electors to dethrone him and put someone in his stead who would guide the country to prosperity and not to ruin, and so to bring to their full fruition the destinies of this great nation. (Loud Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>MR. KING AND THE _CAUSE OF UNEMPLOYED</p>
        <p>QUESTION. Mr. A. J. King moved the following resolution : "That, in view of the overwhelming evidences that his Majesty's Ministers no longer command the confidence of the country, and that the House of Commons has ceased to reflect the opinion of the electorate, this meeting calls upon the Prime Minister to dissolve Parliament and permit the country to pronounce on the Protectionist policy and the general record of the pre- sent Government. And this meeting also declares that it is essential to the welfare of the country that the next Parliament should resolutely apply itself to the retrenchment of the national expenditure, to a settle- ment of the education and licensing questions, and to a bold policy of social and electoral reform." He said the value of Dr. Massie's address had been to give them a peep behind the scenes and to show them by what</p>
        <p>_ _ ALMOST REVOLUTIONARY MEANS' the Government had been able to carry some of their extraordinary legislation. It was amazing that any House of representative men of this country could pass such legislation as we had had in recent years. The question of national expenditure was one of the most important which the country had to consider. He was not one of those who objected to expenditure for its own sake, lie did not think, for instance, that the nation could very well spend too much for education, provided that the money was well spent and not wasted in administration. He did, however, object to unneces- sary expenditure on those great destructive agencies, the army and navy, if we did not get value for money. He was not much in favour of a military or an imperial policy, but he thought that the veriest jingo must see that if we were to have this enormous expenditure on the army and navy we should, at any rate, get value for money. (Hear, hear.) The expenditure on the army ' and navy had doubled during the last ten years. The Government were not only extravagant, but they were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>W.I.STEFITL AND _ _</head>
        <p>W.I.STEFITL AND _ _</p>
        <p>During the Soti-th-A-tclea;.;nr—t-1;;;Ztae; of lives lost through disease was something like 13,000 whereas the number of soldiers killed in battle or dying from wounds was only about 8,000. In the Russo-Japanese campaign, owing to the magnificent efficiency of the medical service, the number of deaths from disease in the Japanese ranks was only about 5 per cent, of the total losses. It spoke badly for the efficiency of administration of our army that we should lose more men by disease than by wounds. We were spending something like £50,000,000 per annum more than we were ten years ago. We heard a great deal now-a-days about the unemployed. How was it that at a time when our oversea trade was larger than ever it had been and more flourishing and prosperous, that we had so many unemployed ? He attributed it to our home trade. Owing to the increased expenditure the people had not as much money to spend and trade suffered. Alluding to the CHINESE LABOUR question Mr. King said there was not the slighest doubt that white men would do the work if they were properly paid and were allowed to work under proper conditions. The mines.he contended, might provide work for 26,000 Englishmen instead of yellow men. The real reason why whitemen were not wanted on the rand was that they would want to form trade unions and control Bile r wages, and when local self-government was in operation they would want to control the policy of the country by their votes. Mr. King alluded to the disclosures in regard to the Government's Irish policy and described the negotiations between Mr. Wyndham and Sir Anthony Mc Donnell. He contended that the Government's reputation of Sir Anthony's policy was contemptible. Mr. Balfour had seen the opportunity when the election came of diverting public opinion from the fiscal question, of which he was heartily sick, and raising the old bogey of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HOME RULE.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>If the Unionists accused Liberals of favouring Home Itule—and he, for his own part, bad always</p>
        <p>been of opinion that the Irish question wont never be settled satisfactorily on any other basis except that of popular government—they could reply that it was a case of Satan rebuking sin. (Laughter). Mr. E.Lloyd Jones in seconding said it was their duty to remove the stigma which rested upon that constituency. He illustrated how an alteration in out fiscal system while it would benefit certain industries would be against the interests of the country generally. The present flourishing condition of the steel trade was an argument entirely in favour of free trade. The present Government was dead to good works and it was time it made way either for a more democratic Conservative Government or else for a Liberal administration (Applause.) The resolution was put to the meeting and carried with three dissentients. SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, in moving a vote of thanks to the speakers, said he would like in the first place, to echo the remarks made by Mr. Lloyd Jones and to express the pleasure it gave him to see Mr. Roberts pre- siding. He hoped that what Mr. Lloyd Jones had said would influence the chairman and induce him to take an active part in assisting Mr. King in this part of the I Knutsford division (Mr. E. England: And you in Warrington). He might also add, as his friend had suggested, that it was a source of satisfaction to know that it was Mr. Roberts's intention, as he had stated that night, to take an active part not only in the Knutsford division but in another neigh- bouring constituency in which he (Mr. Crosfleid) WaS very much concerned, and he hoped that Mr. Roberts would not only take an oar in their boat but pull a very strong oar, too. (Hear, hear.) It was a great pleasure to him to move a vote of thanks to the speakers who had covered so much ground and who had certainly given them a catalogue of the</p>
        <p>MISDEMEANOURS AND SHORTCOMING S of this Government, with which he believed they would all be in hearty accord. It certainly was a most extra- ordinary record, and he should not think that in all the political history of the country, the record could be matched. There .gas a saying which Mr. McMinnies used to be very fond of quoting : "Give a Tory Government enough rope and it is bound to hang itself at the end." (Laughter.) Certainly the record of this Government all through, if they well consider 2. itlshowed that;they had deliberately acted in opposition to the old maxim which guided Liberals at any rate,— they had so deliberately neglected to study the interests of the greatest number of the nation, and had so played into the hands of the lucky view of the vested interests at the expense of the general taxpayer, that it was no wonder</p>
        <p>THE NATION HAD MADE lIP ITS MIND that it had had quite enough of this sort of thing,and the longer the Government held on and the worse the debacle would be when it came. He did not think Liberals nevd be the least distressed if they saw the Government clinging to office a few months longer, except that the ' spectacle had become rather a disgrace to our political life. (Hear, hear.) At any rate, everyone on the Conservative side was simply filled with a holy horror of the day of reckoning. That was quite plain, and really the events in that day's paper seemed to threaten a general smash up of this rickety Govern- ment any time. He hoped, therefore that all in that meeting would be well prepared for the battle which might have to be fought at any minute, and determined to do all they could to support Mr. King in the very plucky, cheerful, and thoroughly sound fight he was making in that constituency. (Applause). Whether the day of reckoning would come immediately or a little bit later they cotild not tell. The Govern- ment were, as they might say, "HANGING ON BY THEIR EYE-LIDS." Their majority was wearing away, and Liberal workers must have their armour ready and be prepared to pat their war-paint on for the impending contest. They were very grateful to Dr. Massie for the comprehensive and instructive address he had given them. Certainly he hadjfilled up the cup of the Government's enormities till it was almost running over, but -the things he bad dealt with were very serious. Dr. Massie had touched on education which was one of the things they valued most as a means towards a better civilization, a better spread of wealth, and more happiness for the people at large. There was one purpose to which education should be devoted,and that was towards following with increas- ing intelligence and vigilance the public affairs of the country. (Hear, hear.) He hoped everyone in the district would do that, and that they would not be led astray by the sort of pressure that was sometimes brought to bear in country districts. Of course, he did not suggest that improper influence was used at Stockton Heath, but still they did hear in country constituencies of voters who were afraid that the ballot was not secret and that they must go the way of the big territorial magnates. He would ask his hearers not to be led astray like that—not to sell their birthright for a moss of pottage. "Take a broad view of the situation" concluded Mr. Crosfield "Recognize, as Sir John Brunner put it the other day, that we want twenty years of good, sonnd progressive government, and further, having made up your minds, do, I respect- fully beg of you, give my friend Mr. King a very hearty backing." (Loud Applause). Mr. C. Jackson Holmes briefly seconded the resolution which was carried with applause. Dr. Massie fittingly replied and urged the need for work on the part of Liberals and union amongst tho ranks of Liberalism at the next general election to en- sure the return of Mr. King. He concluded by moving ivote of thanks to the chairman.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. S. Hughes seconded and spoke of the pleasure with which he witnessed the return of Mr. H. Roberts to active Liberalism. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded. Mr. Roberts in responding said he wished it to be clearly understood that he had never left the ranks of Liberalism. After some years of pAitical work he did think he could take matters more easily but so far as physical strength would allow he intended to do his utmost both in that constituency and in Warrington at the coming general election—as he felt that the fate of the country as a commercial nation was at stake—to preyen', any departure from the principles of free trado--one-sided free trade if they liked. Ile was not afraid cf.discuss- ing the question on those lines, as he believed that half a loaf was bettor than no bread at all. He did not see why any man because he could not get all he wanted should refuse to accept a substantial portion. He hti.t another reason for returning to politics.. Ho.- was the son of a Liberal and he could remember his father saw- ing that as he was a Nonconformist he could not hold any office under the state. He had to thank the,Liberal party for the fact that Nonconformists were now on a level with the rest of the citizens of our great. empire, and he would be recreant to the heritage into Nvhich he had come if he did not hand on to those to . fclbaw him those privileges unimpared. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Directors in submitting their retort and balance , ---- sheet stated that the profit including 211 Is. 9d. brought . Five feet Is not far to fall. It is less than a man's forward from last year was £202 14s. Bd. The reason ' own height. Yet • such a fall has often crippled a for so small a profit this year was owing to the difficul- strong man and led to a painful death. Mr. James ties the Directors had experienced in finding good Byrne. 31, Lower Russell-street, Miles Platting, securities for the balances to the credit of the Society Manchester. said: " I fell five feet on to my back. in the bank during the early part of the year, and for I thought little of the which, only Bank interest was received. The Directors 9ccident at the time, but recommended that as the Society was in a sound c months afterwards I financial position the sum of £2O be transferred from ff er e d agonies from the Reserve Fund to this year's profits, and that a divi- •alysis of the Spine as dend at the rate of £4 per cent per annum free of i iirect result. If you income tax be paid to the shareholders and after allow- ! to hear what won- ing .£7l Is. for working expenses, to carry the balance Dr. Williams' Pink of £6 19s. 6d. forward to next year's account. The can do, here are the directors again urgently solicited the assistance of the ! of my miraculous shareholders in making known the advantages derived I felt no ill-effects at from joining the society. Members were requested to i , but presently pains bring their pass books to be checked by the Auditors. I An, in my back. I went The number of members on the books was 54, of which i i doctor, and lay up for 13 were borrowers. mtnight. Thanks to his ALDERMAN SIITTON'S ABSENCE. I 1 I cot about again; but Mr. W. D. JEANS expressed regret at the absence of ; , pains returned, and the manager (Alderman Sutton) who had been at the I though I managed to fore of the society for the past thirty years. It was ; parajoi.., _ __ from tme struggle on for a time I about thirty years since Alderman Sutton and he (the fail. B - 'ls said Mr. had to go into Hospital, and speaker) were the chief movers in promoting the 1 Blfras was crippled for U/.. even after I was sent home society and he believed they were the only founders ; Dr. Williams' Pink Pia had to return for a further who were left. He was extremely sorry that Alderman . sontyletelu cured him. period. I was nearly eight Sutton had ben in such a had state of health of late months under hospital treatment, and lathe end was</p>
        <p>and he hoped he would soon be himself again—(hear, hear)—and live for many years to be present at their annual meetings. With regard to the society it had, in a small way, been a very prosperous one. It had never done a very large business but what it had done had been of a safe nature, and he thought all the people connected with it had been satisfied with its trans- actions. Every discretion had been used in accepting securities and he was not aware of loss having been sustained by any of them. Mr. HOLPORD also expressed regret at Aldet man Sutton's absence for the first time for at least twenty- four or twenty-five years. He suggested that a vote of sympathy should be sent by the society together with an expression of their hopes for speedy recovery. He had heard Alderman Sutton say that not a penny had been lost in the transactions of the society, and that was in a great measure due to the manager. It was decided to forward the vote of sympathy. The report was adopted. ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The following directors were re-elected :—Messrs. John Andrews (chairman), George Holford, T. H. A. Pilkington, W. Roberts, Henry Houghton and S. F. Welsby. Mr. J. G. CHAPPELL were reappointed professional auditor. Mr. J. H. CLARKE, his fellow-auditor, resigned the office and was heartily thanked for his services. In responding he said he had audited the books for twenty-nine years and they had always been excellently kept. To his knowledge, the society had not lost a single penny, and it was a perfectly sound concern. They all deeply regretted the absence of Alderman Sutton whom he (Mr. Clarke) had met their on similar occasions for thirty years. The appointment of a successor to Mr. Clarke was deferred.</p>
        <p>rorpitcfi</p>
        <p>DISCHARGED INCURABLE.' Imagine my misery when told that I should probably get worse as time went on. I lay in bed waiting for the end." Mr. Byrne had lost all hope. He was crippled and helpless, and had to be fed and washed like a baby. ' Friends used to come in pity to cheer him. One of them, who knew how Dr. Williams' Pink Pills care where all else fails, advised them for him. Here is Mr. , Byrne's account of the result. I "When I had taken the second box I had improved so much that I managed to crawl out of bed and put my clothes on. After the third box I was a bit stronger; my appetite was getting decidedly better. The fourth box of pills enabled me to get out of bed and dress with a litle assistance. I kept on with the pills, and at length could do with one stick. Remember, I had been in bed at home for six weeks; for four weeks I walked with two sticks, and then for nine weeks vclth one. Since then I have had no stick at all. They told me at the Hospital that I had Paralysis of the Spine, and would never walk any more. I-am now not only quite well, thanks to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, but thinking</p>
        <p>of marriage." Seeing that they are proved to cure the very worst forms of nervous disease, as Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, and curvature of the spine, it is not surprising that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People cure lighter cases as sciatica, St. Vitus' Dance, neuralgia, nervous breakdown, and lame back. For anemia, con- sumption, indigestion, rheumatism' and all disorders arising from poor or bad blood, and for all the troubles that ladies suffer in silence, they are unrivalled. A box will be sent post free for 2s. 9d. by Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London. But they can be had at all medicine shops if purchasers will take care to see the full name (seven words) on the package.</p>
