      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>A Datan's FUNLEAL.—An interment ot a rather unique character, was that of the late Mr. WJ. Hayes WhO was laid to rest on Thursday afternoon. After A painful illness, lasting nearly two years, he passed away on Saturday last at the early age of 31 years. The deceased was a member of the Penketh United Lodge of the Order of Druids, and since the inception of the Lodge some 14 years ago, this is the f. rst member's death. His fellow members determined to inter the deceased other with the ceremony and rites peculiar to the order. Some 14 felibw members arrayed in the sashes &amp;c., as dpereed by the rules, I attended at the house to pay the last respects. As the loofa' was brought into the street. the Vice-Grand 'Master of the Order (Bro. D. Houghton), wearing his 1 chain of office, read the special funeral address for Druids. The cortege then proceeded to.tbe Cemetery, the coffin being borne by the Lodge, officers, being relieved by four personal friends of the deceased. The funeral service was conducted by the vicar, Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A., and was witnessed by a large number of people. Several beautiful wseaths were sent by friends and relatives, and included one from the employees of toe Penketh Tanning Company, where the deceased was employed. WESLEYAN CHAPEL.,--At the Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday last, the monthly open Sunday service was held. The services of the Penketh Tannery Prize Band had been obtained for this service and marched through the streets playing sacred music. They also supplied the music for the hymns during the service. The speaker was Mr. Harvey, of Widnes, .who gave an earnest address, which was attentive 4 'stoned tc. There was a large attendance.</p>
        <p>Treat to Poor Children. Some four hundred and fifty poor children from various parts of the town, irrespective of creed, were on Saturday afternoon given a tea by the oMcials of the Buckley-street Band of Hope Society. After the schoolroom had been cleared the children were enter- tained to a concert which fittingly concluded an enjoy- able day. Mr. T. Cook presided. Solos were given by Miss E. Keen, Mr. A. Whalley, and Mr. James Ryder ; recitations by Miss A. Brittain, Miss Cross, Mr. W. Shaw, and Mr. E. Ryder ; and a dialogue entitled " The Wanderer's 'Rescue," by Messrs. H. Woolley, A. Trow, and J. Urmston. Miss M. Plinston was the accompanist. Mr. J. Adams, on behalf of the Band of Hope Committee, presented to Miss A. Brittain a copy of the New Testament, for collecting enough food and donations to provide for 125 children, As she received the present the children gave her a very hearty cheer. At the close of the concert each child was presented with an orange and a bun.</p>
        <p>Railway Servant Injured. Peter Knight, of 63, Winwick-road, was admitted to the Infirmary on Wednesday morning, suffering from a broken leg; the injury having been sustained whilst he was following tii,l ernPloyukellt on the. Cheshire Lines Itailwv.</p>
      </div>
