      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.' RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>i PRECOCIOUS YOUNG THIEF. -- f Joseph Fletcher, aged ten. of Halton-road, Runcorn, A HEARTLESS HUSBAND. , was a week ago sentenced to six strokes with the birch At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday before rod for defrauding a confectioner. On Wednesday he Mr. Geddes and other magistrates, William Henry Steel was again charged with stealing a purse and is. 3d. from was charged with running away from the borough and the house of John Woodward. It appeared that while leaving his wife and children chargeable to the Union. Mrs. Woodward was visiting the prisoner's sick mother —Mr. Shearer stated that on the 21st January prisoner, the priconer entered the house, stole the purse and W i who was engaged at Messrs. Skelton's, left arrington, money, and spent a portion n the purchase of cakes and leaving his wife and children unprovided for. The toffee.—He told the bench be did it because he wanted wife had not been taken to the Workhouse as she was to be sent away.—The prisoner was remanded for a not in a fit condition to be moved.—Mrs. Steel stated week in order to see if a reformatoly school could be that when her husband left her he took all the money in i found for him. the house, saying he was going to Southport for the A FORGIVING WIFE. week-end. Since that time she had not seen or beard At Runcorn on Wednesday, Jobn M'Malion, farm from him.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months' , labourer, Aston, was brought up on remend for the hard labour. ' seventh time on a charge of unlawfully wounding his FATHER ASSAULTS HIS SON. wife, Ellen, on Christmas Day. i The woman had been I very ill, and was now carded into com. on a chair, At the Warrington police court yesterday, William 1 being unable to walk. She said that on Christmas Day Caldwell, John-street, was charged with being drunk . her husband was drunk, and she had had some drink, and disorderly and with assaulting his son. The evid- IHe went out, and in the evening her two daughters left ence showed that the prisoner went home drunk and . the house, and she was in the kitchen alone when her commenced to quirrel with bis wife. His son interfered husband returned. He anused her of having another whereupon prisoner violently assaulted him. Prisoner , mall in the house,and strnck her several times, and then was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour. ' struck a match and threw it at her, setting her cloth- ' lug on fire. She did not know whether he did itpur- posely. Witness now stated that her previous state- ments were not true, and whatever took place she had no desire to press the charge. She wished to withdraw it, as he had promised to beFave better in future, She was as bad as he Was at the time. Under these circum- stances the magistrates discharged the p.isoner,andMr. Imison told him he ought to be thankful his wife was so merciful to him.</p>
      </div>
