      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>gil, -,, i t t –</head>
        <p>gil, -,, i t t -</p>
        <p>e Exaraitter,</p>
        <p>(Copyright.) THE AUNT AND THE LI;iT011:</p>
        <p>By E. NESBIT,</p>
        <p>Author of The 3farden Mystery," "Something Wrong," "Grim Tales," "The Prophi.. t Mantle," "The Butler in Bohemia," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Aunt Kate was the great comfort of Rx.ty's exist- ence. Always kindly, helpful, sympathetic, no girlish trouble was too slight, no girlish question too difficult for her tender heart—her delicate insight. How different from grim Aunt Eliza, with whom it was Kitty's fate to live. Aunt Eliza was severe, methodical, energetic. In household matters she spared neither herself nor her niece. Kitty could darn and mend and bake and dust and sweep in a way which might have turned the parents of the bluest Girtonian green with envy. She had read a great deal, too—the really solid works that are such a nuisance to get through, and that leave a mark on one's mind like the track of a steam-roller. That was Aunt Eliza's doing. Kitty ought to have been grateful—but she wasn t. She didn't want to be improved with solid books. She wanted to write books herself. She did write little tales when her aunt was out on business, which was often, and she dreamt of the day when she should write beautiful books—poems—romances. These Aunt Eliza classed roughly as "stuff and nonsense " ; and one day, when she found Kitty reading the Girls Very Own Friend, she tore that harmless little weekly across and across and flung it into the fire. Then she faced Kitty with flushed face and angry eyes. "If I ever catch you bringing such rubbish into the house again I'll—l'll stop your music lessons." This was a horrible threat. Kitty went twice a week to the Guildhall School of Music. She had no musical talent whatever, but the journey to London and back was her one glimpse of the world's tide that flowed outside the neat, gloomy, ordered house at Streatham. Therefore, Kitty was careful that Aunt Eliza should not, again "catch her bringing such rubbish into the house." But she went on reading the paper all the same, just as she went on writing her little stories. And presently she got one of her little stories typewritten, and sent it to the Girls' Very Own Friend. It was a silly little story—the heroine was svelte, I am sorry to say, and had red-gold hair and a soft traumata voice—and the hero was a "frank-looking young Englishman, with a bronzed face and honest blue eyes. The plot was that with which I firmly believe every career of fiction begins—the girl who throws over her lover because he has jilted her friend. Then she finds out that it was not her lover but his brother or cousin. We have all written this story in our time, and Kitty wrote it much worse than many, but not nearly so badly as most of us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>Th</p>
        <p>Saturday,</p>
        <p>tobacCo smoke, compreezco very dusty, untidy room—in which stood three young men. Their faces were grave and serious, but Kate could not forget that one of them had laughed, and laughed like,that. Her chin went about a quarter of an inch further. "I am sorry to have disturbed you," she said, severely. "I wanted to see—to see the lady who signs herself Aunt Kate." There was a moment of silence which seemed almost breathless. Two of the young men exchanged a glance, but though Kitty perceived it to be significant, she could not interpret its meaning. Then one of the three turned to gaze out of the window at the blackened glass roof of the printing-office below. Kitty felt certain he was concealing a smile; and the second hurriedly arranged a bundle of papers beside him. The third young man spoke, and Kitty liked the gentle drawl, the peculiar enunciation. The poor girl, in the invincible ignorance born of her Streatham seclusion, had never before heard the "Oxford voice." "I am very sorry," he said, "but Aunt Kate' is not here to-day. Perhaps—is there anything I could do?" "No, thank you," said Kitty, wishing herself =lles away-, the tobacco smoke choked her, the backs of the two other men seemed an outrage. She Owned away with a haughty bow, and went down the grimy stairs full of fury. She could have slapped herself. How could she have been such a fool as to come there? There were feet comin; down the stairs behind her—she quickened her pace. The feet came more quickly: She stopped on the landing and turned with an odd feeling of beinw at bay. It was the fair-haired young man with the Oxford voice.</p>
        <p>"I am so very sorry," he said, gently, "but I did not know. I did not expect to see—l mean, I did not know who you were. And we had all been smokingl am so sorry," he said again, rather lamely. "It doesn't matter," said Kitty, more shyly than she had ever spoken in her life. She liked his eyes and his voice as much as she loathed the expressive backs of his two companions. "If you could come again, perhaps Aunt Kate will be here on Thursday. I know she will be sorry to miss you," the young man went on. "I think I won't call again, thank you," said Kitty. "I—l'll write, thank you; it s all. right. I oughtn't to have come. Good-bye." There was nothing for it but to stand back and let her pass. The editor went back slowly to his room. His friends had relighted their pipes. "Appeased the outraged goddess ? " asked one of them. "Good old Aunt Kate I" said the other.</p>
        <p>"Shut up, Sellars!" said the editor, frowning. "Now, which of your correspondents is it ?" pondered Sellars, ruffling the bundle of papers in his hand. "Is it Wild Woodbine,' who wants to know what will make her hands white Chilcott, did you see her hands? Oh, no, of course—Lien chausse, bias gantie. All- brown, too. Is it 'Sylph' l'—no; she wants a pattern for a Zouave. What is a Zouave, if you please, Mr. Editor?" "Dry up!" said the editor; but Sellars was busy with the papers. "Eureka I I know her. She's Nut-brown 'laid '—here's the letter—wants to know if she .uay tali to 'a young gentleman she has not been properly” introduced to '—spells it interduced,' too--</p>
        <p>The editor snatched the papers out of the other's hands.</p>
        <p>"Now, clear out!" said he. "I'm busy." "Am I dreaming P" said Sellars, pensively. "Or is this the editor who invited us to collaborate with him in his Answers to Correspondents ? " "I am the editor who will kick you down the entire five flights, if he is driven to it. You won't drive him, will you?" The two laughed, but they took up their hats and went; Sellars put his head round the door for a last word.</p>
        <p>"What price love at first sight ? " said he, and thA office ruler dented the door as he disappeared round it. The editor, left alone, sat down in his chair and looked helplessly round him. "Well!" he said, musingly. "Well, well, well, well!" Then after a long silence he took up his pen and began the Answers to Correspondents. " DINII-DONNEE. —Your hair is a very nice colour. I should not advise Aureoline.</p>
        <p>" 8 lIY FAIRY. —By all means consult your mother. Heliotrope would suit your complexion if it is, as you say, of a brilliant fairness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>March 4, 1905.</head>
        <p>March 4, 1905.</p>
        <p>THE " SANITAS " COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The Annual General Neeting (27th in number) of the "Sanitas " Company, Limited (Disinfectant Manufac- turers), was held on the 22nd inst., at their new premises in Locksley-stree, Limehouse, London, E., under the presidency of the Chairman, Mr. C. T. Kingsett, F. 1.0., F.C.S., who congratulated the shareholders upon the continued prosperity of the business and their prospects for the future. Although the past year had not been by any means a favourable one to their class of business in particular, and MS generally recognised as a poor one in respect of the general trade of the country, the busi- ness had continued to expand, and their sales were greater than in any previous year. The reason why, under these favourable circumstances, their shares had suffered any depreciation in market price was one of those things dilicult to understand, except as arising out of the machinations of their trade rivals and others who were envious of their proud position. The jealousy, animosity and rivalry of others, however, only instigated them, he rejoiced to say, to new and better efforts in the way of improving their manufactures, inventing new articles and giving greater satisfaction to the millions who bought and used their goods, and he trusted that it ever would be so. The balance to credit of profit and loss account, after making provision for bad and doubtful debts, repairs to plant and machinery and other charges and conting- encies, adding the amount brought forward from 1903 of £2,227 9s. 7d and deducting income tax, amounted to £l_ ,5 582 12s. sd. Pt was resolved to place £5OO to depreciation account £1,500 to reserve account, and to pay a final dividend of .1 per cent., making, with 43- per cent., and a bonus of the interim dividend already paid, the usual total dis- tribution of 71 per cent, for the year. This would leave £1,840 lls. 6d, to he carried forward to 1905. The retiring director (Mr. C. E. Russell) was then re-elected, Mr. Eric P. Kingzett (son of the chairman) was elected as a new director, the retiring auditors, Messrs. Williams and Newman, were re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>GAMBLING WOMAN.</head>
        <p>THE GAMBLING WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Another evidence has been afforded this week in the Courts of the extent to which women nowadays gamble. A story is told of a young, pretty, but unscrupulous woman who played for stakes which, in the event of loss, she could not possibly pay. The game was ecarte, and she went on losing till ruin stared her in the face. " Double or quits ?" suggested the man with whom she was playing. She agreed—and lost. " Are you propos- ing to marry me ? " she asked. Th 9 reply was in the affirmative. They played again, she lost—and is now his wife.</p>
        <p>A TUNNEL THROUGH MONT BLANC. The French Ministry of Public Works has commis- sioned M. Jackuier to project plans for a railway be- tween Chamonix and Aosta. He considers the difficulty would not be so great as with the Simplon tunnel; the tunnel would he 41 miles shorter, and the rock gives no indication of subterranean reservoirs of water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LAND MONOPOLY. PAPER BY MR. CHARLES E. PARKER.</head>
        <p>THE LAND MONOPOLY. PAPER BY MR. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>The following paper on "The Land Monopoly" was submitted by Mr. Charles E. Parker, president of the Warrington Liberal Association, at the meeting of the Social Service League last night neek : The land monopoly is the mother and the foster mother of all monopolies. TJnrestricted private owner- ship of natural material, forces and opportunities, make it possible for individual) possessing these, to compel those who do not possess them (the landowners) to hand over a considerable portion of the result of their labour for permission to live and work in the world into which they are born. Increasing knowledge of the conditions of human life demands with increasing emphasis that there should be equal opportunity for all. If we are all here the off-spring of one Father, surely we the children, all have an equal right to His bounty. As far as the natural man:is concerned, we are treated all alike. He sendeth the rain and the sunshine alike upon the just and the unjust. Nature makes no distinction, it treats the earl and the peasant alike. There is the same travail in birth, the same dependence upon the parents, the same reward for obedience to nature's laws, the same punishment for their violation. The same span of life, the same reward for the same labour, the same downhill for old age -andthe same dust to dust in an equal six feet of mother earth.</p>
        <p>It is because with private possession of natural things there cannot be equality of opportunity that I challenge the justice of private property in land. The subject naturally divides itself into two parts First the wrong which private monopoly involves and second the remedy for this wrong. Dealing with the second part, the remedy but only fora moment (for I recognize that I will require all the time I can pro- perly claim to make clear the injustice which private monopoly of land makes inevitable,) I recognized very clearly the difficulty that any scheme which aims at the State resum- ing its ancient control of the land for the people is Immense. Herbert Spencer in his Social Station (and here I may say that what I here advocate is largely drawn from the teachings of men like Herbert Spencer and John Stuart Mill) says "Had we to deal with the parties who originally robbed the human race of its heritage we might make short work of the matter." This suggests the difficulty, for land has been bought and sold, it has been exchanged for stored-up labour in the form of capital. It has become an investment for the savings of the poor and rich alike, and any remedy which does not fairly recognize</p>
        <p>THE CLAIM OF THE PRESENT HOLDERS would,:in trying to right one wrong, create another. You cannot right justice by injustice. Whether the state resumed control of the land as a whole or took possession by taxation of what is known as unearned increment, fair consideration would have to be given to existing possessors. Leaving then, the difficult question of a remedy,let us return to the primary one, the wrong itself. In dealing with the question it will be necessary to define clearly certain terms which are sure to come up both in this paper and in the discussion which fol- lows such terms as land monopoly, rent, capital, and interest- I can best do this by way of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ILLusTRAiIoN.</head>
        <p>ILLusTRAiIoN.</p>
        <p>and in so doing I can also suggest the argument. Sup- pose that there is an island and a single man is washed up on it. We will callithis man A. Robinson Crusoe- like he takes possession; he calls the island his island. Suppose he belongs to a very early type of the human species, he will then satisfy his hunger by the nuts and the roots growing upon his island. Suppose now, two other men are washed up on the shore whom we will call and B and C. They, being land animals, as we all are, desire to satisfy their wants out of the land. But A being in possession has monopolized the whole of the island by his priority of occupation B and C can only live and work for their food by permission of A. A sees in the situa- tion the opportunity of relieving himself of the obliga- tion to toil, and agrees to let B and C live on his island on condition that they each give to him A a third of the fruits of their labour. B and C having no alterative if they are to live, agree to A's terms. If we take that two parts of nuts mid roots are necessary to sustain each man, then B and C have each to dig and collect three parts, two for themselves one for A. A having two parts, one from each, does not require to work at all, but can live in sloth while B and C work for him. These parts so paid represent true rent, rent in the economic sense. To proceed a step further B sets his brains to work to lighten his task. He finds that to use a stick instead of his hands lightens his toil, by placing a cross piece on the top, his hand has now power in , digging, and by making a shoulder towards the bottom of his tool and using his foot to helphishand his labour is greatly eased. When this is done we have the rudi- ments of a spade. While Bis busy making kis spade he can dig no roots, but he reckons that he will more than catch up for the delay by the possession of a more effective instrument. His labour is stored up in a spade : it is this we call Capital C sees his neighbour getting up roots with his spade in far shorter time than he can with his hands. He goes to B and asks him to make him a spade. B replies that he will make and lend him a spade if C will pay to B certain quantity of the roots he digs with it as a return for the time and trouble B has spent in making the spade. C finds that even with this payment to Bhe will I still have left more roots than he would have had work- ing with his hands, and so he agrees to B's term's and the payment for the use of the capital of B, C pays, is interest in the form of the roots. Herethen we have the Land Monopoly which A exer- cises because of hi a priority of occupation, and that monopoly to relieve him of his primal obligation to earn his living by the sweat of his brow. We have B and C obliged to do extra work because of the monopoly A enjoys and the result of this extra toil being handed over to A, constitutes what is known as rent—economic rent. We have further,B storing a portion of hisJabour in the form of spades, which correspond to what we call Capital, and we have C preferring to borrow a spade from B rather than to make one himself, and pay B for its use by a portion 4tif his roots which corresponds to what we call Interest To carry the illustration further and connect it with our own condition, in course of time men multiply upon the island, the island passes by inheritance to the chil- dren of A who in their turn exact rent from the children of B and C. The land becomes more valuable—or rather it does not alter at all in real value but the necessities of an inc-easing population enable the A's to exact more from the B's and C's. Capital in the form of better appliances are invented by the B's, though A's exact their share of the increased output. Coal is found and the workers commence to dig. The A's claim a royalty so much a ton on all that is brought to the surface. If it has to be brought on any of the ' reserved land of the A's, the worker must pay a rent for this in the form of a "wayleave." If they desire to build houses they can have land by paying a yearly rent the land with the houses on it being the property of the A's say after 99 years. If the workers associate in towns then again the A's can get a bigger rent because of the increased necessity of position. And so we see that the land monopoly is one which in an increasing population enables those who hold the land to levy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>AN. EVER INCRE A SING BLACKMAIL</head>
        <p>AN. EVER INCRE A SING BLACKMAIL</p>
        <p>upon those who are obliged to pay the rent or cease to live. Thus we have a portion of the community living in idleness and increasing luxury, while the workers have ever an increasing buiden to bear. We have here a double wr6ng, those who receive without producing are wronged by the subversion of the principles upon which the human race exists in the planet and which I believe spring from a just Creator: while those who produce are wronged by having a portion of the just result of this toil taken from them to enable the holders of the land to live in ignoble idleness. An equal right to develop and use the bounties of mother earth are essential if we are to have equal opportunity for all. What a man dces with his opportunity rests with himself, here his bulividnally has free scope. But we must all start fair, or a benumbing sense of wrong will oppress those who are handicapped in the race and will</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PARALYSE INDIVIDUAL EFFORT</head>
        <p>PARALYSE INDIVIDUAL EFFORT</p>
        <p>and ambition. A curtailment of the land monopoly is absolutely necessary if we are to have a healthy and vigourous lam aware that the conditions portrayed in the island I used as illustration are not exactly re-produced in modern conditions. The problem looks more compli- cated. I think this is so more in appearance than in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>reality. The complication largely springs from a mis- use of the terms I tried to explain. In common thought and speech, capital is confused with land, interest confused with rent. For example a man here may own a house and receive say £2O rent for it. I might have asked do I challenge his right to receive this because it is rent. By no means, but rent is not the right term for it, at least it is not economic rent. Sup- pose £lOO represents the price paid for the land and £3OO the cost of the house £5 is rent proper and £l5 is interest for the labour in building the house. The latter corresponds to the spade made by B, the former the roots received by A.But it must not be thought that the £3OO the cost of building all went to the " B's " and "C's." Part of the cost of every article in construc- tion paid its tax to the "A" of society. The brick- maker paid his rent for his brickfteld. the joiner paid a rent in the price for his wood which in turn came to the owner of the forest in which the trees were grown, the iron in construction paid its nut in royalties, way- leaves, carriage over land, &amp;c., and so with every single article used, This suggests an important point in this question. The claim to private possession of land is not a claim which can be met and satisfied and thus cleared out of the way. It is a claim which is being perpetually made and pi 'petually meth is a tax on present day industry and every man whether he pays rent directly or not pays his share. It is this system of private ownership in land that cramps our towns and makes , congeste4 areas inevitable, that raises rents in large towns and which makes people herd together in small miserable tene- ments. It renders possible the buying up by large trusts natural products such as oil &amp;c., and so creates a monopoly of some of the most useful and necessary things required to satisfy human wants,</p>
        <p>_ _ IT CRIPPLES INDUSTRY, it helps more than any other thing to bring about the periodical returns of bad trade which occur in every decade. Trade is active, profits are large, wages are good, this causes development of industries, raw material goes up creating a greater demand for the land upon which it is grown. People want better houses, more room, and thus is created a demand for land. Up go land values. Land is bought and held until prices have risen,thus paralysing industries which cannot afford to pay the increased rent. Depression follows, work becomes scarce, wages do down, and so we pass through a few years depression until trade revives or land falls to a figure at which it can be profitably employed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sephton, the medical officer to the Warrington Rural District Council has issued his annual report, from which it appears that during the year 1901 there were 295 births and 144 deaths. The birth-rate was equal to 25-1 per 1,000, as against 26•9 in 1903, and 27• S in 1902, while the death-rate was equal to 12-2 per 1,000 as compared with 13-1 in 1903 and 12.6 in 1902. The principal cause ef death was diarrhcea which claimed 15 victims, thirteen of whom were infants under one year of age. The morality from this disease Dr. Sephton attributed to improper feeding. Of the deaths 45 were of children under one year of age, 11 between the ages of 1 and 5, 55 between the ages of 5 and 65, and 33 over 65 years of age. The infant death rate was higher than the previous year being equal to 1525 per 1000 births registered. Two hundred and eight deaths occurred at the Wintvick Asylum, a death rate of 96 per 1000 of the inmates. Dealing with the principal outline also of disease Dr. Sephton reports that one case of smallpox were recorded front Sankey. The patient was removed to the hospital. No other cases of the disease was reported. Seventy-eight cases of scarlet fever were notified as against 40 the previous year. Of these only one proved fatal the disease being of a very mild type. There had been a serene outbreak of measles in Penketh which had necessitated the closing of the schools for two weeks. [ Eleven cases of enteric fever were notified, one of [ which proved fatal, six cases ocoured in the Asylum Winwick, there were eighteen cases of erysipelas disease reported, one of which proved fatal. Whooping cough was accountable for five deaths. An epidemic of this disease accompanied with Chicken-pox occurred at I Rixton with Glazebrook in November and December. I Bronchitis and pneumonia accounted for twenty-three I deaths which was slightly lower than previous years, and equals a death-rate of 1-9 per thousand. On the subject of hospital accommodation Dr. Sephton says: —The Warrington Borough still co,,tinue to accept into their Infectious Diseases Hospital our cases when they have room, but unfortunately so many cases have occurred in the Borough that very little accommoda- tion has been left and we have only sent two cases of scarlet fever during the year, the rest being isolated at home. On the question of the drainage of Fearnhead and Poulton the report says this subject has frequently, during the year, been before your Council, and many plans have been suggested, but on account of the extreme flatness of the district ithas been very difficult to carry out any scheme, except at great cost. However an arrangement is being made with the Warrington Corporation to receive into their sewers and treat the sewage from the above townships. The nuisance at Longforddepot Dr.Sephton dismisses with the brief remark that " the nuisance arising from Longford depot, which has been so frequently com- plained of, has during the year been considerably abated."—</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLlTRES.—According to Elemp's Mer- cantile Gazette, the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Feb. 24th, was 196, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 105; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 91. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 106; Deeds of Arrangement, 91—Total 197 ; being an increase of 1. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 8 weeks of the present year is 1,453 ; the total numcer re- corded in the corresponding 8 weeks of last year was 11,930 showing an increase of 63.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
