      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Warrington V. Swinton.</head>
        <p>Warrington V. Swinton.</p>
        <p>Warrington's opponents on Saturday were their old- time foes from Swinton, but on this occasion they were looked upon as easy prey, and several changes had been made in the home team, by way of experiment. The introduction of Berry at centre was the most interest- ing innovation, while J. Davies replaced D. Davies at half back. Forward there was some weakness owing to the absence of Boardman, Preston, and shugars. Fine weather prevailed, and there was a crowd of about 4000. Warrington: Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Berry, and Harris, three-quarter backs; R. Bate and J. Davies, half backs; Thomas, Belton, Cook. Jolley, Naylor, Swift, Monson, and Harmer, forwards. Swinton : Dixon, back; 'Gartrell. Valentine, Wall- work, and Flynn, three-quarter backs ; Coletrap. I Cheatham, Barnett, Larkin, Jones, Massey, Cooper, and Pomfret, forwards. Referee, Mr, W. !IMO, Morecambe. Touch judges Messrs. H. Cawley, Runcorn, and E. Williams, Widnes Warrington were early on the attack, once operations commenced, and the Swinton defence was well-tried. The home halves were slow in getting the ball away, however, and the three-quarter line was not so effective as usual. Berry early tried to force his way over, but succumbed to numbers, while on another OCCII.SioII the ball was whipped across to Fish, who was captured near the corner by Gartrell. Swinton retaliated with vigour Molyaeux and R. Valentine handling well in the direction of Gartrell, who twice made strong efforts to get past Fish but was unsuccessful. Berry drove Swinton back again with a ponderous kick, and there was some hot work on the line, Belton failing to take an easy chance of scoring Warrington scrummaged hard near the goal and got the ball out. Davies came round and passed to Isherwood, who should have scored easily but was out of his place when the ball came and he knocked on. Bate tried to scramble over himself, but was held, and Swinton removed play to midfield, half time coming with no score. On resuming, Warrington went off again with great dash and looked liked getting a substantial point. The backs combined too poorly, however, and the wing men were covered. Moiyneux got his side penalised by over-eagerness to clear, and. Berry carefully kicked a goal amid cheers. Warrington still remained on the aggressive and passing between Bate, Davies and Berry looked promising until Valentine snapped the pass from Berry to Fish and got away with a clear field. The lengthy Swintonian, however, lacked pace, and after running well into the home half he had to kick, and Harris cleared. Molyneux, by good touch work, took Play to the home lino, and Swinton made strong efforts to score. Valentine was twice in possession a yard from the line, but could net get through. Warrington eventually took matters into their own hands and Fish made a couple of brilliant individual efforts at the corner. Once he got over but was forced into touch-in-goal. Another excursion by Swinton provided Warrington with the best opening of the day. Fish fielded the ball in his own half and beating Gartrell he came clear away with Berry in attendance. A try seemed a eertainty as the full back was neared, but Fish held on to the ball and was finally hemmed in. Time came very Soon after with Warrington winning somewhat luckily bY a goal (2 points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. SWINTON "A."</p>
        <p>Playe.! at Swinton on Saturday. Soon after the start Warrington were penalized, and Oldtield placed a goal from near midfield. Warrinatonattacked strongly after this reverse. and Bate and Davies opening out the game. Several capital openings were made, but the real men appeared unable to cope with the opposition. An attack on the Swinton line looked like ending in a score, but Short knocked forward. A penalty was awarded War- rington but Knight sent the hall wide. Swinton again defended and a brilliant round of passing having seemed likely to be effective, but Short knocked forward, and a splendid chance was lost. The visitors still kept the home team in their quarters, and Swinton being penal- ised-a shot at goal was taken for Warrington from a favourable position, but failed. The interval arrived with the score—Swinton" A" (one goal) two points ; Warrington" A" nil. In the second half Swinton Opened well, and the Warrington line ;was subjected to a hot fusillade. Knight directed operations to half,and eventually Warrington took up a strong attacking posi- tion, but the defence prevailed and at the close War rington were defeated by a goal (two points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
