      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A FOOD AND A MEDICINE. Remarkable Medical Report.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A FOOD AND A MEDICINE. Remarkable Medical Report.</p>
        <p>Dr. KIRILLOFF in a paper communicated to the Eastern Siberian Medical Association dealt with the virtues of Seaweed in blood diseases. He said :—Sea- weed is widely used as a food in Japan, and is it contains combined lodine, blood diseases which assume such alarming characteristics in other countries run a com- paratively mild course in Japan. Edible Seaweed is 'used as an article of diet in Corn- wall and certain parts of Ireland. Strengthening jellies for invalids are made from it, and it is a very noticeable feature that those who use it possess remarkably pure blood, and are almost free from stomach, liver, and kidney troubles. VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC is the only Medicinal preparation of Seaweed on the market. It contains a deep-water species obtained in the Pacific Ocean which possesses a wonderful strengthening, healing,and purify- ing power. Veno's Seaweed Tonics is prescribed by doctors and is used in many hospitals because of its remarkable curative properties. Its most brilliant effect is produced in diseases of the stomach, liver, kidneys, and blood. The extraordinary cures it accomplishes even in the worse cases is undeniable proof of its superior value as a general strengthening and curative medicine. It is invaluable in the spring and autumn ; it purifies the blood, cures indigestion, wind on the stomach, liver complaints and kidney troubles, female weakness, weak and painful back, and is very effective in " HABITUAL CONSTIPATION." _ _</p>
        <p>The proprietors knowing the great value of this medicine are prepared to guarantee a cure or refund the money. This is a very strong stand to take, but it is justified by the great success of the remedy. Ask for Veno's Seaweed Tonic. Price Is. lid. and 2s. 9d. per bottle at chemists and drug stores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL. Record of Year's Work.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL. Record of Year's Work.</p>
        <p>A feature of the gatherings of the National Free Church Council next week will be the presentation of Federation report by the Rev. Thomas Law on Tuesday afternoon. This report makes a bulky volume of some 150 pages of closely printed matter, and records a year of strenuous activity on the part of that organization. Notwithstanding the number of Councils which are already dotted over the country, we are told that fifty- live new Councils have been formed during the past year, making a total of 882. In addition, these Councils are grouped into fifty-two federations and a coloured map is given with the report _showing the area these federations cover. _ .</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly one of the most prominent features of the past year's work has been the activity on the Edu- cation question. Carefully arranged plans have been formulated for contesting the next election in the interests of education, and many cases are cited where the National Council has had to fight the administration of the Act. . . .</p>
        <p>Turning in a different direction, we find that the spiritual side of the Council's operations has been well marked. One of the leading features in this department has been the remarkable mission of Gipsy Smith in South Africa, and testimonies aregiven from the Councils out there showing the great value of this tour. Missions conducted in the home country have been no less suc- cessful, as the reports show. Dealing with " the great revival," the report gives the action of Free Church Councils in connection with the Welsh awakening, and demonstrates that the revival is already spreading to England. The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., president of the federa- tion, has undertaken his duties with such extraordinary activity that a special section of the report has had to to be devoted to his services. Mr. Meyer undertook to come in contact with the whole organization of the Free Church Councils, for this purpose visiting the District Federations in turn. A series of meetings was held at each centre, one of the remarkable features being the cross-examination to which Mr. Meyer put the local officials as to the efficiency of their work. Free Church Councils seem to touch the life of the nation at every point. We find them intervening at Brewster Sessions, protesting against the desecration of the Sabbath, appointing Chaplains in workhouses and asylums, taking measures for the preservation of the Sabbath, disseminating Free Church principles, etc. There seem to be few branches of services from which they can be kept out. The new developments of the year include the For- mation of the Young Free Churchmen's Auxiliary, a body started to educate young men in the principles of Nonconformity, and introduce them to the work of aid- ing the Councils. The Council has also formulated a Social Questions Scheme, and examples are en of the varied action of the Free Church Councilsin the pur- suit of a Social Redemptive Campaign. Summer _pur- suit at popular seaside resorts also form another feature established during the year. The Free Church Girls' Guild which has been in existence four years for the helping of the girl life of our oountry presents a most successful report, showing that sixteen permanent homes have been established in different parts of the country, and that the membership now attains to over thirteen thousand. The Council, too, has its Publication Department— which during the year has issued quite a remarkable list of successful books—a Circulating Library, a department for giving logical advice, etc. Tha report of Federa- tion Abroad " is even longer this year than last, and shows that the Federation principle is being adopted all over the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WIDNES AND RUNCORN TRANSPORTER BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES AND RUNCORN TRANSPORTER BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>At the ninth general meeting of the shareholders on Monday the Chairman, Sir John Brunner, said the by- laws had been completed and approved by the Board of Trade. The directors hoped to have the bridge open for public traffic in six weeks' time. The date fixed in the contract for the completion of the work had long gone by, and the directors would have a claim to make against the contractors which, he hoped, would not in- volve more than negotiation. The directors had occa- sionally thought of getting a member of the Royal Family to open the bridge, but, loyal as they were, they had not thought fit to do so. The bridge was a friendly junction between Lancashire and Cheshire, and he thought that the chairman of the Lancashire County Council and the Chairman of the Cheshire County Council should be invited to perform the ceremony on their respective sides of the river. The directors would welcome a public expression of opinion on the matter The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 4, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</p>
        <p>6:21, 1 .</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>XXVII.</p>
        <p>Screw and Reverse Rotation.</p>
        <p>1 Before any player can become even moderately proficient at billiards he must obtain a pretty I fair mastery of screw, for a break seldom reaches any respectable dimensions without the, inclusion; of screw strokes of some kind or another.\ Or- dinary players though they may pot a ball or t, play half-ball in-offs fairly well are generally very weak at screw shots, so that some considerable practice of these strokes is very essential if they are desirous of improving their game to any extent. I Not only aee ordinary players as a rule very I poor at screw strokes, but in many cases (and this often applies to Pretty good players as well) ! they do not even understand the theory of screw. , "An ounce of practioe is worth a pound of , theory" is a common quotation, but it is a poor one if it seeks to discredit the value of theory. It is true thatlno one can become proficient at ! billiards by much theory and little practice; but practice plus theory must always be worth more than practice alone. I Many players may perhaps at first find it diffi- cult to believe that in all screw shots the cue, ball never rolls forward before hitting the object ball, but a little 'reflection will convince them that this must be so, for if forward rotation be developed in the cup-ball before it meets the . object ball no screw can possibly take place. The essence of a screw stroke is reverse rotation; thus in all screws the cue-ball rotates in the opposite direction to that in which it travels; or, in other words, although the cue-ball travel* away from the striker it rotates towards him. It may be asked how can a ball which is rotating towards the striker travel forward and away from him. The answer is—that the force of the blow sends the ball forward, but that it does not roll forward at all, but slides or drags along—the cloth. Most people are familiar with the pretty trick with a hoop which consists in throwing the hoop forward in such a way that it returns to the thrower. This is done by the thrower giving the hoop a sharp donwward twist towards him- self as he throws it forward. The twist causes the hoop to rotate rapidly towards the thrower, so that although when it touches the ground it slides forward a short distance, it rushes back to him as soon as the ' force of the throw ;s exhausted. A similar experiment may be made on a dining table. If a serviette ring be forced forward by the downward pressure of a finger it will, after going forward a considerable distance, rush back again. The reverse rotation can be made still more apparent by using the first finger of each hand, for by using these two fingers more rdownward pressure can. be applied to the ring. This example of reverse rotation is perhaps a better one than that of the hoop trick, for whereas the outward journey of the hoop is mostly in the air, that of the ring is on the surface of the table. Of course, not any- thing like as much reverse rotation can be im- parted to a billiard ball with a cue as to a ser- viette ring with one's finger. The reverse rota- tion quickly overcomes the forwaretmotion of the ringPand brings the ring back without its haying come into collision with anything. A ball struck by a horizontally held cue can never come back to the striker except after hitting another ball. If, however, a ball be struck by a vertically held cue it can be made to go for- ward and return without haying come into con- tact with another ball. Such a stroke is, how- ever, a difficult one. If instead of a cue the hand be used it is quite easy to give a ball very powerful reverse rotation. All that is neces- sary is a sharp downward blow, using the side of the hand, and. catching the ball right on the top, but some little distance from the middle. In this way a ball can be made to rush back after having travelled forward. As most players are. aware, it is possible to throw a ball some distance up the table (though • this does the cloth no good) and cause it to re- turn to thrower by reason of the spia (in this ease reverse rotation) imparted to the ball at the moment of throwing. Some few, by long practice, are able to throw a ball from the , baulk end of the table right round the red ball •on the spot and bring the ball back into baulk without its having touched a cushion. This is an extreme case of reverse rotation as applied by the fingers. ! Compared with"the examples given, the amount of reverse rotation that can be imparted to a cue ball by a horizontally held cue is compara- tively slight, but when playing to screw back from a ball which is close at hand the full im- pact takes so much momentum out of the cue ball that the reverse rotation comes into play, and brings the ball back. As already explained. whenever a ball is tra- velling forward, and is at the same time rotating towards the striker it is sliding or dragging along the cloth. This dragging naturally means considerable friction, aid this friction quickly checks and ultimately „destroys reverse rotation. . This is the reason why, although it is quite an easy matter to a moderately good player to screw back when not more than a foot or so from an object ball, compara- tively few players can screw straight , back from a ball- five feet away, and not one player in ten thousand from a ball nine feet : away. (Later on I will refer to the possibility ' of screwing from the D back into baulk from the red on the spot.) Screwing sideways at as angle is, however, very different from screwing straight or nearly straight back, and the effect of screw when an object ball is taken less than full is at once apparent even :bough the cue ball may be only very slightly under the influence of reverse rotation at the moment of contact. That an amount of reverse rotation which would be quite useless to effect a straight -new back is quite sufficient for pretty big F," 'iv at an ang4 is clearly proved by the stroke •kr awn art " screwing in off the red." Thousaads of or- dinary players have occasionally ,made this stroke and often have even over-screwed it, yet ! it is safe to say that no ordinary player could I screw back six inches in a straight line playing from the D on to the red on the spot, even though he attempted the stroke a thousand times. I In a stroke like screwing into the corner pocket off the red on the spot, the reverse rotation ins , only to alter the natural angle of deflection. In a straight screw-back the reverse rotation has , to arrest jive cue ball at the moment of impact with the:object ball, and then to bring it back. i The well-known stab shot is a stroke wherein , the reverse rotation whilst sufficient to arrest the cue ball at the moment of contact has not been sufficient to cause it to recoil or at least not to any appreciable. extent. Stab pots as far as regards causing the cue ball to stop dead, present r o difficulty to fair players, for when the red ball is near at hand all that is required is just a little less strength than is required to screw back, and when a long way off, all that is required is high pace combined with low hitting. (To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of the Game." 100 illustrations. By RISC) LEVI. Is Dow ready, is. net.</head>
        <p>Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of the Game." 100 illustrations. By RISC) LEVI. Is Dow ready, is. net.</p>
        <p>! Mr. Ris,/ Levi will be pleased to answer in these ; column 9 all questions relative to, the, article appearing weekly, and as far as pcssible any ! questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards. Envelope's should be marked " Billiards."</p>
        <p>DR. DALLINGRR AT RUNCORN • At St. Paul's Wesleyan Church on Sunday, the Rev. Dr. Dellinger. the well-known theologian and scientist, preached to large congrecations. On Monday evening Dr. Dellinger delivered his popular lecture on " Ants" in the Public Hall. There was a large audience. Sir John T. Brunner, M.P., presided. He said he had been in that hall before, but he thought never onapleasanter occasion. He looked forward with very great pleasure to hearing Mr. Dallinger. He had heard him in times gone by, when his evenings were not so fully occupied. It was a great inducement, therefore, to him when he was invited to come there and for once to bare an even- ing's holiday. Dr. Dellinger was a great authority upon this subject and was very well acquainted with all that had been written by others upon the subject. The life of ants was made intensely interesting to them and he hoped Dr. Dellinger would forgive hiln for saying so, sometimes very amusing. He was told that these small creatures sometimes kept slaves, and he hoped Dr. Dellinger would tell them whether they were black or yellow, for he (Sir John) did not know. (Laughter.) Dr. Dellinger then proceeded with his lecture, which ace instructive and entertaining.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Monthly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednes- day when there were present Messrs. Halton (presiding) Forshaw, Thompson, blather, Ball, Backhouse, Longton, Houghton, S. Sutton (Assistant clerk), B.S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), F. W. Kerfoot, inspector,,and Dr. Sephton(medical officer).</p>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY TO PEAR TREE FARM. The COUNCIL having served upon the corporation a notice to supply their tenants of Pear Tree Farm with water, the clerk reported that be had received the fol- lowing letter from the Town Clerk :—Herewith I send memorial of the Corporation objecting to the require- ments of your council with regard to the water supply to Pear Tree Farm. The memorial states; (1) Your memorialists are the owners of a farm known as the Pear Tree Farm at Houghton Green, and they have by two notices been required to provide a supply of water for such house '• " (2) your memorialises object to , such requirements on the following grounds :—(a) that it se impracticable to provide a supply at a reasonable I cost; (b) that the local authority ought themselves to provide a supply of water for the district in which such house is situated; (c) that the whole or part of the ' expenses of providing the supply ought to be a charge on the district in which such house is situated. In the absence of Mr. Knowles, the representative for Houghton Green, the matter was adjourned until the , next meeting of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CROFT WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>CROFT WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Hunt, clerk to the Croft Parish Council wrote :—The Parish Council meeting was held last Mon- day evening. The Chairman considers it a very serious charge against the officials of the Corporation. Mr. Ball promised to see Mr. Dees respecting the way he has been iguagirg the wells, and also the tests he returned. Mr. HALL stated he had seen Mr. n,tlS who had promised to enquire into the matter. THE HEALTH Or THE DIST r• ( T. . .</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHTON reported that during ti.e month there had been 28 births and 38 deaths. TI e district was in a very healthy state being entirely free from infectious disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ESTIMATES FOR THE ITALF-YEAR. _ . .</head>
        <p>ESTIMATES FOR THE ITALF-YEAR. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. SurroN stated that the full estimates of expendi- ture for the coming half-year would be laid before the next meeting of the Council. The rate for general ex- penses would be ss. in the £ a reduction of id. as com- pared with the last half-year. With reference to special expenses in the township which supplied their own water. gas, Scc. he proposed to call the assistant over- seers of each township into consultation and go into the whole matter. That would be more satisfactory to both parties as in the past when precepts had been issued enquiries had been made and no one appeared to be satisfied. When however, the assistant overseers were fully cognisant with the amount of the rate before the rate was levied they would be able to satisfy their respective Council. Mr. MATHER said that when he read the report of Mr. Newcombe asking the County Council for a great deal more money than they had hitherto given he thought they might have a still further reduction in the general rate. it. seemed impossible they should have the same length of roads in their district when they had got so much under the county and secondary roads. They were very pleased to get the money from the County Council, bat he thought they should economise with regard to local roads. Mr. SurroN explained that the Surveyor was still going in largely for road improvements. The general rate land been reduced by Id. in the £ and he could almost promise that that reduction u Quid be continued throughout the second half year. Of course if they could see their way to further reduce the rate they would do so. Mr. MATHER: I was going to ask Mr. Newcombe if he could not reduce his estimates. Considering the high rates we have to pay a reduction, however little is acceptable. Mr. SurroN explained that the money received from the County Council had to be expended on County roads. Mr. NEWCOMBE explained that the highway rates were gradually lowering. They received relief from the County on the maintenance of all scheduled secondary roads which relieVed a certain amount of money which they would otherwise have to expend on secondary roads.</p>
        <p>. . Mr. FORSHAW remarked that in Burtonwood Mr. Newcombe was as economical as anyone could be. Mr. THOMPSON That applies particularly to my district. "AS THICK AS TREACLE AND AS BL tCK AS COAL." Mr. KIIRPOOT reported he had received a letter from Mr. William Fairclough tomplaining of the foul state of Phipps Brook, Burtonwood. Mr. FoßsiLivr remarked that the brook was as thick as treacle and as black as coal. . . . .</p>
        <p>The Inspector was instructed to inspect the brook and report at the next meeting of the Council.</p>
        <p>COUNTY ESTIMATES. The SURVEYOR reported :—" Since your last meeting I I have prepared estimates etc., for main roads mainten- ance and main roads improvements. I have thought it advisable to increase the estimates for maintenance grant from £1,596 to £2,024 2s. 6d., and although we could hardly hope to receive that amount, at tne same time, Pat least expect to have the suggested macadam increase of £2OO made permanent, thereby releasing that amount from the ordinary main road expenditure. The increased estimate is also with a view to impressing upon the County Authority the growing traffic and importance of our district. The application for ilmprovement grahts includes estimates for flagging path by gadgate Wesleyan Chapel, flagging path by Padgate Church, completion of Penketh-lane End Improvement, and a proposal to strengthen Manchester-road crown right through. The grant tovArds the cost of Secondary Road maintenance will be the tame as last year £Bl6. For im- provements, I have applied for grants for forming and raising Clay Lane Burtonwood by Catholic schools, forming and raising Myddleton-lane Winwick, maca- damising and widening Delph-lane, Houghton. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
