      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENT. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matters intended for publication must he written on side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not ater than Thursday.</p>
        <p>" Junior Conservative " Meetings. Sir,—The Junior Conservatives evidently like to listen to misrepresentation. Certainly the ebullitions of Mr. E. Draper and Mr. John White were full of anything but facts. The latter gentleman said "That the war had cost over £200,000,000 but they (evidently meaning the bulk of the nation) had secured not only peace but two prosperous and abundant colonies." Now Sir, the men who have secured two prosperous colonies are the diamond magnates from the Rand. The British working man has not beneated one brass farthing from the miserable transaction. The present state of trade is due to the reckless expenditure of an unstatemanlike government and no doubt the British working man will show his utter dislike by giving them the go-bye at the proper time.—Yours,</p>
        <p>A. BRITISHER.</p>
        <p>Justices' Jastice for Anti-Vaccinators. Sir,—l read that. at Birkdale the application of a con- scientious objector who sought to avail himself of the loophole of escape for his child provided by our legis- lators iu IDS was refused, although ho was able to advance such exceptional reasons in support of his claim as ought to have easily satisfied the magistrates. A short time ago I directed the notice of the late Chas. H. Hopwood, Esq., R.C., who up to the time of his recent much-lamented death was Recorder of Liver- pool, to a similar case, and the opinions of such an eminent legal authority may not only prove of interest to your readers but should also serve for the guidance of the justices in question when they are again called upon to adjudicate under like circumstances. An unrnis- takable prououncement upon the same subject, too, was uttered by the Lord Chief Justice at Birmingham Assizes and a copy of this speech has been sent from the Home Office to the Clerk of every Bench throughout the country. My advice to anyone who fails to secure a certificate is : Lay a statement of the facts before your M.P., and the Home Secretary. The conscience clause should not be reduced to an absurdity through the caprice of some members of the great unpaid.— Very truly yours,</p>
        <p>JOHN H. BONNER.</p>
        <p>Leicester, February 20th, 1905.</p>
        <p>The Independent Labour Party and the Elective Auditorship. Sir,—Many peuple have noticed with surprise and regret that Mr. James Ball did not stand for the Elec- tive Anditorship this year. His success as the nominee of the local branch of the Independent Libour Party last year.—both at the poll and in performance of the duties of his office—naturally led many to expect his re-election when the time came, and in order to satisfy frequent inquiries my Committee has instructed me to ask you to insert the following explanation in your next issue. Mr. Ball was asked to contest the election, but he intimated to us that he had recently obtained a new situation in Liverpool, and the duties required very careful attention. He regretted that he could not spare time this year to act as Elective Auditor if elected, but said that if opportunity allowed be would be pleased to stand for the office on some future occasion as la "Labour " candidate. My committee regretfully accepted his decision in the matter, but were glad to find he would perhaps be available on some other occasion as he still retains membership of the local Independent Labour Party and remains resident in Warrington.—Yours truly, E. LEES, Secretary.</p>
        <p>30, Oldham-street, Latchford, March 2ad, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Imports and Exports.—An Object Lesson.</head>
        <p>Imports and Exports.—An Object Lesson.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a previous letter I spoke of the Fiscal ques- tion having been before the country for nearly one year and nine months, but the idea of hitting back when they fancy they are hit, appeals so strongly to the pugnacity of Britishers that there is considerable danger of the fancied thing being mistaken for the real thing. In order, therefore, to avoid being taken in by false appear- ances, it is necessary to take more than a mere passing glance at the question, and in doing so it will be found, whether considered as an abstract principle or in its bearing on the interests of this country. Free Trade is not only of great benefit to us, but is also essential to our prosperity. We have been told that all that comes into this country in excess of the value of what we send out, is paid for in golden sovereigns, and to that extent the wealth of the country is being reduced. In other words, that money is wealth, and that without money no wealth could exist. A very little enquiry will show this to be an error, but it is a very plausible one, and very common. Money performs a definite and important function in all civilized states.' It may not be wealth but the mere symbol of it, as a measure of value and the medium by which exchanges are effected. States can exist and be wealthy without it, but cannot exist with it alone. These are mere truisms which only need stating to carry conviction to the wind. Man in a primeval state would make everything he must have for himself. His needs wottld therefore be few indeed. Such a thing as a luxury would be unknown. But he would very soon .find that he could produce several things of one kind both from aptitude and natural facilities, much better than he could produce odd things of several kinds, and so he would confine himself to the production of that which nature had endowed him with the greatest apiti- tude and natural facilities for. Another man for the same reasons would produce something else, and a desire to exchange one for the other would be con- ceived. But observe there is no money misses from one to the other here. This has not yet come into exist- ence. An exchange of wealth has taken place, but by simple barter, no man would exchange with man and tribe with tribe. As time passed, on, this would be thought a very crude and clumsy method and instead of .exchanging one thing for the ether in this way, a medium would be agreed upon by which the various articles wo Id be measured in value. Coins of the precious metals have been found to be most adaptable to this purpose and so if we take the relative value of a pair of boots to a coat, we shall arrive at a simple example of the function that money performs in the matter of exchange. If therefore one pair of boots is worth Ills. and one coat is worth 20s. it is equiva- lent to saying that one coat isworth two pairs of boots. In its simplest form, what would take place where money was made the medium of exchange would be, that the man who had the coat would exchange it for 265., but as he only wanted one pair of boots for which he would have to pay 10s., he would still have 10s. left to buy other requirements, and it would depend on the exchange value of those requirements, as to how far his money would go. If all articles were to be got at their natural competitive price that is, without being enhanced by taxes, his 10s. would obtain for him a great deal more than if each article was increased in price by a tax or other means. Wealth therefore, is anything which has an exchange value, and money can only be wealth when it fulfills this condition. A person might have it in abundance, but if he could not get anything in exchange for it, he would be in a sorry plight, for it would neither clothe nor feed him, and though he had plenty of it, he would soon die of starvation. Money therefore is a medium of exchange,and is only of value in that respect, in the country in which it circulates. Only enough is coined to meet the needs of circulation, and anything above that is superfluous, and it is not sent from one country to another, because it would be useless for the purpose for which it was made. It follows then, that excess of in the value of Imports over Exports is not paid from money.—Yours faithfully.</p>
        <p>A. RATHBONE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Mount, Grappenhall. Councillor Stott and the Next Election.</head>
        <p>The Mount, Grappenhall. Councillor Stott and the Next Election.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—ln your report of the Progressive Labour League's Demonstration which was held last week in the Co-operative Hall, I find that two of the speakers had referred to some statements made by me at a Labour meeting held in Brick street school, regarding my attitude at the next election. With your kind permis- sion, I will place before your readers what was meant by those statements. i‘ly position, which I believe is also the position of the Independent Labour Party, is that if there is no Labour ca42Alidate in lVarringtaa at the general election shall have no interest in the election as far as actzta 'work is soricerned, and intend to devote all the time I/ possibly can to try and secure the return of Mr. J. A. *dden for Newton Division. As far as my vote and the result of the election are concerned I shall be very much interested. Being so keenly interested in national politics as I am it would be impossible for me to be otherwise than interested. In stating what my attitude will be, and also In appealing to those who believe in Independent Labour Representation to work for Mr. Seddon I am only acting consistently with my attitude, and policy, of the past ten years. The aims of the Independent Labour Party iire to build up a Labour Party both outside, and inside the House of Commons, Which shall be independent of either of the two great political parties. In a constituency almost at my own door, a candidate has been put for- ward who, for many years, has been a close friend of mine. He is well known as a man who has rendered valuableservices for the labour cause, and has consis- tently advocated, and worked for, the building up of a i Labour party. Furthermore, he s contesting as a labour candidate, under the auspices of the Labour Representation Committee. He is a man of exceptional ability, and is opposing one of the most bitter opponents of trades unionism and labour that sits in the present House of Commons, I further recognize, that if Mr. Seddon is returned for the Newton cOnStitileheYl he</p>
        <p>will represent the workers of Warrington,quite as much as the working classes of the Newton Division. I there- fore consider it my duty to do all I possibly can to secure his return. Who is there, that desires to see Labour strongly represented in the House of Commons, that can conscientiously say I am neglecting my duty 1 by taking this course? I can frankly endorse Mr. Cosfield's statement that "he had made no frantic efforts to capture the Socialist vote." If I may offer an opinion I would say that hehad attempted, quite unintentionally no doubt, to alienate the Socialists' vote by the insult he offered to them in a letter to the press in August last year, when he referred to the Independent Labour Party as "a few irrecon- cilables." The Rev. H. Dunnico, at the Progressive Labour League Demonstration, appealed to the members of the Independent Labour Party to support Mr. Crosfield at the next election, he, Mr. Dunnico, evidently having forgotten the bitter attacks made by himself and his friends on the Labour Party. Ido not refer to this in any bitter or vindictive spirit, but mention the fact thet theseattacks were very ill-advised on their part. However, if Mr. Dunnico has seen the error of his ways, and has now arrived at a more reasonable frame of mind, he may become a useful member of the Liberal Party. The members of the I.L.P. have become accustomed to these attacks, having been subject to them since the formation of the Party. We, however, have too much faith in the righteousness of our cause to be influenced to any great extent either by vilification or flattery. In reading the report of Mr. Dunnico's address an impression may have been gained that, in my remarks at the Brick-street meeting, I had advised abstaining ' from voting, or to vote against Mr. Crosfield. No mention of votes, or voting, was made either by myself or any other speaker at that meet- ing. It is not my intention to advise any members of the Independent Labour Party or Labour Representation Committee how to use their votes. I consider that the men belonging to those organizations are sufficiently conversant with the great political questions of the day,and have sufficient intelli- gence to use their votes to the best advantage to them- selves and their class, without requiring advice from myself or any other member of the Independent Labour Party. In conclusion, let me sly that the election of a Labour candidate is of far more importance to us than the return of either Liberals or Tories, for only through a Labour Party will the desires and aspirations of the workers be voiced in the House of Commons.—Yours faithfully FRED STOTT. 160, Battersby-lane.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield and His Critics Mr. A. H. Crosfield has addressed the following letter to the editor of the "Observer " : Sir,—There is something delightfully naive in "Observer's " method of closing a controversy. He will graciously excuse me from defending Mr. Lloyd- George any further, and he will not " bear heavily " upon me, if I drop the subject and go on instead to answer certain questions which he is pleased to set me, 'this is certainly quite a skilful way of begging for the last word. It is most magnanimous of" Observer" to promise to let me off easily if I will refrain from reply- ing to him. But I have less objection than he seems to have to continue the controversy, and at the risk of seeming disagreeable I must really ask permission to expose some of the fallacies and misapprehensions which he begs me to leave alone. Tam glad that" Observer" has misedthe point which he thinks he makes so triumphantly against me as to the balance of our imports and exports to and from America. He says that the fact that while we " buy " from America 119 million pounds worth we only sell some 20 millions proves that our buying from foreign countries does not make them buy from us. This sounds plausible, but only to a mind ignorant of the A B C of economics. International trade cannot be compared to the process of going into a shop and buying a pound of tea and putting two shillings on the counter. It is really a process of barter. Hardly any money passes. How then is it possible that we take 119 millions and only send 20 millions in exchange? In two ways : • 1. America owes us money. She is anewcountry and like all new countries has been developed mainly by means of the capital of the older European countries. The parent land has set up the • younger son in business. To take only one instance, the railways of the States were built very largely with British capital. Now on all this money intere i lt has to be paid. But it is not paid n gold. It is paid in kind. A very large part of these imports of ours from America represents Imerica's annual debt to us. We draw our dividends in the shape of wheat and raw cotton. 2. The process of international barter is seldom made direct. It is more often what is called " tri- angular." The carpenter made a table for the butcher,but the butcher happens to be a vegetarian. So they arrange between them that the butcher shall supply the tailor with a quarter's meat while the tailor makes a suit for the carpenter. So between nations. We take imports from America but instead of exporting as much in return, we send our wares to France which in turn exports their value in silks and Paris genres to the United States. (I give this illustration for simplicity's sake—what really happens is much more complex and involves not three nations but the whole world.) There need be no balance of imports and exports as between one country and another. The balance works out only when you take a view of the whole civilised world, and then you have to remember that we export services (e.g. shipping and banking) and capital lent out at interest as well as manu- factured goods. There is some satisfaction in dealing with such a point as this for it helps to clear up the misapprehen- sions which lie at the basis of the whole protectionist case. But really I must be excused from dwelling at length on all the trivialities of " Obsermr's " reply to me. I said that Mr. Lloyd-George was "chaffing "Mr. Chamberlain, and forthwith " Observer " claims that I have admitted that Mr. Lloyd-George is "never to be taken seriously." I suppose "Observer " claims to be a serious politician. Are we to infer that he has never made a joke in his life? If a devotion to Tariff Reform entails that awful penalty, Heaven preserve us from it! I must also waste a little ink and paper in exposing a little trick which "Observer" tries to play upon me in the matter of the statistics of th&amp;cotton trade. I gave the comparative figures for 1903 and 1904 to support Mr. Lloyd George's contention that our exports despite Mr. Chamberlain's prophecies had risen during the past rear. He replies by substituting the figures for 1902 and 1904. This is shifting the whole question and raising a false issue. Nobody denied that as a result of the cotton corner raw cotton was dear in 1902. What 1 said was that the increase in our imports for 1904 as compared with 1903 was an increase in bulk and weight and not merely in value. We sent out mach more cotton counted by pieces and cwts than in the previous year. This "Observer "cannot and does not dispute, despite his rash assertions to the contrary in his first series of notes. One word about Canadian Preference and I am done with this half of our controversy. " Observer " is so delighted because a single figure went wrong in Mr. Lloyd George's speech that he ignores the whole point of the argument. It is true that our trade with Canada has risen since preference by some five millions. He is quite sure this is because of preference. But in the same period and without any preference at all the trade of the United States with Canada has risen by ten millions. What is the inference? Simply that there has been a wave of prosperity in Canada. She has been buying more largely than before and we have benefited just as America has done. Preference has probably helped us a little but we should have shared in this sub- stantial advance without any preference at all. I now come to the questions " Observer" asks me. It is somewhat unusual to attempt to vindicate the heads of your opponent's sermon but lam quite ready to face " plain questions "and While I have little hope Ithat "Observer" will be pleased with ray answers I have no fear of the verdict of Warrington. Questions (1) and (2) can best be answered together. "Observer "asks in effect whether I would approve of retaliation against a foreign country which drew up a tariff deliberately designed to damage British in- dustries, particularly if these industries happened to he situated in Warrington. Well, to begin with, it is worth while to point out that such a, case has not yet arisen. No foreign country draws up tariffs specially ngainst Great Britain. On the contrary we enjoy all over the world what is called "most favoured nation treatment." We may not like the American tariff but It certainly springs from no hatred towards England. It is the same for us as for all thelrest of the world. Protectionist Germany gets no better treatment in America than Free Trade England. Why should we suppose that by adopting a tariff of our own we could wring concessions which no Protectionist country is able to obtain for itself? Whatever advantage any European power may obtain for itself by means of a tariff war in a third market is at once extended to us by virtue of our " most favoured nation " treaties. If on the other hand we began retaliating we should at once expose ourselves to direct and special acts of hostility, and in the course of the conflict we should certainly stiffer ourselves even if we also managed to inflict a good deal of injury upon our opponent. Take for examplethe case of the present Sugar Convention. We have ex- cluded Russian sugar from our markets for the odd reagon that it is too cheap. Partly as the result of this sugar has doubled in price. We lose some £7,000,000 a year while the West Indian planters gain a bare £300.000, and lf am not at all sure that this estimate of our National loss is not considerably under the mark, but I take the figives from the "Westminster Gazette," which I believe to be a most reliable paper. And finally in the confectionery trade in London alone 12,000 work- men have been discharged and 50.000 put on half time. (I may say in passing that if "Observer" is spoiling for a fight he might very well set himself to answer the letter of January 25th, in whith Mr. Parker examined Mr. Pierpoint's defence of the Sugar Convention. By the way, I note with amusement that Mr. Pierpoint fights shy of this letter from Mr. Parker. Will not "Observer" come gallantly to the rescue?) Here is a pretty result of retaliating! But even this is not all. IRussia has not taken our attack on her "dumped" sugar "lying down." She replies with a "big revolver" by putting a prohibitive duty on Indian tea. If the West</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 4, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>Indies gain over sugar the East Indies lose over tea. It is true no doubt as Mr. Brodrick said the other day that Vladivostock is still a free port. If Indian tea can force the ice and run the Japanese blockade and make its way across Siberia it may still enter Russia! I am afraid this will seem cold comfort in Assam.</p>
        <p>In principle we see no objection to retaliation pro- vided that it is likely to succeed and that it will not I hurt us in the process. Adam Smith himself went as I , far as this. But let me remind " Observer " that he is ! harking back to an old policy which his own party tried 1' in the past and discarded as disastrousAVe did plenty of retaliating in the forties, and the "big revolver "was in constant use. It only resulted in checking our export trade and adding to the cost of everything at home. It may interest '• Observer" to hear how Sir Robert Peel summed up the morals of these efforts : " Wearied with our long and unavailing efforts to enter into satis- factory commercial treaties with other countries, we have resolved at length to consult our own interests and not ta punish other countries for the wrong they do us in continuing theirhigh tariffs upon the importation of our products and manufactures, by continuing high duties ourselves." I doubt if Mr. Balfour could do more execution with the retaliation revolver than Sir Robert Peel. His first efforts in the sugar business show that he has armed himself with a weapon that recoils I suppose "Observer" will reply that conditions have changed since 1845. Let him study the official report on the long tariff war between France and Italy (Cd 1938 Commercial No. 1 1901). He will find that this essay at retaliation cost the two countries something like 120,000,000 pounds. When at last they made peace they found that their trade had sought other channels and still stands at just half what it was before they entered • on the game of tit for tat. France and Italy have more experience of tariffs than we have. This Government of muddlers would not be likely to handle them more skilfully. I will just add in conclusion on this matter that the onus of proof for retialiation must always rest with those who propose it, and a mighty strong case it will have to be before we listen to it. Nor will I for one ever touch retaliation when it is really introduced as the thin end of the wedge to lead to protection— precisely the use that would be made of it by the gentlemen who have joined the Tariff Reform League. Do I advocate a return to Sugar Bounties? No. It lies with the liounty giving powers to decide that question for themselves, but so long as they are willing to make us a present of several millions sterling a year for the benefit of our people at large and important industries into the bargain, it seems to me little short of idiocy to refuse to take the money; and further I protest most strongly against the closing of our markets to Russian and Argentine sugar, a measure which has allowed the French and German cartels to form a " combine" and so to raise the price of sugar in our markets. With free competition this could not have happened. Would I forbid British Ministers to discuss Preferen- tial trade at the next Colonial Conference? I would do nothin.,,,,, which might seem discourteous to the Colonies. If at the next Conference they should make the famous " offer " I would of course discuss it. But I would not bring Colonial Ministers here on a fool's errand to any special conference without making it quite clear in advance (what indeed the next election will make abundantly clear) that this country is not • prepared to gumn for Preference if it involves the taxa- tion of food. Mr. Balfour himself has said as much and it wonld not benews to the Colonies, who recognise ' as Sir George Reid, the Premier of the Australian Commonwealth has put it, that our attitude is perfectly natural au,l reasonable. Bat I contest the whole assump- tion on which the Tariff Reform movement is founded. Trade is not our only link with the Colonies, and it is not by Preferential tariffs that we shall be best able to strengthen the ties, the healthy ties, that bind and con- solidate the British Empire.—Yours Szc., A, H. CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MR. HARRY BERREY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MR. HARRY BERREY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The Apollo Prize Choir, re-organized to some extent made a welcome re-appearance at a concert held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday evening. Such organizations serve a distinctly useful purpose in local musical life, and we are pleased the members are once more taking up the work with commendable spirit. Mr. Harry Berrey, the able conductor and voice-trainer to the choir, was responsible for the arrangement of the concert, which reflected credit upon him. Although doubtless owing to the miserable weather, the attend- ance was disappointing, the display of interest in the items made up in some measure for hack of numbers, and the efforts of the artistes called forth the warmest praise. The choir, of course, figured prominently in an agreeably varied programme. They sang in a manner which evidenced careful practice, the voices being nicely blended. The chief work attempted was "Martyrs of the Arena " and although there was hardly the crispness of attack and delicacy of expression which the chorus calls for, the rendering was effective and elicited high approbation. The quartette was entrusted to Messrs. Radcliffe, Clayton, Gittens and Nurse whe quite justified their selection. Other choral items were "Annie Laurie," " Lovely maiden " and "The young musicians." The sprightly music of the last-mentioned number proved especially popular, the quartette party this time consisting of Messrs. R Cook, W. Critchley, K. Walker, and W. Wood- cock. Mr. Harry Berrey was at his accustomed post of conductor and is to be congratulated on the way in which he piloted the choir through the intricacies of the various selections. A special measure of praise must be accorded the Lyric Prize Quartette party— Miss Woodland, Miss Fairhurst, Mr. R. Cook and Mr. Percy George—whose singing was one of the most meritorious features of the evening. " Killarney " was given with such excellent taste as to call forth an encore, and the party responded with another old favourite "The Shamrock." Later they submitted "Moonlight and Music "—the test piece at the Warrington Eisteddfod when the party carried off the prize—in equally pleasing style. There was quite anarray of soloists and, almost without exception, they acquitted themselves well. Mr. Charles Rigby "The was in splendid voice, and "The Curfew" and The Toreador" afforded him good scope for the display of his powers. He is to be com- plimented upon doing fall justice to each item. Miss Lily Fairhurst the possessor of a rich and powerful contralto voice, delighted the audience with an artistic interpretation of the difficult song The Enchantress." Mr. John Willett, although suffering from a cold, sang " Good-byo " creditably. Miss Waterworth, of Runcorn, rendered "The Moorish Maid" with taste and expression, her soprano voice being of good compass and flexibility. Later she was associated with Mr. Rigby in the duet "Of fairy wand" ' from "Maritana." Miss Carrie Roscoe gave an adequate interpretation of "Love smiles but to deceive," while Miss Cissy Rimmer earned high encomiums for her delicate and feeling treatment of the rather long number "The Raft." Miss Jennie Jones contributed "Sing sweet bird" with acceptable piquancy. Mrs. Summer's sweet interpretation of "Down the vale" was marred by the nervousness !attendant upon a first appearance in public. Other very acceptable items of the entertainment were humorous duets by Messrs. Harry Berrey and W. Milne which were given N ith fine spirit, trio 'The jolly gipsies " by Messrs. Berrey, Radcliffe and Rigby, and the duet "Pray teach me your language to learn." by Miss Jones and Mr. Milne. Humorous songs by Mr. Milne formed a popular diver- sion and created no little merriment. Mrs. Berrey supplied the pianoforte accompaniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LIBERAL ORGANIZATION.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL ORGANIZATION.</p>
        <p>Series of Ward Meetings.</p>
        <p>During the week a series of Liberal Ward meetings have been held, the large attendance at each, going to show the increased interest which is being taken in the cause of Liberalism.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the St. Austin's Ward Liberal Committee on Monday evening Councillor Tinnion was re-elected chairman. The vice-chairmen appointed were Councillor Barton, Messrs. W. Roberts, J. W. Hayward, J. Rymer Young, F. Dixon, J. Critchley, and W. Croskill, Messrs. S. Dunbobbin, W. E. Roberts, R. Atkinson, J. J. Corradine, J. J. Pritchard, and W. Croskill were appointed hon. secretaries. Councillor Tinnion remarked that St. Austin's ward had always done its duty in the past but he wanted the ward to be so much ahead that it would make up for any deficiencies which might possibly occur in other wards. Councillor WILKINSON presided over a meeting of the Orford Ward Liberal Committee which was held in the Silver-street schools on Thursday evening. Councillor A. Peacock was elected chairman. Councillor Wilkinson Messrs. John Hughes and John Upton, being appointed vice-chairmen, Councillor P. Peacock, Messrs. A. H. Sanders and A. Ashton were appointed secretaries. The Whitecross Ward Liberal Committee met in the Thewlis-street Schoolroom on Thursday evening, Councillor James Evans presiding. Mr. Joseph Howes was also present. The officers elected were : Chairman Councillor James Evans ; vice-chairmen, Councillors Brough, and Barton, Messrs. H. Roberts, W. Wright Nathan Cook, E. Osborne, and the Rev. H. Dunnico ; secretaries, Messrs. J. T. Barton and R. W. Cook. Mr. Henry Roberts wrote apoligising for non-attendance. In the unavoidable absence of Councillor Clegg Mr. A. Templeton presided over a meeting of the Howley Ward Liberal Committee which was held at the Liberal Club on Thursday evening. The officers elected were : Chairman. Councillor Clegg ; vice-chairmen, Messrs. J. T. Locker, S. Dunbobbin, and T. J. Bigby ; hon. secretaries, Messrs. A. Downes and W. Vose. The meeting for St. John's Ward was held last even- ing, when Mr. W. Furness was re-elected chairman. Mr. Fish was added to the list of vice-chairmen and Mr. J. Vose was elected general secretary. The Town Hall Ward Committee also met last evening. Mr. P. O'Brien was re-elected chairman and Messrs. E. J. Mitchell and E. A. Sanders were elected secretaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LAUNCH AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>A LAUNCH AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Novel Scene.</p>
        <p>At one time the launching of a coasting vessel at Sankey Bridges would perhaps not have been considered a novel event. For the past twenty-six years, however, the ship-building industry which formerly flourished in the locality has been allowed to almost die out Recently, it has been revived and Thursday witnessed the launching of a large barge built by Messrs. Clare and Ridgway for the United Alkali Com- pany, Widnes, who will use it for river and coasting purposes. For the past eight months workmen have been busily employed constructing the boat in the yard on the Warrington side of the St. Helens and Sankey ' Canal. Mr. T. Cawley, the foreman, superintended the work and to him great credit is also due for having designed the vessel in accordance with specifications prepared by Mr. J. S. Davies, surveyor to the United Alkali Company. The progress of the building has been watched with interest and when the finishing touches were completed the launching was eagerly looked for- ward to by the inhabitants of the 'district. The " Eustace Carey," as the vessel has been christened, is probably the largest craft ever launched at Sankey Bridges. She is 74 feet in length and 194 feet beam,the depth of the hold being 8 feet, 9 inches. She will carry a cargo of 200 tons. The following are particulars of the materials used in the construction of the barge : bottom 2i inches rock elm; plank from bilge to bends 2,1 inch English oak; bends 3 inch English oak ' • bottom ceiling 21: Inch English oak ; stringers 3 inch pitch pine ; inside bilges 4 inch pitch pine ; oak beams main and mast 9 inch sided and 10 inch middle; deck frame 12 inches by 5 inches ; knees 5 inch sided and moulded ; deck ceilings, 5 inches by 4 inches. Sankey Bridges wore quite a holiday aspect on Thurs- day afternoon when the christening and launching of the vessel took place with fitting ceremony. The trams carried quite a large traffic, and at three o'clock there would be over a thousand people gathered on the canal banks and swing bridge. The company present in the ship-building yard included Mr. Brock, chairman of the United Alkali Company, Mrs. Harrison, of Huyton, Mr. Eustace Carey, Mr. Wattleworth, Mr. John Davies, Colonel J. C. Ridgway, Mrs. Ridgway, Miss Ridgway, "".fr. and Mrs. Ernest Ridgway, Captain and Mrs. Avery, Mr. and Mrs. Sloane, Mr. P. J. Frith, Mr. George Thompson and Mr. Fowkes (engineer to the London and North-Western Railway Company). The vessel was gaily bedecked with flags and was boarded by a number of bolder spirits who had determined to experience the sensations of the launch. She was raised into position preparatory to taking the plunge by the driving home of wedges which the workmen hammered at with great energy. The christening was the next incident. Mrs. Harrison, daughter of Mr. Brock (the chairman of the United Alkali Co.) having been presented by Miss Ridg- way with a lovely bouquet performed this ceremony by ' breaking a bottle of wine, suspended by a red,white and blue ribbon, against the ,side of the vessel. The " Eustace Carey " was then released and having slid sideways clown inclined planks took a fine plunge into , the canal, causing quite a shower of spray. The deck rails almost touched the water, the passengers having to hold tightly to ropes to avoid immersion, but in a , moment the vessel righted herself and was punted a short distance down the canal. The whole ceremony , passed off without the slightest hitch and loud cheers were raised.</p>
        <p>THE NEED FOR NATIONALIZATION OF CANALS. .IMPROVEMENT OF WIDNES LOCK SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Afterwards the invited guests- assembled in the offices of the firm where light refreshments were served.</p>
        <p>Colonel R/DGWAY proposed "Success to the Eustace Carey." He observed that the boat had been built for the purpose of trading between Runcorn, Widnes and Liverpool, and hlso an the Doe and to tFleetwood. She had been constructed particularly strong to stand the strain she might have to bear in going down the river when the tides were not very suitable. The work had been done to the specification of Mr. J. S. Davies, surveyor to the United Alkali Com- pany, and he (the speaker) thought he was well satisfied with the way in which his instructions had been carried out. He (Colonel Ridge way) was happy to say that the Company had since given him an order for another boat to a similar specifi- cation, altd he could assure Mr. Brock and Mr. Carey trust the firm would do their best to build the vessel, under Mr. Davies's inspection, so that every necessary to make her strong and suitable for the trade would be forthcoming. He was sorry to say they were rather handicapped at Sankey Bridges 'for building larger vessels than the " Eustace Carey" by the shortness of the locK entering the river at Widnes. Perhaps they were not aware that a vessel of her size carrying 200 tons dead weight, cost no more to work than one carrying only 100 tons. Of course, the canal was in the hands of the London and North-Western Railway Company, and railway companies and canals were perhaps rather antagonistic to each other. Still he thought some canals might be of considerable benefit to the railway companies by bringing the raw materials which would afterwards be sent out by rail when they had been turned into manufactured goods. The engineer of the London and North-Western Railway Company was present that afternoon. He (Colonel Ridgway) did not know whether Mr. F'owkes had any qualms of con- science, as to not having power to lengthen the lock at Widnes but he (the speaker) felt sure that if the company gave him permission to do it he would be only too happy to carry it out. He had noticed that at the meetings of the Associated Chambers of Commerce held this week in London there bad been a strong feeling that the canals should be put in other hands than the railway companies, and a committee was considering the question of nationalization. If nationaliz- ation were accomplished it would possibly be a great improvement, in fact he felt sure it would as far as many canals were concerned. The toast was heartily drunk. Mr. CAREY said he was sure the way in which the vessel had been built did Colonel Ridgway and his staff great credit. He was pleased to hear that the firm had received another order but, do what they could, he did not think they would build a better vessel than the "Eustace Carey" seemed to be. He would ask the company to drink "Success to Colonel Ridgway and his staff, and the ship building industry in this neighbourhood." Colonel Ridgway had alluded to the question of the nationalization of canals. He (Hr. Carey) only wished that could be brought about, because in France and Belgium, he was informed, the canals like our highways were free of tolls and rates and were used just like our high roads. Mr. Carey also alluded to the necessity for an improvement of the lock at Widnes, and said that as the railway company were always enterprising they might perhaps be induced to carry out the work. Mr. BROOK said that as chairman of the United Alkali Company he would be able to report very favourably of the vessel, and he cordially congratulated Colonel Ridg- way and his staff. He wished very much they could build a larger boat for the reasons mentioned by Colonel Ridgway. As to the suggested improvement of the Widnes lock, he hoped Mr. Fowkes would do allhe could to have it both lengthened and widened. It was undoubtedly rather antiquated for present-day traffic and the modern style of vessel, and it must be altered if the , boats were to pay. If in the construction of the second boat Colonel Ridgway exercised the same care in the , selection of materials and workmanship, he ventured to say in advance that his company would be very well satisfied. The toast was honoured with cordiality. 1 Colonel RIDGWAY responded. He could only say that the staff, and particularly Mr. Cawley, the fore- man, had done their hest to carry out the wishes of Mr Davies and the work appeared to be very satisfactory.' He sincerely hoped Mr. Fowkes would bring the matter of the Widnes Lock before the Company. The lock was the same size now as when the canal was cut, and having regard to the improvements which had taken place all round and to the great importance of convey- ing raw materials to Widnes as cheaply as possible, it washighly desirable that larger vessels should be em- ployed. This concluded the proceedings. In the evening the workmen and ataff and a number of visitors from the United Alkali Co. were entertained to a capital spread at the Sloop Hotel. Mr. G. J. Brown presided over an attendance of between seventy and eighty. A number of toasts were honoured, including that of `. Success to the Eustace Carey" which was responded to by Hr. Tracey, of Widnes, who said the work reflected the greatest ceedit upon the firm and particularly upon the foreman. enjoyable musical programme was submitted, including humorous songs by Mr. Alfred Legge, a mandoline solo and song by Mr. H. Foden, and a duet, by Messrs. Jennings and Cook. Mr. E G. Rickets acted as accom- panist and ho and Arr. A. Warburton gave a pianoforte duet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Royal Honour for a Local Veteran..</head>
        <p>Royal Honour for a Local Veteran..</p>
        <p>Es-Colour Sergeant H. Corney, of the Ist 17th Foot, has just received the King's medal, together with the following letter :—I am directed to acquaint von that His Majesty the King has been pleased to approve of the grant of an annuity of £lO, together with a silver medal to you for your long and meritorious services, including the Crimean Campaign. The annuity is to date from Ist April, 1904.—Signed, H. Stoney Smith, Captain. Adjutant 17th Regi- mental District. Glen Parva Barracks, Leicester.— Mr. Corney is well known in Warrington, having served for many years on the Staff at Orford Barracks. He finally retired from the Army with a pension about 15 years ago, and for several years after was the genial host of the " King's Arms "Inn, Buttermarket- street. He now resides at 69, Marshhouse-lane. He is still hale and hearts', with a fund of dry humour, and presents a soul-stirring appearance with his : medal-covered breast, especially when recounting some of his former experiences and exploits. We heartily congratulate Mr. Corney upon the signal honour conferred upon him, and. hope that the sturdy • old veteran may long be spared to reside among us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES . AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES . AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.";</p>
        <p>If there were any Morecambe representatives at Warrington on Saturday they would get the impress:on that after all the sea-siders had not much to fear in the first round of the Cup-ties. Warring- ton's display was so elementary that any self-respect- ing second division club must have been ashamed to feel any trepidation at coming in contact with the fifteen then representing the "Wirepullers." A poorer game has not been seen at Wilderspool for a long time—not that it was uninteresting —but the want of finish all round was appalling. Yet the homesters won by a lucky penalty goal, but Swinton were nearly as had. They did, however, deserve to get a point, and a drawn game would have been a fairer result.</p>
        <p>Perhaps Witty Wa,tty Walton's presence had a demoralising effect on the teams. At any rate, after he had set the ball rolling, the game developed pretty much into a farce. By the way, the Comedian's presence did not seem to be welcomed very warmly ! And no wonder. A ridiculous figure in petticoats is somewhat out of place among the players of a League team and Witty Watty did not add to the dignity either of the Warrington Club or the Northern I Union.</p>
        <p>There is not much to be said of the subsequent proceedings, which lacked earnestness somehow. Warrington were very soon the attacking party, but their —lethods advertised themselves beforehand and were easily frustrated. I never saw a set of backs show more impotence with the ball. They could toss it about freely enough, but as for taking it over the goal line, well, their efforts in this direction would not have frightened a rabbit. Valentine, Dixon, Gar- trell and Co., probably never had such a soft thing in the way of tackling. They always knew where a man was,and they put him down carefu!ly. The Warrington crowd has not often been more disappointed. They came with the expectation of K•eing the once-fierce " Lions " utterly routed, and instead were nearly " chawed up " themselves.</p>
        <p>The men most to blame on the Warrington side were the two halves and Isherwood. Bate was funereally slow, while if Davies did get away with the ball he usually fell down at the critical moment. As a pair their ineffectiveness on the attack was pitiable. Isherwood seemed to be " wandering." I never saw a centre so often out of position. Frequently when in the Swinton half he was yards behind Berry and Fish, and once when the ball came in his direction from the halves he missed a certain try through not , being up in time to take the pass. This is not like the popular " Danny." He :is usually on the spot when wanted, but Saturday must have been an " off' day.</p>
        <p>Berry made a very creditable debut at right centre, and his display was about the most pleasing feature of the game. He handed the ball well and kicked and tackled excellently, but he has, of course, some- thing to learn in the way of tactics. I hope he will not fall a victim to the disease which seems to have taken hold of the Warrington centres—that is, runn- ing to touch. The goal line is the direction to aim for and the more progress young Berry can make down the centre of the field, and the greater will be his use- fulness. A cultivation of the art of doubling inside coupled with his strength as a runner, should make him an ideal centre.</p>
        <p>The great want of the Warrington team is a bustling half back, and I think the committee will be driven to experimenting with either Forster, Harris, or Thomas in this position ere long. An old Warrington three- quarter—one of the best men who ever donned the club jersey—suggest3d to me the other day the advisability of playing Fish at half or extra three- quarter, and bringing Kenyon in again. The idea is well worthy of trial, if only to afford Fish the oppor- tunities he is languishing for. On Saturday he was practically an ornament owing to the inability of the backs to play in his direction. The only runs he made were entirely os his own, and they proved once again his individual suprem icy over any single opponent. Some drastic remedy is called for and expected by the club's supporters.</p>
        <p>On the Swinton side, Dixon did btilliant work at full-back, while Flynn arid Gartrell on the wings were both aggressive. Valentine was the best of the centres, but he lacks entirely the clash and determina• tion of his more famous elder brothers. The Blues were very weak forward, and only just managed to hold their own against the very light pack Warring- ton placed in the field. At half-back, the visitors were as badly-off as Warrington, and the general scheme of attack suffered ',similarly. One dashing player in this position would have won the game comfortably for either side. As it was Warrington scraped home by an inglorious penalty goal kicked by young Berry.</p>
        <p>The one outstanding match on Saturday %vas at Broughton, where the p-wverful Bradford team just won on the post, the margin being only a point, gained by superior goal kicking. This defeat almost puts the Rangers out of the running, which is still made very closely by Oldham and Bradford. At the other end Batley earned a point on Hunslet territory and really look like saving themselves from the second division. They have revived in remarkable fashion.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>—For— —Against— P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. L Pts Pts. Oldham 27...20... 6... 1...38 51-229...22 19-101...41 Bradford 27...19... 6... 2...31 57-239...21 30-132...40 Brou't'n R 26...18... 7... 1...50 54-222...31 20-128...37 Hunslet 27...16...10... 1...54 34-210...25 32-146...33 Leeds Wigan 26. 15. 10 . 1...26 40-172...27 28-138...31 Warr'gt'n . 10... 2...28 35-161...21 25-117...30 Sal ford Hull K.R 26-13-13... 0...31 34-161...28 34-158...26 Hull 26...10...12... 4...25 31-152...28 34-158...24 Widnes Hallfax 28...10...16... 2...23 31-139...23 30-136...22 Leigh Swinton 27... 9...17... 1...15 28-114...32 31-166-19 Batley 27... 8...17... 2...30 22-126...39 42-214...18 Wakefield 27... 8...17... 2...29 13— 97...29 40-178...18 St. Helens 26... 8...17... 1...31 28-146...38 64-268...17 Runcorn</p>
        <p>Since writing the above comments, the team for to-cl ay's Cup-tie has been published, and I see that I the Committee have also come to the conclusion that ' some drastic changes were required. The principal change is at half-back, where Thomas has been selected in preference to Bate. Readers of this column will recollect that some si weeks ago I suggested giving the clever forward a trial at half, whore a player with dash has been required all season. He cannot possibly do worse than some of the men who have operated behind the pack, and there is much to.be hoped from his aggressive tactics. Another big change is the. " dropping " of Isherwood, Dickinson coming in, while Berry retains his place. In the forwards, the return of Preston and Shugars will be welcomed.</p>
        <p>Morecambe should furnish vary little opposition to Warrington's passage in the second round. They are the bottom club in the second division, and there should be a great disparity in form between them and Warrington. The fixture is reminiscent of the cup-tie with Goole, who once upon a time visited Wilderspool to find Fish and Coin a very merry mood. A display of equal smartness by Warrington to-day will be welcomed. They have done very little to electrify the crowd of late.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team is : Hallam, full back ; Fish, Berry, Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters ; Thomas and J. Davies, half backs ; Preston, Shugars, Jolley, Belton, Naylor, Cook; Harmer and Swift, for- wards.</p>
        <p>The "A" team disappointed all their friends on Saturday by failing to win at Swinton. It speaks ill for the attack, that the men were unable to wive off an adverse score of two points though they had a big share of the game. Short again proved a source of weakness twice knocking on when he had good Openings. This defeat places Warrington once more in an inferior position to Wigan "A," who look likely to carry off the me dais.</p>
        <p>The combination table up-to-date is as follows : P. W. D. L. Pts. Wigan (A) lB ... 13 ... 3 ... 2 ... 29 Warrington (A) l6 ... 12 ... 1 ... 3 ... 25 Chadderton Widnes (A) l7 ... 7 ... 4 ... 6 ... 13 St. Helens (A) l9 ... 8 ... 3 ... 8 ... 19 Broughton R'g'rs(A) 16 ... 7 ... 3 ... 6 ... 17 Oldham (A) l3 ... 6 ... 5 ... 7 ... 17 Swinton (A) Rochdale H. (A) l9 ... 3 ... 6 ... 10 ... 12 Runcorn(A) l6 ... 5 ... 1 ... 10 ... 11 Leigh (A) Salford (A) .. .......</p>
        <p>Most people will by now be aware of the charity match to be played at Warrington on Tuesday even- ing next, in aid of the widow of the late J. Richard- son of Runcorn. Warrington will oppose the Cheshire County team, which during the season defeated both Cumberland and Lancashire in the County Championship, and a good game should result. in fact Warrington will have to be at their best to beat the strong Cheshire fifteen, which will be as follows :</p>
        <p>Cheshire County: S. Houghton (Runcorn), full back; S. Johnson (Leigh), J. Butterworth and H. Price (Runcorn), J. Dunbavin (Bradford), three- quarters; J. Jolley (Runcorn), and H. Dunbavin (Leigh), half backs; S. Walker, A. Moulton, A. Richardson, Padbury, Dixon (Runcorn), McDonald (Widnes), Jones (Birkenhead) I Tomlinson and Wood (Runcorn), forwards.</p>
        <p>NOTES FROM MORECAMBE. THE SEASIDERS CONFIDENT.</p>
        <p>The Morecambe team, writes a Morecambe corres- pondent, are looking forward to their meeting with Warrington with the keenest interest. Their very much improved form during the past month has given rise to confident hopes and rosy prophecies.</p>
        <p>Their victory over Castleford on the Yorkshire enclosure, was followed by a decisive victory over Barrow St. George's in the qualifying round of the Northern Union Cup, and on Saturday last with ordinary lucls they should have vanquished Rochdale Hornets.As it was they drew,the game being one of the finest witnessed on the Aloss Lane ground for some time</p>
        <p>The committee have practically selected the same fifteen that did duty in the Rochdale match, with the exception that a little more " weight " has been intro- duced into the forward rank. In fact no effort has been lipared to get the strongest fifteen together.</p>
        <p>The Seasiders are not corning to Warrington think- ing that defeat is a foregone conclusion. Oh dear no, for they actually anticipate a win—or at least a draw. "Every man is a tryer " says the Captain of the More- cambe fifteen, "and those people who imagine that the match will be a runaway affair for Warrington, will, I fancy, be very much mistaken."</p>
        <p>Throughout the week the team has undergone strict training and every man is reported as "fit as a fiddle." Whilst recognizing Warrington's capabilities and their characteristics as keen cup fighters, More- cambe intend to put in every ounce and show equal keenness. Of course, to the average Warringtonian some of these prophecies may sound rather too rosy, but one never knows what happens in football.</p>
        <p>Morecambe team :—Full back, Levi Jackson; three- quarters, T. A. Lamb, D. Bell (captain), W. Longrnire and E. Flasington ; halves, S. Thornton, and W. Threlfall ; for Wards, T. Shaw, G. Halton, F Jackson Toulmin, Balmforth, Woodhouse, Cornthvvaite and Had wen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>[By R.G.P.]</p>
        <p>The North v. South match played at Bowden last Saturday ending in a victory for the South by 4-2. In the first half the South forwards had things pretty much their own way, and after obtained three goals against the slope, it looked as if their would be something like a repetition of the game played at Kersal two years ago, when the South won by 12-0. The great strength of the southerners was their three inside forwards, and time after time they ran through the Northern defence. Had their wing men been good, nothing could have saved the North,but after the first twenty minutes they fell away, and this allowed the North defenders to keep in close touch with Horne, Shoveller and Jordan, Half-time score : South 3 North 0.</p>
        <p>Opening the second half the South at once attacked; but good play by Kenna and Peel transferred operations and at length Fabel scored the first goal for the North. The North now played with great dash, and when Fabel scored a second from a pass by Draper, the South looked like a beaten team. However following up some good work by Nightingale, Shoveller took the pass beautifully and tricking both Williams and Peel, scored a characteristic goal, with which Parker had no chance. The whistle immediately went for time, leaving the South winners by 4-2.</p>
        <p>Turning to the individual merits of the players, and taking the South team first, it must be said, that they were far and away superior to the home side. Edgell in goal played well, and on several occasions dashed out and saved, when remaining in goal would have proved fatal. Pimm and Stocks at back, hit well but although a better pair than the home backs, did not strike me as being brilliant. The halves, Tennant, Bacon and Hopkins played well, especially Tennant and Bacon. The forwards were splendid, especially Shoveller who quite sustained his reputation, his passing and dribbling being a long way above anything on the North side.</p>
        <p>The South are a good team, who play superior hockey to we Northerners, and their scientific combination should be a lesson to some of the hockey players who were watching the match.</p>
        <p>I was very disappointed with the display given by the North, and on the days form several bett sr players than those chosen had been passed over. Parker in goal was good,and the only thing that might be urged against him is, that he might have left his goal on two occasions, instead of waiting. Williams at back was poor in the extreme, lacking both pace and hitting power, and how he ever got chosen is a mystery. L. G. Peel was fairly sound, but was rather too fond of trying to beat a man before clearing. Of the halves Blatherwick and Kenna were both good, and I had hoped to see them both chosen for England. Wordsworth was the weakest man of the team, but the least said about him the better, as his faults were too many to enumerate. The forwards were also weak, the only few worthy of mention being Draper, F. B. Peel, Ashcroft, Tetlow. and Fabel being unable to show any signs of combination.</p>
        <p>Owing to an unfortunate mistake having arisen, the Stockton Heath club are without a fixture for this afternoon, but a scratch game with Latchford Second will probably be arranged. After the North v. South trial match at Bowdon on Saturday, the English Selection Committee chose the following team to meet Wales at Cardiff on March 11 :L. Parker (Timperley), goal ; H. S. Freeman (Staines) and H. C. Boycott (Northampton), backs: L. P. Morris Davies (Midlands), F. F. Blatherwick (Chester), and J. N. Burns (Edgbaston), half backs ; E. Green (Staines), T. Pettick (West), S. H. Shoveller (Hampstead), A. R. Ranger (Northampton), and F. B. Peel (Bebington), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>BILLIARDS. Conservative Club v. Liberal Club.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS. Conservative Club v. Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>The first of the annual home and home matches between the Warrington Liberal and Conservative Clubs was played on Thursday at the headquarters of the Conservatives in Sankey-street. Teams of 14 a side were played, the gatnes being 200 up. Last year the Liberals made a very good show on the "foreign " tables, and won on the aggregate totals, but on Thurs- day night, the Conservatives had their revenge. Only three out of the 14 games were won by the Liberals, who at the close were in a minority of 475 points, an adverse balance which will take a lot of wiping off when the return game is played at the Liberal Club next Thursday evening. At the conclusion of play, Alderman Smethurst made a brief spEech welcoming the Liberals and wishing them, better luck next time. His remarks were sup- ported by Mr. T. Horrabin (captain) and Mr. Henry I Collin. Councillor Wilkinson, Mr. J. Worrell, and Mr. E. V. Walker (captain) responded on behalf of the Liberals. Scores</p>
        <p>LIBERALS. CONSERVATIVES. M. V Walker 2oo T. Cooper R. Woods E. Piertioint J. McKenzie G. Barton 179 T. Sutton J. Davies 147 R. Straw 2OO E. Wilson J. Halliwell E. J. Mitchell S. J. Beaman 176 J. Keatinge W. Woods W. Patrick lOB G. H. Smith C. Jordan J. Howes jun., 2718 ' 2422</p>
        <p>- 2243</p>
        <p>e</p>
        <p>. i o ) , . . , • , I . ' • , I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Majority for Cons.... 475</head>
        <p>Majority for Cons.... 475</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the country. The visitation of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring; it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful , influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive 1 at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much. fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know ofnothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Head- ache, accompanied by difficulty in breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and, between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. By common consent Patients every where admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness, and Langour, with greater success than any other known remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indigestion,Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands unequalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d, and 4s. 9d., by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Mgnufacturing Company, Limited, Llanellv, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name " -Gwilym Evans "on Label Stamp and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Concert in Parr Mall.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Musical Society's concluding con- cert of the season embodied something in the nature of an experiment. For the first time, in at least two decades of the society's existence, the services of pro-. fessional solo vocalists of repute have been dispensed with, opportunity being thus found of bringing to the fore singers drawn from the ranks of members them- selves. The fact that Tuesday's concert was a great success and that the change of policy thus received abundant justification, is therefore a matter for pardon- able self-congratulation on the part of the society's executive, for seldom has any concert in the society's recent history given greater and more unalloyed pleasure; any misgivings as to whether public support would be extended to the new departure being happily set at rest by an attendance quite normal in point of numbers, and by tha enthusiastic way in which much of the vocal music was received. We have alluded to the change as a tentative one because, with a single ex- ception,the ladies and gentlemen who took a prominent part on Tuesday sang only ie concerted pieces. Taking courage from the result it is clear that the next step of advancing individuals to the position of undertaking songs and solo work may be taken in the certain hope of receiving due recognition, and further developments of the idea will, we aresure, be awaited by an interested public. The biggest thing to which members collectively addressed themselves on Tuesday was Mendelssohn's "Loreley " a short and unfinished yet finely effective work, admirably adapted to the special purpose of the moment. Mrs. Arthur Booth's interpretation of the solo part of Leonora marked a clear advance on the road to that goal of professional rank to which she might well aspire. Mrs. Booth was somewhat nervous at first, but, as the climax was approached, an unmis- table improvement in the management of her vocal resources and a praiseworthy steadiness of intonation were clearly apparent in her singing. An intensely dramatic situation, in the invocation of Heaven's justice and in the cey for retribution and vengeance, might well have warranted a more whole-hearted abaniloa, yet Mrs. Booth's Leonora was by no means a tame impersonation, a degree of declamatory power being reached at the words "Agreed! thus as I rend this veil" which was as artistic as it was undoubtedly highly creditable. The chorus portions of the finale to "Loreley" were most effectively presented. Precision in attack and vigour in delivery are here first essentials and these were the matters in which the sing- ing of the choral members excelled. Of the part-songs which further engaged the attention of the chorus the most interesting were Elgar's unpretentious yet charm- ing little composition "The Snow"—for ladies' voices int three parts and accompaniments for piano and two violins,—and the well-known "Where art thou, beam of light?" in which last mentioned a splendid tone volume and some admirable gradations were noticeable. Except that, heard against the solid background of the glee a fine effect of contrast was secured, there seemed little justification for bracketing with it the flimsy part- song "You stole my love!" which, now out-worn at these concerts, might well be allowed to rest. The Pilgrim's chorus froin"Tannhauser," in which of course the male-voices were alone concerned, served unfortun- ately to show how unreliable in intonation a body of tenors can sometimes be, despite extraneous aid such as was here rendered in the early part by Mr. Payton at the organ. How happens it that since the time when this telling excerpt was loudly encored in Parr Hall,the tenors have so far" gone off," to use a vulgar but ex- pressive phrase ? Coining to the concerted pieces it d was a little unfortunate that the sextet from" Lucia di Lammermoor "(in which Mrs. Booth, Miss M. Henshall, Messrs. .1. Rabbitt, J. E. Hirnan, W. H. Wallington an Mr. J. W. Bdabv were associated) given, as it was some i - what early n the programme, attracted so little'atten- tion. It was exceedingly well sung by all concerned and the ensemble,—in which the chorus participated' and in which orchestral colour is so richly laid on,—was exceptionally fine. The quartet " Strange adventure ! " brought back pleasant memories of recent high achievements by members in the region of light opera, and, being here sung by the same ladies and gentlemen who rendered it at the per- formances of "The Yeomen of the Guard," it wag enthusiastically received and encored. Nor was the quintet from "Merrin England," "Love is meant to make us glad" by Miss G. Pemberton, Miss M. W. Collin, Messrs. J. H. Draper, F. Fieldhouse and J. W. Bibby, less successfully vocalised, a repetition of thia also being demanded. An augmented orchestra had been made a feature of this concert. The instrumental piece de 'rsistance wag Tschaikowsky's "Casse Noisette" suite, of the excellent performance of which all concerned may well feel Proud. That ragginess in the violins in the very exact- ing first number which, having regard to the fact that so many amateur players were engaged in it, might well have been looked for, was, it is pleasing to note, conspicuous by its almost total absence, and all went well with this movement. Of the later numbers the "Danse Russo, Tr4pac" and the "Dense Ohineise," manifestly came as a pleasant surprise to the audience, both baying to be repeated in compliance with loud acclamation—high testimony to the fact that the whole of the suite,—with its wealth of engaging melody and its superabundance of picturesque detail and colour,.— received an adequate and entirely satisfactory rendering. Mr. Alfred Ross's solos contributed largely to the success of the concert. Seldom has a more gifted violinist appeared before a Piarrington audience, and seldom has one been able to so completely rivet its attention. In the two movements from the VieustempS concerto and in Sarhsate's " Zigeunerweisen," Mr. Ross's playiag had for its chief characteristics a crisp- ness and neatness of execution and a truthfulness of intonation which bid fair to place him in the front rank of present-day virtuosi. Some explanatory notes, by Mr. P. H. Crossley the society's energetic and capable conductor, appended to many items, made the printed programme both interest- ing and informative, thereby doubtless enhancing the enjoyment of the music by many of the audience. A fine performance of the rousing chorus " Hail ! bright abode " (Wagner) and the singing of the National Anthem brought the concert to a close.</p>
        <p>DUET WITH A VENTILATING FAN. Mr. Plunket Greene, who imagined himself to be singing a solo, found at a Leeds concert that he was engaged in a duet, the other puticipant being an electric: fan. As the fan did not keep in tune, Mr. Greene asked that he might be allowed to sing alone. Later in the evening the fan joined in again, and the would-be soloist had again to request that it should be stopped. "At the annual Licensing Sessions for Birkdalet Southport, because of a complaint that Bo 7ril and other refreshments could not be obtained at the Palace Hotel, it was suggested that the license should not be renewed. This should be regarded as important bY those in the license trade.—" Dundee Courier."</p>
